(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.8adf382ea4.js"]={"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the primary hypothalamic nuclei responsible for synthesizing ADH and oxytocin, and their respective predominant products?","choices":{"A":"ADH is synthesized primarily in the supraoptic nuclei and oxytocin is synthesized primarily in the paraventricular nuclei, with each nucleus capable of producing approximately one-sixth as much of the other hormone","B":"ADH is synthesized primarily in the paraventricular nuclei and oxytocin is synthesized primarily in the supraoptic nuclei, with both hormones transported through the hypophyseal portal vessels to reach the posterior pituitary","C":"Both ADH and oxytocin are synthesized in equal amounts by the supraoptic nuclei alone, with the paraventricular nuclei serving only as a storage site before axonal transport through the pituitary stalk","D":"ADH is synthesized in the anterior pituitary corticotroph cells in response to hypothalamic CRH stimulation, while oxytocin is synthesized by the lactotrophs in response to suckling-triggered TRH release","E":"Both ADH and oxytocin are synthesized de novo in the posterior pituitary itself by pituicyte glial cells, which convert precursor peptides transported from the hypothalamus into the active hormones"},"explanation":"ADH (vasopressin) is produced predominantly by neuronal cell bodies in the supraoptic nuclei, while oxytocin is produced predominantly in the paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus; each nucleus retains the capacity to synthesize approximately one-sixth as much of the other hormone. Both hormones are bound to carrier proteins called neurophysins and transported down axons through the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract to nerve terminal endings in the posterior pituitary, where they are stored in secretory granules until nerve impulse-triggered exocytosis releases them into adjacent capillaries. Pituicytes are glial-like supporting cells in the posterior pituitary that do not synthesize hormones. Neither hormone is produced in the anterior pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH from supraoptic nuclei; oxytocin from paraventricular nuclei. Both transported via neurophysins. First Aid: posterior pituitary stores and releases both hormones, made in hypothalamus, derived from neuroectoderm. COMLEX: Posterior pituitary pathology affecting the stalk can impair both hormones simultaneously.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei and axonal transport to the neurohypophysis via neurophysin carrier proteins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"Neither hormone is produced in"}},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"After synthesis in hypothalamic neuronal cell bodies, ADH and oxytocin must be transported to the posterior pituitary for storage. Which of the following correctly describes this transport mechanism?","choices":{"E":"ADH and oxytocin are transported bound to carrier proteins called neurophysins down hypothalamic axons through the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract to bulbous nerve endings in the posterior pituitary, a process requiring several days","A":"ADH and oxytocin are transported from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal blood vessels, which carry peptide hormones directly from the median eminence into the posterior pituitary capillary bed","B":"ADH and oxytocin are transported as free peptides through the cerebrospinal fluid of the third ventricle, diffusing across the ependymal lining into posterior pituitary capillaries to reach the storage granules within pituicyte cells","C":"ADH and oxytocin are synthesized as pre-pro-hormones in pituicyte glial cells within the posterior pituitary itself, using precursor molecules selectively transported from hypothalamic periventricular neurons through gap junctions","D":"ADH and oxytocin are secreted into hypothalamic portal capillaries at the median eminence and travel retrogradely into the posterior pituitary through a specialized countercurrent vascular exchange mechanism unique to the neurohypophysis"},"explanation":"After synthesis in hypothalamic neuronal cell bodies, ADH and oxytocin are packaged in secretory granules together with carrier proteins called neurophysins. The granules travel by axonal transport down the fibers of the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract through the pituitary stalk. This process takes several days before adequate hormone stores accumulate in the bulbous nerve terminal endings on posterior pituitary capillaries. Release occurs by exocytosis when action potentials propagate down these axons. The hypophyseal portal system carries releasing and inhibitory hormones from the median eminence to the anterior pituitary — not to the posterior pituitary. Pituicytes provide structural support only and do not synthesize hormones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH and oxytocin travel via axonal transport with neurophysins. After release, neurophysin separates and has no known function. First Aid: made in hypothalamus, transported via neurophysins, stored and released from posterior pituitary. COMLEX: Know the anatomical route through the pituitary stalk for understanding how stalk pathology disrupts posterior pituitary hormone delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei and axonal transport to the neurohypophysis via neurophysin carrier proteins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The hypophyseal portal system ","C":"Pituicytes provide structural ","D":"The hypophyseal portal system "}},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":1,"stem":"If the pituitary stalk is surgically transected above the posterior pituitary gland while the hypothalamus is left completely intact, which of the following best predicts the subsequent pattern of ADH secretion?","choices":{"D":"ADH secretion decreases transiently for a few days and then partially recovers, because ADH is made in hypothalamic cell bodies and the surviving cut fiber ends in the upper stalk can still release ADH into capillaries at the median eminence","A":"ADH secretion ceases permanently because the nerve terminal endings that line posterior pituitary capillaries are the exclusive site of hormone release, and severing their connection from hypothalamic cell bodies permanently abolishes all ADH output","B":"ADH secretion doubles immediately after transection because the transected stalk removes tonic somatostatin-mediated inhibition that normally suppresses hypothalamic ADH neuron activity through retrograde axonal signaling","C":"ADH secretion remains completely unchanged because the posterior pituitary contains an independent ADH synthesizing reserve in pituicyte glial cells that activates automatically when hypothalamic input is lost through a compensatory transcriptional program","E":"ADH secretion ceases permanently and is replaced by excess oxytocin, because the paraventricular nucleus compensates for supraoptic nucleus disconnection by upregulating oxytocin synthesis and converting it to ADH through a shared enzymatic pathway"},"explanation":"Stalk transection above the posterior pituitary causes a transient decrease in ADH secretion lasting several days, after which secretion partially normalizes. The key principle is that ADH is synthesized in hypothalamic neuronal cell bodies, not in the posterior pituitary nerve terminals. After a transection below the median eminence, the proximal cut ends of axons within the hypothalamic stalk and median eminence can still release ADH into portal capillaries, allowing some hormonal delivery to systemic circulation. If the lesion is at or above the median eminence (close to the hypothalamus), the release site and many magnocellular neurons are lost and DI is more likely to be permanent. This experiment demonstrates that the posterior pituitary nerve terminals are the usual — but not the only — release site for ADH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central DI after pituitary stalk damage may be transient if the damage is below the median eminence, because hypothalamic ADH can still reach systemic circulation via median eminence portal capillaries. First Aid explicitly notes this. COMLEX: Pituitary stalk lesions from trauma, surgery, or tumors can cause DI; transience depends on lesion location.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — posterior pituitary hormone synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei and axonal transport to the neurohypophysis via neurophysin carrier proteins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"This experiment demonstrates t"}},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy volunteer drinks a large water load, plasma osmolality falls, and ADH secretion stops. Which change in the collecting duct principal cells follows, and over what time course does it occur?","choices":{"C":"Aquaporin vesicles leave the apical membrane for the cell interior within about 5 to 10 minutes, so the ducts become nearly impermeable to water","A":"Aquaporin-2 proteins are degraded in lysosomes, and water permeability stays high for 24 to 48 hours until the existing channels are used up","B":"Basolateral aquaporin channels are removed over several days, so water permeability falls slowly as the medullary gradient washes out","D":"V2 receptors stay occupied for many hours because ADH binds them irreversibly, so water reabsorption continues long after secretion stops","E":"Apical aquaporins remain in place, but the cells close their tight junctions, which stops water from crossing between cells within seconds"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 76) explains that ADH binds membrane receptors that activate adenylyl cyclase and form cAMP, which phosphorylates elements in special vesicles containing aquaporins; the vesicles insert into the apical membranes within 5 to 10 minutes. In the absence of ADH, the entire process reverses in another 5 to 10 minutes, and without ADH the luminal membranes of the collecting ducts are almost impermeable to water, so dilute urine is excreted. The vesicles are reused rather than degraded, so permeability does not stay high for a day or more. The rapid change is at the apical membrane, not a slow removal of basolateral channels over days. ADH binding is not irreversible; the effect ends within minutes. Water crosses principal cells through aquaporins rather than through tight junctions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH → V2 → Gs → cAMP → PKA → aquaporin-2 vesicles insert into the apical membrane; on and off within about 5 to 10 minutes each way (Guyton Ch 76). COMLEX: Because the effect switches off within minutes, a water load is excreted quickly once ADH falls; in SIADH, ADH is not suppressed and free water is retained (First Aid 2025 p 342).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled GPCR signaling via cAMP is the V2 receptor pathway responsible for aquaporin-2 insertion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":1,"stem":"An experiment injects a concentrated electrolyte solution into the artery supplying the hypothalamus and observes that ADH secretion rises to 20 times normal within minutes. Which of the following correctly describes the cellular mechanism by which increased extracellular fluid osmolality stimulates ADH secretion?","choices":{"D":"Increased extracellular osmolality directly depolarizes ADH-secreting neurons by increasing sodium conductance through concentration-sensitive membrane channels, bypassing any osmoreceptor intermediary structure entirely","A":"Increased extracellular osmolality draws water out of osmoreceptor cells by osmosis, causing them to shrink; the reduction in cell volume generates nerve signals in the hypothalamus that drive ADH release from posterior pituitary nerve endings","B":"Increased extracellular osmolality is detected by baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which send afferent nerve signals to the hypothalamic supraoptic nuclei to increase ADH synthesis and release from posterior pituitary terminal endings","C":"Increased extracellular osmolality stimulates atrial stretch receptors in the right atrium that respond to changes in plasma protein concentration; the unexcited receptors send vagal signals to the hypothalamus to stimulate ADH secretion from posterior pituitary nerve endings","E":"Increased extracellular osmolality triggers renin release from juxtaglomerular cells, generating angiotensin II, which crosses the blood-brain barrier at the organum vasculosum to directly activate V1 receptors on ADH-secreting neurons as the primary osmosensing pathway"},"explanation":"Osmoreceptors located in or near the hypothalamus — possibly in the organum vasculosum of the anteroventral third ventricle region — detect extracellular osmolality changes. When extracellular fluid becomes concentrated, water is drawn out of osmoreceptor cells by osmosis, causing these cells to shrink. This decrease in cell volume activates mechanosensitive channels or other volume-sensitive signals, generating nerve impulses that propagate to ADH-secreting magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, triggering action potentials that release ADH from posterior pituitary nerve terminals. Injection of a dilute solution causes the opposite: water moves into osmoreceptor cells, they swell, nerve firing decreases, and ADH secretion nearly ceases. Baroreceptors and atrial stretch receptors mediate the separate blood volume pathway for ADH regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plasma osmolality is the primary regulator of ADH; hypovolemia is secondary but can override osmolality. Osmoreceptors are in or near the hypothalamic supraoptic region. First Aid diagram shows: increased serum osmolality → hypothalamus → ADH → decreased urine osmolality. COMLEX: Dehydration raises osmolality first, stimulating ADH before significant volume depletion occurs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled GPCR signaling via cAMP is the V2 receptor pathway responsible for aquaporin-2 insertion in renal principal cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Baroreceptors and atrial stret"}},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient sustains acute hemorrhage with approximately 20% blood volume loss. Which of the following best explains how decreased blood volume stimulates ADH secretion, and what hemodynamic effect results from the very high ADH concentrations achieved in this setting?","choices":{"A":"Decreased blood volume reduces atrial stretch receptor and carotid, aortic, and pulmonary baroreceptor firing, removing tonic inhibition and greatly increasing ADH secretion; at high concentrations ADH activates V1 receptors on arteriolar smooth muscle, causing vasoconstriction that raises blood pressure","B":"Decreased blood volume is detected by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamic organum vasculosum that interpret reduced plasma volume as increased plasma osmolality; subsequent ADH release acts exclusively through V2 receptors on renal principal cells to conserve water without any direct effect on vascular smooth muscle tone","C":"Decreased blood volume triggers renal macula densa cells to release prostaglandins into portal circulation, which travel to the hypothalamus and directly activate ADH-secreting neurons to increase secretion; the resulting ADH then raises blood pressure through increased aquaporin-2 expression in the proximal tubule","D":"Decreased blood volume activates hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus neurons through angiotensin II crossing the blood-brain barrier; the resulting ADH secretion acts exclusively on renal V2 receptors to conserve water, while angiotensin II independently handles the vasoconstrictor response without ADH involvement","E":"Decreased blood volume directly stimulates pituicyte glial cells in the posterior pituitary through sympathetic innervation; the activated pituicytes release pre-stored ADH independently from hypothalamic neuronal signaling and the released ADH raises blood pressure through renal water retention alone"},"explanation":"Blood volume regulation of ADH operates through stretch-sensitive mechanoreceptors. Atrial stretch receptors in the cardiac atria are excited by overfilling and tonically inhibit ADH secretion; when blood volume falls, the atria underfill, these receptors are unexcited, and their inhibitory tone is removed, greatly increasing ADH secretion. Baroreceptors in the carotid, aortic, and pulmonary regions similarly contribute: reduced stretch with low blood pressure stimulates ADH. With severe hemorrhage, ADH can rise to 50 times normal. At these high concentrations, ADH activates V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle — Gq-coupled receptors that trigger calcium release and vasoconstriction — which raises mean arterial pressure. This vasopressor action explains ADH's alternative name, vasopressin, and its clinical use in septic shock.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypovolemia is a powerful ADH stimulus; 15-25% blood volume loss can raise ADH up to 50x normal. V1 = vasoconstriction; V2 = antidiuresis. Vasopressin is used clinically in septic shock to raise MAP. First Aid: blood volume regulation is secondary to osmolality but overrides it in severe hypovolemia. COMLEX: ADH dual roles in volume regulation and osmolality are tested in clinical scenarios involving hemorrhage, CHF, and cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled GPCR signaling via cAMP is the V2 receptor pathway responsible for aquaporin-2 insertion in renal principal cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old male presents with severe polydipsia and polyuria producing 10 liters of urine daily. His serum sodium is 152 mEq/L, serum osmolality is 312 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality is 75 mOsm/kg. MRI of the brain demonstrates a mass lesion compressing the pituitary stalk. Serum ADH level is undetectable. Which of the following best identifies this diagnosis and explains the pathophysiology?","choices":{"D":"Central diabetes insipidus from ADH deficiency caused by stalk compression; without ADH, aquaporin-2 is not inserted into the collecting duct apical membrane, so the tubule stays nearly impermeable to water, causing dilute polyuria and hypernatremia","A":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from V2 receptor resistance caused by stalk compression-induced local inflammation; ADH is undetectable because the high urine output has diluted plasma ADH below assay detection, not because hypothalamic secretion is impaired","B":"SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion by the stalk mass lesion; the dilute urine and hypernatremia are characteristic of ADH-mediated free water retention with preserved urinary sodium excretion maintaining euvolemia","C":"Primary polydipsia from hypothalamic thirst center stimulation by the mass lesion; the undetectable ADH results from normal suppression by excessive water intake, and the dilute urine reflects voluntary water loading rather than any hormonal insufficiency","E":"Psychogenic DI from anxiety triggered by the MRI findings; the serum hypernatremia, undetectable ADH, and dilute urine all reflect voluntary water restriction rather than a pathological deficit in ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary"},"explanation":"Central DI results from ADH deficiency caused by disruption of hypothalamic ADH-secreting neurons or their axons. Without ADH, V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells cannot be activated, cAMP does not rise, and aquaporin-2 vesicles remain in the cytoplasm. The collecting duct becomes nearly impermeable to water, allowing massive free water loss into the urine. The result is polyuria with very dilute urine (low osmolality and specific gravity), and the loss of free water concentrates plasma sodium, producing hypernatremia and elevated serum osmolality. SIADH produces the opposite: water retention, hyponatremia, and concentrated urine despite low serum osmolality. Primary polydipsia would show low or normal serum osmolality, not elevated, because water intake exceeds loss. The undetectable ADH here reflects absent secretion, not dilutional suppression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central DI = absent ADH → dilute polyuria + hypernatremia. Serum osmolality high, urine osmolality low. Urine specific gravity less than 1.006. First Aid: ADH level is low in central DI, normal or high in nephrogenic DI. COMLEX: Central DI is a named CP3 condition; recognize from polyuria, dilute urine, and elevated serum osmolality/sodium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH as pituitary pathology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The undetectable ADH here refl","B":"SIADH produces the opposite: w"}},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with polyuria and polydipsia undergoes a water deprivation test. After fluid restriction, serum osmolality rises to 304 mOsm/kg and urine osmolality remains at 115 mOsm/kg. Desmopressin is then administered. Two hours later, urine osmolality rises to 845 mOsm/kg. Which of the following best explains the robust response to desmopressin in this patient?","choices":{"B":"The significant urine osmolality increase after desmopressin confirms primary polydipsia; the medullary osmotic gradient is intact and the concentrated urine after desmopressin represents restoration of baseline collecting duct permeability that was temporarily impaired by water diuresis","A":"The significant urine osmolality increase after desmopressin confirms nephrogenic DI; exogenous desmopressin at pharmacological doses can overcome V2 receptor resistance by activating a compensatory cGMP-dependent aquaporin-3 insertion pathway not accessible to endogenous ADH","C":"The failure to concentrate urine during water restriction despite rising serum osmolality indicates primary polydipsia; desmopressin corrects hypothalamic osmoreceptor desensitization from chronic water loading rather than replacing absent endogenous ADH secretion","D":"The significant urine osmolality increase after desmopressin confirms SIADH; ectopic ADH from a paraneoplastic source is structurally abnormal and cannot activate V2 receptors, while desmopressin as a normal ADH analog restores full V2 receptor signaling","E":"The significant urine osmolality increase after desmopressin confirms central DI; the collecting duct V2 receptors, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP machinery, and aquaporin-2 vesicles are all intact, but endogenous ADH secretion is absent due to posterior pituitary or hypothalamic pathology"},"explanation":"In central DI, the posterior pituitary or hypothalamic ADH-secreting pathways are damaged, but the kidney's entire ADH-response machinery is intact. Desmopressin (DDAVP), a synthetic ADH analog, activates V2 receptors on principal cells normally, generating cAMP, phosphorylating AQP-2 vesicles, and driving apical membrane insertion. The dramatic rise in urine osmolality (greater than 50% increase, typically exceeding 700 mOsm/kg) confirms that the kidney can concentrate urine when given exogenous ADH, proving the defect is in hypothalamic ADH production. In nephrogenic DI, the kidney is resistant to ADH — V2 receptors or downstream signaling are defective — so desmopressin produces minimal or no rise in urine osmolality. Treatment of central DI is desmopressin; treatment of nephrogenic DI targets the underlying cause and uses HCTZ, amiloride, or indomethacin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic board question — polyuria with high serum osmolality, low urine osmolality, and robust desmopressin response = central DI. No response to desmopressin = nephrogenic DI. First Aid table: central DI responds to desmopressin, nephrogenic does not. COMLEX: Desmopressin is also used for nocturnal enuresis and von Willebrand disease type 1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH as pituitary pathology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"In nephrogenic DI, the kidney "}},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes central diabetes insipidus from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus in terms of ADH levels, kidney responsiveness, and appropriate treatment?","choices":{"E":"In central DI, ADH is low due to deficient hypothalamic secretion and the kidney remains fully responsive to ADH; treatment is desmopressin. In nephrogenic DI, ADH is normal or elevated due to intact hypothalamic secretion but the kidney fails to respond due to V2 receptor or downstream signaling defects; treatment targets the underlying cause with low-solute diet, HCTZ, or amiloride","A":"In central DI, ADH is elevated due to autonomous secretion by posterior pituitary adenoma and the kidney is resistance to the excess ADH; treatment is desmopressin to restore V2 receptor sensitivity. In nephrogenic DI, ADH is absent and the kidney has no receptor target; treatment is recombinant ADH administration","B":"In central DI, AQP-2 channels are congenitally absent from collecting duct principal cells due to a genetic mutation preventing AQP2 gene expression; in nephrogenic DI, ADH is absent from the posterior pituitary while AQP-2 channels are overexpressed, causing paradoxical water retention despite polyuria","C":"Central DI and nephrogenic DI both produce elevated ADH levels because osmoreceptor stimulation drives increased hypothalamic output in both conditions; the key distinction is that central DI responds to low-dose vasopressin while nephrogenic DI requires high-dose vasopressin to overcome resistance","D":"In central DI, treatment is a low-solute diet combined with HCTZ to reduce tubular fluid volume and partially compensate for absent aquaporin-2 insertion; in nephrogenic DI, treatment is desmopressin to replace the deficient endogenous ADH and restore V2 receptor activation in resistant principal cells"},"explanation":"Central DI results from deficient ADH secretion; the hypothalamic-posterior pituitary pathway is damaged, and circulating ADH is low. Because the kidney's V2 receptors and AQP-2 machinery are intact, desmopressin replacement is effective and diagnostic. Nephrogenic DI results from renal resistance to ADH — V2 receptor mutations (hereditary), drug effects (lithium, demeclocycline), or electrolyte abnormalities (hypercalcemia, hypokalemia) — with normal or elevated ADH levels because osmoreceptors respond normally but the kidney does not. Desmopressin is ineffective in nephrogenic DI. Management involves correcting the underlying cause, using a low-solute diet to reduce obligatory urine output, and paradoxically using thiazide diuretics or amiloride to reduce tubular fluid delivery. Pairing HCTZ with central DI and desmopressin with nephrogenic DI reverses the correct treatments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central DI: low ADH, responds to desmopressin. Nephrogenic DI: high ADH, does not respond. Causes of nephrogenic DI include lithium, demeclocycline, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, and hereditary V2 receptor mutation. COMLEX: Both central and nephrogenic DI are CP3 named conditions; differentiation guides therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — pathogenesis, clinical presentation, and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH as pituitary pathology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes oxytocin's primary role in the process of labor and the mechanism by which uterine contractions amplify their own intensity during delivery?","choices":{"D":"Oxytocin stimulates contraction of the pregnant uterus; cervical stretching during labor sends sensory nerve signals to hypothalamic paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, causing increased oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary, which intensifies contractions, further dilates the cervix, and amplifies the cycle through positive feedback","A":"Oxytocin inhibits uterine contractions during early labor to prevent premature delivery and transitions to a stimulatory role only after cervical dilation reaches 6 cm, when a progesterone-withdrawal threshold removes tonic oxytocin inhibition of myometrial smooth muscle","B":"Oxytocin stimulates prostaglandin synthesis from cervical stromal cells, which then diffuses into the myometrium to cause contractions through a paracrine mechanism; oxytocin itself has no direct contractile effect on uterine myometrial smooth muscle cells","C":"Oxytocin acts exclusively on uterine endometrial decidual cells to release local cytokines that indirectly activate myometrial stretch receptors during the latent phase of labor, with no role in the active phase once contractions are established","E":"Oxytocin causes uterine contraction by directly activating voltage-gated sodium channels in myometrial smooth muscle through ligand-gated ion channel coupling, producing depolarization that requires a critical estrogen-primed receptor density before any uterotonic effect occurs"},"explanation":"Oxytocin powerfully stimulates contraction of the pregnant uterus, particularly near term when estrogen-driven upregulation of oxytocin receptors has increased myometrial sensitivity. Evidence for oxytocin's role includes: hypophysectomized animals have prolonged labor; plasma oxytocin rises progressively during labor, reaching peak levels in the final stage; and cervical stimulation in pregnant animals triggers increased oxytocin secretion. The cervical stimulation-hypothalamus-oxytocin release loop is a positive feedback mechanism (the Ferguson reflex) that amplifies uterine contractions as the cervix dilates. Synthetic oxytocin (Pitocin) is used clinically to induce or augment labor, directly confirming the physiological role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oxytocin causes uterine contractions; cervical dilation triggers more oxytocin release via positive feedback. Used clinically as Pitocin for labor induction. First Aid: responsible for milk letdown reflex and uterine contractions. COMLEX: Distinguish oxytocin role in labor from its milk ejection role; both are posterior pituitary functions and both are CP3-relevant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — consequences of over- and underproduction of pituitary hormones, including oxytocin deficiency causing prolonged labor and impaired milk ejection","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the complete neuroendocrine pathway responsible for the milk ejection reflex triggered by suckling in a breastfeeding mother?","choices":{"A":"Suckling activates stretch receptors in the areolar skin that send signals directly to the posterior pituitary gland without any hypothalamic involvement; the posterior pituitary stores oxytocin within pituicyte cytoplasm and releases it independently upon direct mechanical areolar stimulation","B":"Suckling directly stimulates prolactin release from anterior pituitary lactotrophs through dopamine pathway inhibition; prolactin then travels to mammary alveoli and activates contractile proteins within ductal epithelial cells to physically push accumulated milk into larger ducts for infant feeding","C":"Suckling causes oxytocin release directly from hypothalamic nerve terminals into the median eminence portal blood, which carries oxytocin to the anterior pituitary where it stimulates prolactin secretion; prolactin then contracts the smooth muscle lining of mammary lactiferous ducts to produce milk ejection","D":"Suckling stimulates sensory nerve afferents in the nipple that transmit signals to oxytocin neurons in hypothalamic paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, triggering oxytocin release from posterior pituitary nerve terminals; oxytocin travels via blood to contract myoepithelial cells surrounding mammary alveoli, ejecting milk into lactiferous ducts within less than one minute","E":"Suckling activates sympathetic adrenergic efferents to the mammary gland that directly contract myoepithelial cells through alpha-adrenergic receptor activation, while oxytocin serves only a modulatory sensitizing role by upregulating adrenergic receptor expression on myoepithelial cells"},"explanation":"The milk ejection reflex is a neuroendocrine arc: sensory afferents from the nipple carry suckling signals to the hypothalamus, where oxytocin neurons in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei fire action potentials. These impulses propagate down the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary, triggering exocytosis of oxytocin into posterior pituitary capillaries. Oxytocin enters systemic circulation, reaches the mammary glands, and binds oxytocin receptors on myoepithelial cells that form a contractile lattice surrounding the alveoli. Myoepithelial contraction squeezes milk from alveoli into lactiferous ducts and to the nipple within less than one minute — the milk letdown. Prolactin (anterior pituitary) stimulates milk synthesis, not milk ejection. Pituicytes do not store or release hormones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oxytocin causes milk ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells. Prolactin causes milk production (lactogenesis). Stress inhibits oxytocin release and impairs letdown. First Aid: oxytocin responsible for milk letdown reflex in response to suckling. COMLEX: The distinction between milk synthesis and milk ejection is a classic board concept connecting anterior and posterior pituitary functions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — consequences of over- and underproduction of pituitary hormones, including oxytocin deficiency causing prolonged labor and impaired milk ejection","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Pituicytes do not store or rel","B":"Prolactin (anterior pituitary)","C":"Prolactin (anterior pituitary)"}},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman 3 weeks postpartum presents with engorgement of both breasts but poor milk flow during attempts to breastfeed her newborn. Her lactation consultant suspects a problem with milk ejection rather than milk production. Which of the following correctly identifies the distinction between the hormone responsible for milk synthesis and the hormone responsible for milk ejection, and identifies which one is most likely deficient in this patient?","choices":{"C":"Prolactin from the anterior pituitary stimulates alveolar cell milk synthesis and is adequate as evidenced by breast engorgement, while oxytocin from the posterior pituitary contracts myoepithelial cells to eject milk; deficient or inhibited oxytocin-mediated ejection best explains this patient's poor milk flow","A":"Oxytocin from the anterior pituitary stimulates alveolar cell milk synthesis and is elevated as evidenced by engorgement, while prolactin from the posterior pituitary contracts myoepithelial cells to eject milk; the pattern of engorgement without letdown reflects prolactin deficiency from posterior pituitary insufficiency","B":"Both prolactin and oxytocin are secreted from the anterior pituitary in response to suckling; prolactin drives early-phase milk ejection through direct ductal smooth muscle activation, while oxytocin drives late-phase ejection through myoepithelial contraction, and this patient lacks the late-phase oxytocin response","D":"Both prolactin and oxytocin are secreted from the posterior pituitary in response to suckling and act together on the same myoepithelial-alveolar complex; prolactin synthesizes milk while oxytocin simultaneously ejects it, and the poor flow here reflects a combined deficiency of both posterior pituitary hormones","E":"Estrogen from the ovary stimulates postpartum milk production from alveolar epithelium while progesterone triggers milk ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells; the poor ejection reflects postpartum progesterone deficiency after placental delivery removes the gestational progesterone source"},"explanation":"Lactation requires two distinct hormonal processes. Prolactin, secreted from anterior pituitary lactotrophs in response to suckling, binds receptors on mammary alveolar epithelial cells and drives the synthesis of milk components including casein, lactalbumin, and lactose — this is lactogenesis. Oxytocin, released from posterior pituitary nerve terminals in response to the suckling neuroendocrine reflex, binds oxytocin receptors on myoepithelial cells that form a contractile lattice around alveoli and drives milk ejection. A woman with engorged breasts has adequate prolactin-driven milk production. Her poor milk flow despite engorgement indicates an intact synthesis mechanism but a deficient ejection reflex, most consistent with inadequate oxytocin release. Psychological stress and anxiety are well-known inhibitors of the suckling-oxytocin reflex arc, explaining why many new mothers experience this problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolactin from anterior pituitary = milk synthesis; oxytocin from posterior pituitary = milk ejection. Stress inhibits oxytocin release and impairs letdown. First Aid: oxytocin = milk letdown reflex; prolactin = lactogenesis. COMLEX: A mother with breast engorgement but poor flow needs oxytocin support, not prolactin; common clinical scenario in early postpartum period.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — consequences of over- and underproduction of pituitary hormones, including oxytocin deficiency causing prolonged labor and impaired milk ejection","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist examines an H&E section of the posterior lobe of the pituitary at autopsy. Among the unmyelinated nerve fibers and glial-type cells she sees scattered small islands of eosin-stained, granular material lying close to fenestrated capillaries. Electron microscopy shows these islands are dilated axon segments packed with membrane-bound vesicles. Which structures are these?","choices":{"D":"Herring bodies, dilated portions of hypothalamic axons near their terminals that store neurosecretory vesicles of ADH or oxytocin","A":"Pituicytes, glial-type supporting cells of the posterior lobe that synthesize ADH and oxytocin and store them in cytoplasmic granules","B":"Acidophil clusters, groups of somatotropes and lactotropes that migrate into the posterior lobe and store growth hormone granules","C":"Colloid follicles, remnants of Rathke's pouch in the pars intermedia that fill with secreted protein and stain pink in routine sections","E":"Lipofuscin deposits, wear-and-tear pigment granules in aging glial cells that collect near capillaries in the posterior lobe of older adults"},"explanation":"Pawlina (Ch 21) describes neurosecretory vesicles of the posterior lobe accumulating in axon terminals and forming accumulations that dilate portions of the axon near the terminals; these dilations, Herring bodies, are visible by light microscopy and in H&E preparations appear as small islands of eosin-stained substance. Each vesicle contains oxytocin or ADH with its neurophysin and ATP, and the Herring bodies lie near fenestrated capillaries, surrounded by pituicytes. Pituicytes are supporting cells that do not secrete hormones (Guyton Ch 76). Acidophils are anterior lobe cells and do not form axon dilations. The colloid-filled follicles of the pars intermedia are in the anterior lobe and are not axonal swellings packed with secretory vesicles. Lipofuscin is a pigment, not vesicle-filled axon segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior lobe = axon terminals of supraoptic and paraventricular neurons plus pituicytes; stored hormone appears as Herring bodies (Pawlina Ch 21). ADH and oxytocin are made as larger precursors with their neurophysin and cleaved while traveling down the axon. COMLEX: Pituicytes act only as supporting structures for the nerve endings and secrete no hormone (Guyton Ch 76).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — ADH and oxytocin are made in hypothalamic nuclei and carried down the hypothalamohypophyseal tract","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Acidophils are anterior lobe c","E":"Lipofuscin is a pigment, not v"}},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A research team performing immunohistochemistry on hypothalamic tissue finds that antibodies against neurophysin co-localize with ADH-containing secretory granules throughout the axons of the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract. When they apply the same antibody to posterior pituitary capillary extracts after nerve stimulation, neurophysin is present in low concentrations but ADH cannot be detected bound to it. Which of the following best explains this immunohistochemical finding in the post-secretion samples?","choices":{"B":"Neurophysin is co-secreted with ADH when action potentials trigger vesicle exocytosis at nerve terminals; although the two are released together, they then immediately dissociate in the extracellular space because they are only loosely bound, and neurophysin has no known function after they separate","A":"Neurophysin is cleaved from ADH by extracellular peptidases at the posterior pituitary capillary surface immediately before ADH enters circulation; the cleaved neurophysin accumulates locally while ADH is rapidly transported into the bloodstream as a free bioactive peptide without any carrier requirement","C":"Neurophysin remains tightly bound to ADH throughout its circulation in the blood and is only cleaved by renal brush-border peptidases at the glomerular filtration barrier; the low neurophysin in post-stimulation capillary extracts reflects rapid clearance of the ADH-neurophysin complex before sampling","D":"Neurophysin functions as a competitive inhibitor that blocks ADH binding to V2 receptors on renal principal cells; the low post-secretion capillary neurophysin reflects rapid uptake by V2 receptor-expressing principal cells, which strip neurophysin from ADH to allow the hormone to bind its receptor","E":"Neurophysin and ADH are packaged in separate secretory granule subpopulations within posterior pituitary nerve terminals; the absence of co-localized post-secretion binding indicates that the two proteins are released at different thresholds of action potential frequency and do not normally co-secrete"},"explanation":"Neurophysin is a carrier protein that is synthesized together with ADH and oxytocin as part of a larger precursor (pre-pro-hormone) molecule. In the secretory granules, neurophysin is non-covalently bound to the hormone and serves as a carrier during axonal transport from hypothalamic cell bodies to posterior pituitary nerve terminals. When action potentials trigger exocytosis, both neurophysin and its associated hormone are released simultaneously into the extracellular space. However, the neurophysin-hormone interaction is non-covalent and loose enough that they dissociate almost immediately upon entering the aqueous extracellular environment. Neurophysin has no identified function after separation and is eventually cleared without contributing to hormone regulation. ADH circulates as a free peptide in the blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurophysin is the carrier protein that transports ADH and oxytocin during axonal transport; they are co-secreted but dissociate immediately. Neurophysin has no known function after release. First Aid mentions neurophysins in the posterior pituitary transport pathway. COMLEX: Neurophysin mutations are associated with familial central DI — a connection between carrier protein dysfunction and hormone delivery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — hypothalamic hormone synthesis and transport via neurophysin carrier proteins to posterior pituitary storage sites","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"ADH circulates as a free pepti"}},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the synthesis of ADH and oxytocin in the hypothalamus and their storage in the posterior pituitary.","batch":2,"stem":"A pharmacology student notes that synthetic oxytocin administered at high doses during labor induction can occasionally cause antidiuretic effects, and that vasopressin at high concentrations can produce weak uterine contractions. The student asks her professor why these two posterior pituitary hormones share partial pharmacological activities. Which of the following best explains this cross-reactivity?","choices":{"C":"ADH and oxytocin are both 9-amino acid polypeptides that differ at only two positions in their amino acid sequences; the structural similarity between the two hormones allows each to bind and partially activate the other's receptor at pharmacological concentrations, producing weak cross-reactive effects","A":"ADH and oxytocin are both derived from cholesterol as steroid hormone precursors and share a common receptor-binding pharmacophore that enables cross-reactivity at all physiological concentrations; the antidiuretic effect of oxytocin and the uterotonic effect of vasopressin are thus expected even at normal hormone levels","B":"ADH and oxytocin are co-packaged in the same secretory granules within every posterior pituitary nerve terminal and are always co-released in a fixed 3:1 molar ratio; high-dose synthetic oxytocin mimics this ratio by providing sufficient ADH-equivalent activity through dose-dependent receptor activation","D":"ADH and oxytocin share an identical amino acid backbone, differing only in their C-terminal amide group modifications; the C-terminal amide of oxytocin selectively activates V2 receptors in the kidney while the C-terminal amide of ADH selectively activates oxytocin receptors in the uterus","E":"ADH and oxytocin are synthesized by the same enzyme in hypothalamic magnocellular neurons, and high concentrations of either hormone trigger transcriptional upregulation of the receptor for the other hormone in target tissues through a shared hormonal co-regulation mechanism"},"explanation":"ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin share a remarkable structural similarity: both are nonapeptides (9 amino acids) with the sequence Cys-Tyr-X-Gln-Asn-Cys-Pro-X-GlyNH2, differing only at positions 3 and 8. Vasopressin has phenylalanine at position 3 and arginine at position 8; oxytocin has isoleucine at position 3 and leucine at position 8. This near-identical structure means each hormone has some structural complementarity with the other's receptor. At pharmacological (supratherapeutic) concentrations, oxytocin can partially activate V2 receptors to produce antidiuresis, and vasopressin can partially activate oxytocin receptors to produce weak uterotonic effects. This cross-reactivity is clinically relevant during high-dose oxytocin labor induction, which can cause mild water retention. Both hormones are peptides, not steroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH and oxytocin are both 9-amino acid polypeptides differing at only 2 positions; their structural similarity explains pharmacological cross-reactivity at high doses. COMLEX: High-dose oxytocin infusion during labor induction can cause water retention by activating V2 receptors; monitor for hyponatremia in prolonged inductions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — ADH and oxytocin synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei; structural similarity between the two nonapeptides explains partial receptor cross-reactivity at pharmacological concentrations","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Both hormones are peptides, no"}},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher administers tolvaptan, a selective ADH V2 receptor antagonist, to healthy volunteers for 5 consecutive days. She measures collecting duct principal cell AQP-2 membrane localization and urine characteristics daily. Which of the following best predicts the effect of tolvaptan on renal principal cells and the resulting urine characteristics?","choices":{"E":"V2 receptor blockade prevents cAMP generation in principal cells; without PKA-mediated phosphorylation, AQP-2 vesicles cannot insert into the apical membrane and remain in the cytoplasm, leaving the collecting duct nearly impermeable to water and producing large volumes of very dilute urine despite circulating ADH","A":"V2 receptor blockade activates a compensatory V1 receptor upregulation on principal cells within 24 hours; V1 receptors use Gq-IP3-calcium signaling to drive AQP-3 insertion into the apical membrane as an alternative water reabsorption pathway, maintaining near-normal urine osmolality despite V2 blockade","B":"V2 receptor blockade reduces cAMP in principal cells but increases cGMP through compensatory ANP receptor activation; the elevated cGMP maintains AQP-2 vesicle insertion at a reduced baseline level, producing only mildly increased urine output and modest reduction in urine osmolality throughout the treatment period","C":"V2 receptor blockade prevents ADH from entering principal cells where it normally acts on intracellular nuclear receptors; without nuclear receptor activation, the AQP-2 gene is progressively silenced over 2 to 3 days, causing gradual depletion of AQP-2 protein stores and delayed onset of water diuresis","D":"V2 receptor blockade prevents ADH-stimulated sodium reabsorption in collecting duct principal cells, causing natriuresis and intravascular volume depletion; the resulting hypovolemia activates renin, generating angiotensin II that partially restores water reabsorption through AT1 receptor-mediated AQP-2 trafficking independent of cAMP"},"explanation":"Tolvaptan and conivaptan are ADH V2 receptor antagonists (vaptans) used clinically to treat SIADH. By blocking V2 receptors, they prevent ADH from activating Gs coupling and generating cAMP in principal cells. Without cAMP and PKA, AQP-2 vesicles cannot be phosphorylated and do not insert into the apical membrane, leaving the membrane water-impermeable. The result is excretion of large volumes of free water as very dilute urine (aquaresis) — the desired effect in SIADH where the goal is free water excretion to correct hyponatremia. Tolvaptan has no significant effect on sodium excretion; the aquaresis is selective for free water. There is no compensatory V1-driven AQP-3 insertion pathway. ADH is a peptide hormone acting on membrane receptors, not nuclear receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vaptans (tolvaptan, conivaptan) block V2 receptors to treat SIADH by promoting free water excretion. V2 blockade prevents cAMP-driven AQP-2 insertion. First Aid: ADH antagonists listed as SIADH treatment. COMLEX: Vaptans cause aquaresis (electrolyte-free water loss) rather than natriuresis; useful when fluid restriction fails in SIADH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gs-cAMP second messenger signaling at V2 receptors drives aquaporin-2 vesicle insertion; tolvaptan blocks this pathway by competitive V2 receptor antagonism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"There is no compensatory V1-dr","C":"ADH is a peptide hormone actin","D":"Tolvaptan has no significant e"}},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old man with severe congestive heart failure and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 20% develops progressive hyponatremia with serum sodium of 118 mEq/L and serum osmolality of 246 mOsm/kg. His plasma ADH is markedly elevated. His physician explains that despite low plasma osmolality that should suppress ADH, blood volume signals are overriding the osmolality-based suppression. Which of the following best explains how blood volume signals can override osmolality in regulating ADH secretion?","choices":{"C":"Severe reduction in effective circulating volume in CHF causes underexcitation of atrial stretch receptors and arterial baroreceptors; the loss of inhibitory tone strongly stimulates ADH secretion and can override osmolality-based suppression when volume depletion is severe","A":"In CHF, chronically elevated angiotensin II binds osmoreceptors in the organum vasculosum and pharmacologically desensitizes them, preventing detection of low serum osmolality so that ADH continues to be secreted at rates appropriate for a perceived high-osmolality state","B":"In CHF, markedly elevated ANP released from stretched atria directly activates hypothalamic ADH neurons through cGMP-dependent signaling; the paradoxical ADH elevation reflects a clinical scenario in which ANP normally inhibits ADH release but in heart failure switches to a stimulatory role","D":"In CHF, impaired hepatic degradation of ADH from hepatic congestion causes markedly prolonged ADH half-life; the excess ADH accumulates despite osmolality-based suppression of new synthesis because clearance fails to keep pace with normal physiological hypothalamic ADH secretion rates","E":"In CHF, elevated aldosterone from secondary hyperaldosteronism directly acts on hypothalamic osmoreceptor cells through mineralocorticoid receptors to prevent their normal inhibitory response to low osmolality, sustaining ADH secretion by blocking cellular shrinkage detection in osmoreceptor neurons"},"explanation":"ADH secretion responds to two primary inputs: plasma osmolality is the dominant regulator under normal conditions, but blood volume and pressure signals can override osmolality when volume depletion is severe. In advanced CHF, reduced cardiac output decreases effective circulating volume despite potential total body fluid overload. Atrial stretch receptors — normally excited by cardiac overfilling — are underexcited when cardiac output is low, removing their inhibitory signals to ADH neurons. Arterial baroreceptors in the carotid and aortic arch similarly signal low pressure, further stimulating ADH secretion. This volume-mediated ADH elevation drives water retention and hyponatremia despite low plasma osmolality that would normally suppress ADH. This is also the mechanism of dilutional hyponatremia in cirrhosis and nephrotic syndrome where effective arterial blood volume is reduced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In CHF, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome, ADH remains inappropriately elevated despite hyponatremia because baroreceptor-mediated volume signals override osmolality suppression. This is SIADH-like physiology from non-pituitary causes. First Aid: hypovolemia stimulates ADH even with low serum osmolality. COMLEX: Volume regulation overriding osmolality is a key clinical concept for hyponatremia boards questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling connects baroreceptor and atrial stretch receptor inputs to hypothalamic ADH secretion through second messenger modulation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe ADH's mechanism of action via aquaporins and its regulation by osmolarity and blood volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with refractory septic shock is receiving vasopressin infusion at a high dose as a vasopressor adjunct. His intensivist notes vasoconstriction and rising mean arterial pressure but also documents reduced urine output consistent with antidiuresis. He asks a medical student to explain why vasopressin exerts vasoconstriction at V1 receptors using a different second messenger pathway than its antidiuretic V2 receptor effect. Which of the following correctly contrasts these two receptor pathways?","choices":{"B":"V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq-coupled and activate phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG, which release SR calcium and activate PKC to cause vasoconstriction. V2 receptors on renal principal cells are Gs-coupled and activate adenylyl cyclase, driving PKA-mediated AQP-2 vesicle insertion for water reabsorption","A":"V1 receptors and V2 receptors both use Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP in their respective target cells; V1 receptors in smooth muscle produce vasoconstriction when high cAMP activates a smooth muscle-specific PKA isoform that phosphorylates myosin rather than aquaporin-2, and V2 receptors produce water reabsorption through a principal cell-specific PKA isoform","C":"V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are directly ligand-gated calcium channels that open upon vasopressin binding without any G protein coupling; V2 receptors are Gs-coupled GPCRs that increase cAMP and drive aquaporin-2 insertion through PKA, making these two receptors mechanistically entirely distinct in their signal initiation","D":"Both V1 and V2 receptors use Gq-phospholipase C signaling, but differ in downstream targets: V1 receptors use IP3-generated calcium to activate calmodulin kinase for smooth muscle contraction, while V2 receptors use DAG-activated PKC to phosphorylate aquaporin-2 vesicles for apical membrane insertion in principal cells","E":"V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are nuclear receptor steroid-binding domains expressed on the plasma membrane; vasopressin activates them by mimicking a steroid-like conformation and upregulating contractile protein genes. V2 receptors on renal principal cells are Gs-coupled GPCRs that increase cAMP to drive AQP-2 insertion"},"explanation":"ADH acts through two distinct receptor subtypes with completely different G protein couplings. V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle cells are Gq-coupled GPCRs; their activation generates IP3 (which triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum) and DAG (which activates PKC), both contributing to smooth muscle contraction and arteriolar vasoconstriction. This is why ADH is also called vasopressin, and why it is used clinically in shock to raise mean arterial pressure. V2 receptors on renal collecting duct principal cells are Gs-coupled GPCRs; their activation increases cAMP, activates PKA, and drives phosphorylation-dependent apical membrane insertion of AQP-2 vesicles. At low physiological ADH concentrations, the antidiuretic V2 effect predominates. At high ADH concentrations — in hemorrhage or when pharmacologically infused in sepsis — V1-mediated vasoconstriction becomes prominent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: V1 = Gq = vasoconstriction (vasopressin); V2 = Gs = antidiuresis (aquaporin-2). First Aid: ADH regulates blood pressure via V1 receptors and serum osmolality via V2 receptors. COMLEX: Vasopressin is used in septic shock to raise MAP through V1-mediated vasoconstriction; also causes antidiuresis through V2 at the same time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gs-cAMP and Gq-IP3/DAG signaling represent the two G protein-coupled second messenger pathways activated by ADH V2 and V1 receptors respectively","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with schizophrenia is evaluated for polyuria of about 7 L per day. Serum sodium is 133 mEq/L and serum osmolality is 275 mOsm/kg. Urine osmolality is 90 mOsm/kg. During supervised water restriction, her urine osmolality rises to 760 mOsm/kg. Which best explains her polyuria?","choices":{"A":"Primary polydipsia: excess water intake suppresses ADH, and once intake stops, ADH release and the collecting duct response restore concentrated urine","B":"Central diabetes insipidus: deficient hypothalamic ADH release causes water loss, and restriction concentrates urine by raising the medullary gradient","C":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus: the collecting duct cannot respond to ADH, and restriction concentrates urine through a rise in proximal reabsorption","D":"SIADH: autonomous ADH release dilutes the serum, and restriction raises urine osmolality by removing the excess water that ADH was retaining","E":"Osmotic diuresis: an unmeasured solute keeps water in the tubule, and restriction concentrates urine once the solute load has been excreted"},"explanation":"First Aid 2025 (p 342) distinguishes primary polydipsia from diabetes insipidus: in primary polydipsia serum osmolality is low and ADH is low or normal, and water restriction produces a significant rise in urine osmolality to above 700 mOsm/kg. In central and nephrogenic DI, serum osmolality is high, and water restriction produces no change or only a slight rise in urine osmolality. Her low serum osmolality and marked concentrating response show an intact ADH axis that was suppressed by excess intake; causes include psychiatric illness and hypothalamic lesions affecting the thirst center. Central DI would not concentrate the urine much with restriction. Nephrogenic DI also fails to concentrate with restriction. SIADH produces urine osmolality greater than serum osmolality, not a urine osmolality of 90 mOsm/kg. An osmotic diuresis produces urine that is not maximally dilute.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary polydipsia vs DI (First Aid 2025 p 342): serum osmolality ↓ in primary polydipsia, ↑ in DI; water restriction raises urine osmolality above 700 mOsm/kg in primary polydipsia but little in DI; desmopressin then separates central DI (rise above 50%) from nephrogenic DI. COMLEX: Treat primary polydipsia with water restriction; treat central DI with desmopressin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — diabetes insipidus and SIADH pathogenesis, diagnosis, and management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Central DI would not concentra","C":"Nephrogenic DI also fails to c","D":"SIADH produces urine osmolalit"}},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with fatigue, confusion, and nausea. Her serum sodium is 119 mEq/L and serum osmolality is 250 mOsm/kg. Urine osmolality is 620 mOsm/kg and urine sodium is 48 mEq/L. She is euvolemic on exam with no edema. Chest imaging shows a central hilar mass. Which of the following correctly identifies the diagnosis and explains the ADH-mediated mechanism responsible for her hyponatremia?","choices":{"B":"SIADH from ectopic ADH secretion by a small cell lung carcinoma; the tumor secretes ADH autonomously independent of plasma osmolality, causing V2 receptor-mediated AQP-2 insertion in collecting duct principal cells, excessive free water retention, diluted serum sodium, and inappropriately concentrated urine","A":"Central DI from paraneoplastic destruction of hypothalamic ADH-secreting supraoptic neurons by anti-Hu antibodies produced by the small cell lung carcinoma; absent ADH causes free water wasting, elevated serum osmolality, and dilute polyuric urine with hypernatremia rather than the hyponatremia seen here","C":"Nephrogenic DI from small cell carcinoma-secreted anti-V2 receptor antibodies that block ADH binding to renal principal cells; the free water wasting despite elevated endogenous ADH produces dilute urine and hypernatremia, not the concentrated urine and hyponatremia present in this patient","D":"Primary hyperaldosteronism from ectopic aldosterone secretion by the hilar tumor mass; excess aldosterone causes sodium and water retention through MR-mediated ENaC upregulation and Na-K-ATPase stimulation, producing hypertension, hypokalemia, and concentrated urine through volume-driven suppression of ADH","E":"Adrenal insufficiency from paraneoplastic ACTH-receptor antibodies blocking adrenal cortisol synthesis; the resulting cortisol deficiency removes normal inhibition of ADH secretion, permitting mild ADH elevation that produces the hyponatremia, but free water retention from cortisol deficiency also contributes independently"},"explanation":"This is the classic SIADH presentation from ectopic ADH secretion by small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC). SCLC is the most common malignant cause of SIADH. The tumor secretes functional ADH (arginine vasopressin) autonomously, bypassing normal osmolality-based feedback. Persistently elevated ADH activates V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, driving continuous AQP-2 insertion and free water reabsorption. The body retains water, diluting serum sodium and osmolality. The kidneys continue excreting sodium normally (reflected by urine Na+ above 30 mEq/L per Bootcamp and concentrated urine osmolality above serum osmolality), producing euvolemic hyponatremia. First Aid mnemonic for SIADH causes: HEELD-up water — Head trauma, Ectopic ADH (SCLC), Exogenous hormones, Lung disease, Drugs (SSRIs, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH from SCLC = euvolemic hyponatremia, urine osmolality greater than serum osmolality, urine Na greater than 30 (Bootcamp). HEELD mnemonic. First Aid lists ectopic ADH from SCLC as a classic SIADH cause. COMLEX: SIADH is a named CP3 condition; recognize the clinical triad of hyponatremia, concentrated urine, and euvolemia. Treatment: fluid restriction first line, then hypertonic saline, demeclocycline, or vaptans.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — SIADH pathogenesis from ectopic ADH secretion produces euvolemic hyponatremia through autonomous V2 receptor activation independent of osmolality","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with bipolar disorder has been taking lithium carbonate for 9 years. He develops polyuria of 5 liters per day and polydipsia. Serum osmolality is 298 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality is 120 mOsm/kg, and plasma ADH is elevated at 22 pg/mL. Desmopressin administration produces no significant rise in urine osmolality. Which of the following correctly identifies the mechanism by which lithium causes this condition?","choices":{"C":"Lithium enters collecting duct principal cells through apical ENaC channels and inhibits adenylyl cyclase or downstream cAMP signaling pathways, reducing the cAMP available to phosphorylate AQP-2 vesicles, preventing their insertion into the apical membrane despite normal or elevated plasma ADH and normal V2 receptor expression","A":"Lithium accumulates in posterior pituitary nerve terminals and disrupts the calcium-dependent exocytosis mechanism required for ADH granule fusion with the plasma membrane, producing central DI with absent plasma ADH; the elevated plasma ADH measured in this patient represents cross-reactivity of lithium metabolites with the ADH radioimmunoassay","B":"Lithium chelates neurophysin carrier proteins in hypothalamic axons, blocking ADH transport from supraoptic neuronal cell bodies to posterior pituitary nerve terminals; ADH accumulates in hypothalamic cell bodies producing local toxicity, while falsely elevated serum ADH reflects overflow of unbound ADH from damaged hypothalamic neurons","D":"Lithium competitively antagonizes ADH at the V2 receptor extracellular binding domain, preventing ADH from activating Gs protein coupling; because desmopressin uses the identical receptor-binding domain as endogenous ADH, desmopressin administration is also ineffective because lithium simultaneously blocks exogenous desmopressin from binding V2 receptors","E":"Lithium activates the phosphodiesterase enzyme that degrades cAMP in principal cells; even when ADH successfully activates V2 receptors and adenylyl cyclase generates normal cAMP, lithium-activated PDE rapidly hydrolyzes the cAMP before PKA can phosphorylate AQP-2 vesicles, preventing all aquaporin-2 insertion despite normal upstream signaling"},"explanation":"Lithium causes nephrogenic DI by interfering with the intracellular cAMP signaling pathway in collecting duct principal cells. Lithium is a monovalent cation that enters principal cells through apical ENaC channels (mistaken for sodium). Inside the cell, lithium impairs adenylyl cyclase activation and may also affect other downstream cAMP pathway components, reducing the cAMP available to activate PKA. Without adequate PKA activity, AQP-2 vesicles are not phosphorylated and do not insert into the apical membrane. Because the V2 receptor and upstream signaling are not blocked at the receptor level, desmopressin — which also activates V2 receptors — cannot overcome the intracellular block; desmopressin would be equally ineffective since the downstream machinery is impaired. Elevated plasma ADH reflects normal osmoreceptor-driven secretion in response to rising serum osmolality; the kidneys cannot respond. This is classic nephrogenic DI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lithium causes nephrogenic DI by blocking intracellular cAMP signaling downstream of the V2 receptor; elevated ADH, no desmopressin response. Also: demeclocycline, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia cause nephrogenic DI. First Aid: nephrogenic DI from lithium or demeclocycline is hereditary or drug-induced ADH resistance. COMLEX: Long-term lithium use requires monitoring for nephrogenic DI; treat with low-solute diet, HCTZ, and amiloride.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — nephrogenic DI pathogenesis from intracellular ADH signaling disruption, distinguishable from central DI by elevated ADH and absent desmopressin response","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Because the V2 receptor and up"}},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old G1P0 woman at 41 weeks gestation is admitted for elective labor induction. Her obstetrician starts an oxytocin infusion (Pitocin) and explains the mechanism of action to a medical student on the team. Which of the following correctly identifies the oxytocin receptor type on uterine myometrial cells and the intracellular signal transduction pathway that produces uterine contractions?","choices":{"B":"Oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrial smooth muscle are Gq-coupled GPCRs that activate phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG; IP3 triggers SR calcium release and DAG activates PKC, both driving smooth muscle contraction; receptor density increases near term due to estrogen-driven upregulation, enhancing oxytocin sensitivity","A":"Oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrial smooth muscle are Gs-coupled GPCRs that activate adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP and activate PKA; PKA phosphorylates myosin light chain kinase to enhance myosin-actin cross-bridge cycling, a pathway identical to that of beta-2 adrenergic agonists but producing contraction rather than relaxation through a cell-type-specific PKA substrate","C":"Oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrial smooth muscle are tyrosine kinase receptors that dimerize upon oxytocin binding and undergo autophosphorylation; activated intracellular kinase domains then phosphorylate JAK2, which activates STAT5, translocates to the nucleus, and upregulates myosin heavy chain gene expression for sustained myometrial contractility","D":"Oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrial smooth muscle are nuclear hormone receptors located in the cytoplasm; oxytocin crosses the plasma membrane as a partially lipophilic nonapeptide, binds the cytoplasmic receptor, and the complex translocates to the nucleus to activate estrogen-response elements in myosin promoter regions for sustained contractile gene expression","E":"Oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrial smooth muscle are Gi-coupled GPCRs that decrease intracellular cAMP; reduced cAMP removes PKA-mediated phosphorylation of myosin light chain phosphatase, allowing the phosphatase to remain inactive and permitting myosin light chain to stay phosphorylated and sustain contraction through tonic smooth muscle activation"},"explanation":"Oxytocin receptors are Gq-coupled GPCRs. Activation of Gq stimulates phospholipase C-beta, which cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. IP3 binds IP3 receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing stored calcium into the cytoplasm; the calcium binds calmodulin, activates MLCK, and promotes myosin light chain phosphorylation and smooth muscle contraction. DAG simultaneously activates PKC, which phosphorylates additional contractile proteins and potentiates contraction. The density of oxytocin receptors on uterine myometrium rises dramatically near term, driven by increasing estrogen concentrations; this explains why the uterus becomes progressively more sensitive to oxytocin as pregnancy approaches 40 weeks. Gs-cAMP-PKA in smooth muscle actually promotes relaxation (beta-2 agonists are used as tocolytics), not contraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oxytocin receptor = Gq = IP3/DAG = smooth muscle contraction. Gs-cAMP in smooth muscle = relaxation (tocolysis). Important pharmacological contrast: beta-2 agonists such as terbutaline are tocolytics because they work through Gs-cAMP-PKA to relax uterine smooth muscle. COMLEX: Oxytocin receptor upregulation near term by estrogen explains why labor timing is estrogen-dependent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — consequences of pituitary hormone overproduction include uterine hyperstimulation and fetal distress from excessive synthetic oxytocin, and underproduction causes prolonged labor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Gs-cAMP-PKA in smooth muscle a"}},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old G2P1 woman in active labor is at 7 cm cervical dilation. Her labor and delivery nurse explains to a nursing student that internal examination at this dilation will likely intensify contractions. The nurse refers to this as the \"Ferguson reflex.\" Which of the following best describes the neuroendocrine pathway underlying this positive feedback mechanism?","choices":{"E":"Cervical stretch exerts negative feedback on oxytocin secretion during the latent phase to prevent premature labor; at the transition to active labor, rapidly rising cortisol from fetal adrenal maturation removes this negative feedback gate, allowing the Ferguson reflex to convert to a stimulatory positive feedback arc","A":"Cervical stretch activates local COX-2-mediated prostaglandin E2 synthesis in cervical stromal fibroblasts; prostaglandin E2 then directly activates myometrial smooth muscle through EP2 receptors coupled to cAMP-independent IP3 generation, bypassing the hypothalamic-pituitary axis entirely and making the reflex completely autonomous","B":"Mechanical stretch of the cervix generates sensory afferent signals that travel to the hypothalamic paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, increasing oxytocin release from posterior pituitary nerve terminals; stronger contractions then dilate the cervix further, stimulating more stretch receptor firing and amplifying the cycle","C":"Cervical stretch activates adrenal medullary chromaffin cells through the lumbosacral sympathetic chain, releasing epinephrine that binds alpha-1 receptors on uterine myometrium to directly cause contractions; the oxytocin axis serves only a permissive priming role by upregulating myometrial alpha-1 receptor density near term","D":"Cervical stretch activates hypothalamic ghrelin neurons in the arcuate nucleus, which release ghrelin into the median eminence portal blood; ghrelin then activates GHS-R1a receptors on posterior pituitary oxytocin nerve terminals to directly trigger oxytocin exocytosis without involving hypothalamic magnocellular neuron firing"},"explanation":"The Ferguson reflex is a well-characterized positive feedback loop in labor. Cervical distension and stretch activate mechanosensitive afferent nerve fibers in the cervical stroma and ligaments. These signals travel through ascending sensory pathways to the hypothalamus, specifically activating magnocellular oxytocin neurons in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei. Increased action potential firing propagates down the hypothalamic-hypophysial tract to posterior pituitary nerve terminals, triggering exocytosis of oxytocin into circulation. The oxytocin reaches uterine myometrium, activates Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors, releases intracellular calcium, and intensifies contractions. Stronger contractions dilate the cervix further, stimulating more stretch receptor firing and continuing the positive feedback cycle. This loop accelerates until delivery. Clinical evidence: hypophysectomy prolongs labor; plasma oxytocin rises progressively during active labor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ferguson reflex = cervical stretch → hypothalamic oxytocin neurons → posterior pituitary oxytocin release → more contractions → more cervical dilation → positive feedback loop. This is the classic example of positive endocrine feedback. First Aid: oxytocin causes uterine contractions during labor. COMLEX: Understand the neuroendocrine arc; disruption by posterior pituitary or stalk pathology would impair this reflex and prolong labor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — consequences of pituitary underproduction: posterior pituitary dysfunction impairs the Ferguson reflex-mediated oxytocin surge, prolonging labor as seen in hypophysectomized animals","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe oxytocin's mechanism of action and roles in labor and lactation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old first-time mother at 3 weeks postpartum calls her lactation consultant reporting that despite her breasts feeling full and engorged before every feeding, very little milk flows when her newborn latches and suckles. She describes significant anxiety, sleep deprivation, and stress since returning home from the hospital. Her infant has had inadequate weight gain. The lactation consultant suspects an impaired milk ejection reflex. Which of the following best explains the physiological mechanism by which psychological stress impairs milk letdown in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Psychological stress activates the sympathetic nervous system and elevates catecholamines, which inhibit the hypothalamic oxytocin neuron firing that suckling afferents normally trigger; reduced oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary impairs myoepithelial cell contraction and milk ejection despite adequate prolactin-driven milk synthesis","B":"Psychological stress directly suppresses anterior pituitary prolactin secretion through hypothalamic dopamine pathway activation; the resulting prolactin deficiency prevents milk synthesis in alveolar epithelial cells, producing apparent engorgement from unprocessed milk precursors that accumulate without being converted into mature breast milk","C":"Psychological stress causes cortisol-mediated downregulation of oxytocin receptor expression on alveolar epithelial cells through glucocorticoid response element repression of the OXTR gene; the reduced receptor density means that normal circulating oxytocin cannot effectively trigger myoepithelial contraction even when secreted normally","D":"Psychological stress causes excessive posterior pituitary action potential firing and depletes the total oxytocin store within posterior pituitary nerve terminals faster than hypothalamic synthesis can replenish it; the oxytocin reserve becomes exhausted within 2 to 3 days of sustained stress, and cannot be rebuilt while emotional stressors remain present","E":"Psychological stress produces excessive prolactin secretion through CRH-mediated anterior pituitary stimulation; the very elevated prolactin then suppresses GnRH, and the resulting estrogen deficiency impairs maintenance of myoepithelial cell contractile protein expression needed for the milk ejection response to oxytocin"},"explanation":"Milk ejection requires a neuroendocrine reflex: suckling → sensory afferents → hypothalamic oxytocin neurons → posterior pituitary exocytosis → systemic oxytocin → myoepithelial cell contraction. This reflex arc is highly sensitive to psychological state. Stress and anxiety activate the sympathetic adrenergic system, releasing epinephrine and norepinephrine that inhibit hypothalamic oxytocin neuron activity and suppress oxytocin release from posterior pituitary terminals. The result is a mother with full, engorged breasts from adequate prolactin-driven milk synthesis who cannot release that milk because oxytocin-dependent myoepithelial contraction is blocked. This is a clinically significant and common problem managed with lactation support, relaxation techniques, skin-to-skin contact with the infant, and occasionally intranasal oxytocin spray. Prolactin from the anterior pituitary drives synthesis and is not primarily impaired by sympathetic stress activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress inhibits oxytocin release and impairs milk ejection; milk production from prolactin is preserved. Engorgement with poor flow = oxytocin deficiency. First Aid: oxytocin is responsible for milk letdown reflex; prolactin is responsible for lactogenesis. COMLEX: Lactation physiology is a board topic; distinguish the anterior pituitary prolactin role in synthesis from the posterior pituitary oxytocin role in ejection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — consequences of pituitary hormone underproduction: inadequate posterior pituitary oxytocin release under stress impairs milk letdown while anterior pituitary prolactin-driven synthesis continues normally","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Prolactin from the anterior pi"}}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":1,"stem":"What percentage of the pituitary gland does the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) comprise, and which lobe receives hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones via the portal circulation?","choices":{"A":"The adenohypophysis comprises approximately 80% of the pituitary gland and receives hypothalamic hormones via the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal venous system originating from the median eminence","B":"The neurohypophysis comprises approximately 80% of the pituitary gland and receives hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones via a dedicated portal venous system running through the infundibular stalk","C":"The adenohypophysis comprises approximately 50% of the pituitary gland and receives hypothalamic hormones via direct arterial branches from the circle of Willis rather than a portal venous system","D":"The neurohypophysis comprises approximately 50% of the pituitary gland and receives direct axonal projections from hypothalamic nuclei that deliver releasing hormones at axon terminals","E":"The adenohypophysis comprises approximately 80% of the pituitary gland but does not receive hypothalamic input directly; it is regulated solely by peripheral hormone feedback from target endocrine organs"},"explanation":"The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) makes up ~80% of the pituitary and is entirely dependent on the hypothalamic-hypophysial portal system for trophic regulation. The portal blood flows from fenestrated capillaries at the median eminence down the pituitary stalk to the anterior lobe sinusoids, delivering TRH, CRH, GHRH, GnRH, somatostatin, and dopamine. The neurohypophysis (posterior lobe, ~20%) is not regulated by portal flow; instead, hypothalamic neurons from the SON and PVN project axons directly into the posterior lobe where they release ADH and oxytocin at Herring bodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic question pairing — \"anterior lobe = portal blood, posterior lobe = axonal transport.\" Disruption of the stalk (trauma, compression) paradoxically elevates prolactin (loss of dopamine inhibition) while lowering all other anterior pituitary hormones. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflex levels for pituitary/hypothalamic pathology involve the cervical and upper thoracic spine; students are expected to know the HP axis anatomy in the context of osteopathic evaluation of head/neck complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs provide the cellular framework for understanding pituitary hyper- and hypo-function","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The neurohypophysis (posterior"}},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":1,"stem":"Which transcription factor lineage specifies somatotroph, lactotroph, and thyrotroph cells of the anterior pituitary, and which distinct lineage specifies corticotroph cells?","choices":{"C":"Somatotroph, lactotroph, and thyrotroph cells share the POU1F1 (Pit-1) transcription factor lineage; corticotroph cells are specified by a separate TBX19 (Tpit) transcription factor lineage","A":"Somatotroph, lactotroph, and thyrotroph cells share the TBX19 (Tpit) transcription factor lineage; corticotroph cells are specified by the SF-1/GATA-2 lineage that also governs gonadotroph differentiation","B":"Somatotroph and corticotroph cells share the POU1F1 (Pit-1) lineage; lactotroph and thyrotroph cells are specified by the TBX19 transcription factor and exhibit overlapping regulatory control with corticotrophs","D":"All anterior pituitary cell types share the POU1F1 (Pit-1) transcription factor as a master regulator; cell-type differentiation is then determined by downstream hormonal signals and receptor expression rather than distinct lineage-specifying factors","E":"Gonadotroph and lactotroph cells share the POU1F1 (Pit-1) lineage; somatotrophs and thyrotrophs are specified by the SF-1/GATA-2 transcription factor lineage shared with adrenal cortex cells"},"explanation":"Anterior pituitary cells are classified by transcription factor lineage, which is diagnostically important in PitNET pathology classification. POU1F1 (Pit-1) lineage: somatotrophs (GH), mammosomatotrophs (GH+PRL), lactotrophs (PRL), and thyrotrophs (TSH). TBX19 (Tpit) lineage: corticotrophs (ACTH). SF-1/GATA-2 lineage: gonadotrophs (FSH/LH). Knowing the lineage predicts which hormones a PitNET might co-secrete — for example, a POU1F1-lineage PitNET might express both GH and PRL.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transcription factor lineages are tested in the context of PitNET classification. If a tumor co-secretes GH and PRL (mammosomatotroph), it is POU1F1 lineage. If a tumor secretes ACTH, it is TBX19/Tpit lineage and causes Cushing disease. COMLEX: Understanding lineage helps predict Nelson syndrome risk — the corticotroph (TBX19) lineage tumor that causes Cushing disease is the same tumor that expands after bilateral adrenalectomy removes cortisol feedback.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs provide the cellular framework for understanding pituitary hyper- and hypo-function","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":1,"stem":"What minimum percentage of anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed before clinically significant hypopituitarism becomes apparent, and what accounts for this large functional reserve?","choices":{"B":"Approximately 75% of the anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed before clinically significant hypopituitarism is detectable; the gland has a large functional reserve because each cell type is present in numbers far exceeding the minimum needed for basal hormone secretion","A":"Approximately 25% of the anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed before clinically significant hypopituitarism develops; the gland has a limited reserve because each cell type occupies a strict anatomical zone and focal destruction of any zone produces immediate hormonal deficits","C":"Approximately 50% of the anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed; this threshold reflects the moderate reserve capacity of pituitary cells, which can upregulate hormone synthesis twofold to compensate for partial cell loss before clinical deficits emerge","D":"Approximately 90% of the anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed; the gland has exceptional reserve capacity because remaining cells undergo mitotic amplification within weeks of significant cell loss, rapidly restoring hormone secretory capacity","E":"Approximately 60% of the anterior pituitary parenchyma must be destroyed; this threshold is considerably lower in postpartum women because estrogen-induced lactotroph hyperplasia during pregnancy leaves the gland with a smaller functional reserve after involution"},"explanation":"The 75% threshold for hypopituitarism is a classic board fact. The anterior pituitary has enormous reserve capacity — only a fraction of each cell type is needed to maintain trophic secretion for target endocrine glands. This explains why slow-growing pituitary adenomas can reach considerable size before producing hypopituitarism, and why acute infarction (as in Sheehan syndrome) requires destruction of most of the gland before multiple hormonal axes fail. Clinical deficits appear sequentially, with GH first and ACTH/TSH last.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"75% of pituitary must be destroyed for hypopituitarism\" is a First Aid classic. Also remember the sequence of hormone loss in progressive hypopituitarism: GH → FSH/LH → TSH → ACTH (mnemonic: \"Go Find The Adrenal\"). Prolactin rises rather than falls with stalk lesions due to loss of dopamine inhibition. COMLEX: Hypopituitarism following head trauma is a known consequence of TBI; osteopathic management of cervical spine dysfunction may be relevant to evaluating pituitary stalk injury in the post-traumatic patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs provide the cellular framework for understanding pituitary hyper- and hypo-function","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":1,"stem":"A molecular analysis of a somatotroph pituitary neuroendocrine tumor reveals an activating point mutation in GNAS, the gene encoding the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein. Which mechanism best explains how this mutation drives autonomous tumor growth and hormone hypersecretion?","choices":{"E":"The GNAS mutation impairs the intrinsic GTPase activity of Gsα, preventing hydrolysis of bound GTP to GDP; the alpha subunit remains locked in the active GTP-bound conformation, constitutively stimulating adenylyl cyclase to produce cAMP and driving continuous cellular proliferation and GH hypersecretion independent of GHRH receptor activation","A":"The GNAS mutation activates a receptor tyrosine kinase pathway by phosphorylating Gsα at a serine residue, stimulating downstream RAS-MAPK signaling and bypassing normal GPCR-dependent growth regulation in the somatotroph cell","B":"The GNAS mutation causes Gsα to bind GDP with higher affinity, preventing its normal dissociation from the receptor complex and prolonging downstream signaling through the IP3/DAG second messenger pathway rather than the cAMP pathway","C":"The GNAS mutation disrupts the Gβγ subunit interaction surface on Gsα, allowing free Gβγ to constitutively stimulate phospholipase C and drive calcium-mediated cellular proliferation and GH secretion independent of normal receptor coupling","D":"The GNAS mutation causes receptor internalization to fail after GHRH binds, increasing surface density of GHRH receptors on somatotroph cells and amplifying GH release by prolonging ligand-receptor contact beyond normal feedback termination"},"explanation":"GNAS activating mutations are found in approximately 40% of somatotroph PitNETs. Normally, after GHRH binds its GPCR, Gsα exchanges GDP for GTP and stimulates adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → GH secretion. GTPase activity on Gsα then hydrolyzes GTP to GDP, terminating the signal. The activating GNAS mutation eliminates this GTPase \"off-switch,\" locking Gsα in the active state, producing constitutive cAMP → proliferation and GH hypersecretion independent of hypothalamic input. The wrong choices describe RAS-MAPK, IP3/DAG, Gβγ/PLC, and receptor internalization — none of which is the GNAS mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GNAS mutations are the only somatotroph PitNET mutation listed in most Step 1 resources. The mechanism is \"constitutive Gs-alpha → constitutive cAMP\" — same principle as cholera toxin (ADP-ribosylates Gsα, preventing GTPase activity). COMLEX: Acromegaly physical exam findings include prognathism, macroglossa, widely spaced teeth, and carpal tunnel syndrome; these are named CP3 physical exam findings for the endocrine system.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling is constitutively activated by GNAS mutations in somatotroph pituitary neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The wrong choices describe RAS","B":"The wrong choices describe RAS","C":"The wrong choices describe RAS","D":"The wrong choices describe RAS"}},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":1,"stem":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma and papillary craniopharyngioma are two histologic variants arising from Rathke pouch remnants. Which combination of molecular mutation, age predilection, and pathognomonic histologic finding correctly distinguishes the adamantinomatous variant?","choices":{"D":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma predominantly affects children and adolescents (peak age 5–15 years), carries activating CTNNB1 (β-catenin) mutations, and is characterized by \"wet keratin\" ghost cells with dystrophic calcification and cysts containing dark brown \"machine oil\" fluid; the papillary variant instead affects adults, with BRAF V600E mutations and solid sheets of non-keratinizing squamous epithelium without calcification","A":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma predominantly affects adults over 65 years, carries BRAF V600E mutations, and is characterized by papillary fronds of non-keratinizing squamous epithelium without calcification; the papillary variant affects children and adolescents with CTNNB1 mutations and the \"machine oil\" fluid cysts","B":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma exclusively occurs in adults over 65 years without cystic components, carries USP8 deubiquitinase mutations causing EGFR overactivation, and shows oncocytic cell morphology; the papillary variant occurs in children and contains cholesterol-rich yellow fluid cysts","C":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma predominantly affects children and adolescents, carries USP8 mutations, and shows characteristic deposition of amyloid-like material between tumor cell clusters; the papillary variant affects adults, carries CTNNB1 mutations, and shows the \"machine oil\" fluid and ghost cell keratinization","E":"Both adamantinomatous and papillary craniopharyngiomas carry BRAF V600E mutations but differ only in age of onset and cystic content; CTNNB1 mutations are associated with a third rare variant called the granular cell type that occurs predominantly in elderly patients"},"explanation":"Craniopharyngioma has a bimodal age distribution, 5 to 15 years and 65 years or older (Robbins Ch 24); the adamantinomatous type is mostly seen in children and the papillary type in adults. Adamantinomatous: CTNNB1/β-catenin activating mutation → nuclear β-catenin accumulation in \"whorled\" clusters; wet keratin (ghost cells), dystrophic calcification visible on CT, cysts with dark cholesterol-rich fluid. Papillary: BRAF V600E in >90% of cases → clinically important because papillary variant responds to BRAF inhibitors; solid sheets of benign squamous epithelium without calcification or \"wet keratin.\" Both arise from Rathke pouch epithelial remnants along the pituitary stalk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Craniopharyngioma = calcified suprasellar cystic tumor in a child; BRAF mutation = papillary variant in adults that responds to vemurafenib.\" First Aid also lists bitemporal hemianopia, DI, and hypopituitarism as presenting features. COMLEX: Craniopharyngioma is listed in CP3 as a named pituitary/suprasellar tumor; students are expected to recognize CT calcification and \"machine oil\" fluid as distinguishing features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling is constitutively activated by GNAS mutations in somatotroph pituitary neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Papillary: BRAF V600E in >90% ","E":"Papillary: BRAF V600E in >90% "}},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":1,"stem":"A biopsy was obtained from a sellar mass in a 45-year-old man presenting with progressive bitemporal hemianopia and headache. Histologic sections demonstrate sheets of uniform cells with round nuclei, inconspicuous nucleoli, and pale to eosinophilic cytoplasm. A reticulin stain (not shown) reveals absence of the normal acinar network. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these histologic findings?","choices":{"C":"Metastatic carcinoma to the pituitary — metastatic lesions typically show pleomorphic cells with prominent nucleoli, irregular nuclear contours, mitotic figures, and necrosis; the striking monomorphism and absence of nuclear pleomorphism here favor a primary neuroendocrine neoplasm over a metastatic process","A":"Craniopharyngioma — this tumor arises from Rathke pouch remnants and characteristically shows squamoid epithelial cells with peripheral columnar palisading, central ghost cell keratinization, dystrophic calcification, and cyst formation rather than the monomorphic endocrine-type sheets described here","B":"Rathke cleft cyst — this benign developmental cyst is lined by a single layer of cuboidal to columnar ciliated epithelium; it does not produce the solid cellular sheets, absent reticulin pattern, or the degree of cellular monomorphism seen in this biopsy","D":"Pituitary neuroendocrine tumor (PitNET) — cellular monomorphism, an absent reticulin acinar network, and a primary sellar location establish the diagnosis; the hormonal phenotype (null cell, somatotroph, etc.) requires immunohistochemistry for transcription factors and hormones","E":"Normal anterior pituitary — the normal gland is organized into acini defined by a reticulin network containing mixed cell types including acidophils, basophils, and chromophobes; the loss of acinar architecture and cellular monomorphism described in this biopsy indicate a clonal neoplastic process rather than normal tissue"},"explanation":"PitNET (formerly \"pituitary adenoma\") histology is defined by two key features: (1) cellular monomorphism — all cells look the same, unlike the mixed cell types in normal pituitary; (2) absent reticulin network — normal pituitary has reticulin organizing cells into acini; PitNETs disrupt this framework, replacing it with sheets or trabeculae. These features are diagnostic by H&E and reticulin stain. Immunohistochemistry (GH, PRL, ACTH, TSH, FSH/LH, SF-1, POU1F1, TBX19) is needed to classify the tumor further. The other choices describe the characteristic histology of each alternative diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"PitNET histology = monomorphic cells + absent reticulin.\" This is also why PitNETs are sometimes called \"chromophobe adenomas\" on H&E when non-secreting (pale cytoplasm). Know that PitNETs can invade the cavernous sinus or erode the sella floor — invasion alone does NOT make a PitNET malignant; malignancy requires documented metastasis. COMLEX: Pituitary macroadenoma (>1 cm) causes bitemporal hemianopia by compressing the optic chiasm from below — a named CP3 visual field finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling is constitutively activated by GNAS mutations in somatotroph pituitary neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"A lactotroph pituitary neuroendocrine tumor causing amenorrhea and galactorrhea is treated with a dopamine agonist. Which receptor-level mechanism explains why dopamine agonists reduce both serum prolactin levels and tumor volume in this setting?","choices":{"B":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin secretion via D2 receptors on lactotrophs; agonists such as bromocriptine and cabergoline activate Gi signaling that lowers cAMP and calcium influx, suppressing prolactin synthesis and secretion and shrinking the tumor","A":"Dopamine agonists bind D1 receptors on pituitary somatostatin neurons, stimulating somatostatin release from the median eminence; the increased somatostatin then acts on somatostatin receptors on lactotroph cells to suppress prolactin secretion through a SSTR5-mediated reduction in intracellular calcium","C":"Dopamine agonists block estrogen receptors on lactotroph cells, removing the primary mitogenic stimulus for prolactin gene transcription and lactotroph proliferation; tumor shrinkage results from estrogen receptor-mediated apoptosis that is unmasked when estrogen signaling is pharmacologically suppressed","D":"Dopamine agonists compete with TRH at thyrotroph receptors, preventing the normal TRH-stimulated TSH surge that cross-activates prolactin gene transcription via shared cAMP/PKA downstream elements in the anterior pituitary cell nucleus","E":"Dopamine agonists increase GnRH pulsatility from the arcuate nucleus by disinhibiting kisspeptin neurons; the resulting FSH/LH surge then feedback-suppresses prolactin via a cross-regulatory HPG-lactotroph axis that is active during the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle"},"explanation":"Prolactin is unique among anterior pituitary hormones in being under predominant inhibitory hypothalamic control via dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor). Dopamine acts on D2 receptors (Gi-coupled) on lactotrophs → inhibits adenylyl cyclase → reduces cAMP → reduces prolactin synthesis and secretion. Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) mimic this inhibition and are the first-line treatment for prolactinoma. Cabergoline is preferred (half-life about 65 hours versus about 7 hours for bromocriptine, and less nausea). Dopamine agonists shrink prolactin-secreting tumors, lower prolactin and restore ovulation in about 70% of women with microadenomas and 30% with macroadenomas (Katzung Ch 37).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolactinoma is the most common functioning PitNET. Key triad: amenorrhea + galactorrhea + infertility in women; decreased libido + erectile dysfunction in men. Lab: high prolactin, low FSH/LH (prolactin suppresses GnRH). Treatment: dopamine agonists FIRST (even for macroadenomas), NOT surgery first. Cabergoline > bromocriptine. COMLEX: Galactorrhea in a non-pregnant woman is a CP3 clinical presentation; students should recognize dopaminergic inhibition of prolactin as the key mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — GH excess before epiphyseal fusion causes gigantism; after fusion it causes acromegaly, distinguishing pediatric from adult-onset somatotroph PitNET","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman presents with progressive jaw enlargement, increasing ring size, and difficulty fitting her shoes. Serum IGF-1 is markedly elevated. Growth hormone fails to suppress below 1 ng/mL after a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test. Which mechanism best explains the failure of GH to suppress on OGTT in this patient?","choices":{"D":"Chronic GH excess causes progressive downregulation of IGF-1 receptors on somatotroph cells, reducing the normal IGF-1-mediated feedback loop; after OGTT, insufficient receptor-mediated signaling prevents the expected rise in negative feedback, allowing GH to remain elevated","A":"Glucose administration stimulates GLP-1 and GIP release from intestinal L- and K-cells; these gut hormones bind receptors on somatotroph cells and directly stimulate GH secretion, causing a paradoxical GH rise after OGTT rather than the expected suppression seen in healthy individuals","B":"The somatotroph PitNET secretes GH autonomously, independent of hypothalamic GHRH regulation; the normal mechanism — rising blood glucose stimulating somatostatin release and IGF-1 feedback to suppress GH — cannot override constitutive secretion from the tumor cells","C":"Oral glucose stimulates pancreatic glucagon release from alpha cells, which binds Gsα-coupled receptors on somatotroph cells and maintains GH secretion via cAMP; the glucagon-GH axis overrides the somatostatin-mediated suppression that would normally lower GH after glucose ingestion","E":"Glucose intolerance caused by GH insulin-antagonism prevents adequate blood glucose elevation after OGTT; without a sufficient hyperglycemic stimulus, the hypothalamic somatostatin-release signal never triggers, explaining the failure of normal GH suppression"},"explanation":"In normal physiology, hyperglycemia after OGTT → hypothalamic somatostatin release and IGF-1 feedback → GH suppression below 1 ng/mL within 2 hours. In acromegaly, the somatotroph PitNET secretes GH constitutively; neither somatostatin nor IGF-1 feedback effectively suppresses tumor-derived GH. The OGTT is the gold-standard diagnostic confirmatory test for acromegaly: failure to suppress below 1 ng/mL confirms autonomous secretion. Note: GH may paradoxically rise after glucose in acromegaly (paradoxical GH response) — this is a classic board trap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acromegaly diagnosis = elevated IGF-1 (screening) + failure of GH suppression on OGTT (confirmation). Measure GH after glucose load, NOT random GH (which fluctuates). Treatment priority: transsphenoidal surgery; if incomplete, add somatostatin analogs (octreotide/lanreotide) or GH receptor antagonist (pegvisomant). COMLEX: Acromegaly physical exam: prognathism, macroglossa, widened tooth spacing, carpal tunnel, coarse skin, increased shoe/ring/hat size — all named CP3 findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — GH excess before epiphyseal fusion causes gigantism; after fusion it causes acromegaly, distinguishing pediatric from adult-onset somatotroph PitNET","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"Nelson syndrome develops in some patients with Cushing disease who undergo bilateral adrenalectomy as treatment. Which sequence of pathophysiologic events best explains the development of Nelson syndrome after this procedure?","choices":{"E":"Bilateral adrenalectomy eliminates cortisol production; loss of cortisol-mediated negative feedback removes restraint from the pre-existing corticotroph PitNET, which expands aggressively and hypersecrete ACTH along with other POMC-derived peptides including alpha-MSH; the excess alpha-MSH binds MC1R melanocortin receptors on cutaneous melanocytes, causing the characteristic diffuse hyperpigmentation of Nelson syndrome","A":"Bilateral adrenalectomy triggers compensatory aldosterone hypersecretion from residual adrenal cortex cells, stimulating a secondary ACTH-independent gonadotroph PitNET that then acquires POMC gene expression via an epigenetic switch, producing ACTH and alpha-MSH in excess","B":"After adrenalectomy, rising ACTH levels stimulate adrenal regeneration from capsular reserve cells; failure of regeneration leads to compensatory hypothalamic CRH hypersecretion that drives de novo formation of a second corticotroph adenoma, separate from the original Cushing disease tumor","C":"Bilateral adrenalectomy causes acute electrolyte imbalance and hypocortisolism that directly damages pituitary vasculature via oxidative stress, leading to a reparative angioproliferative reaction that compresses the pre-existing corticotroph adenoma and paradoxically increases ACTH secretion","D":"Removal of the adrenal glands increases CRH pulsatility from the hypothalamus, which selectively upregulates CRH receptors on null-cell PitNETs; these previously nonfunctioning cells then adopt a corticotroph phenotype by activating TBX19 transcription, producing the hyperpigmentation and mass effects"},"explanation":"Nelson syndrome (post-adrenalectomy tumor expansion and ACTH hypersecretion) can follow bilateral adrenalectomy in Cushing disease patients who had an unresected or residual corticotroph PitNET. The mechanism is simple: cortisol normally provides negative feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary; remove the adrenal glands → zero cortisol → unopposed CRH drives the pre-existing corticotroph PitNET to grow rapidly and secrete massive amounts of ACTH + POMC-derived peptides (α-MSH, β-MSH, β-lipotropin). α-MSH activates melanocortin MC1R receptors → hyperpigmentation. The tumor can also cause chiasmal compression and headache.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nelson syndrome = bilateral adrenalectomy → no cortisol feedback → corticotroph PitNET expands → high ACTH + MSH → hyperpigmentation + mass effects. Compare to Addison disease: primary adrenal insufficiency also causes hyperpigmentation via ACTH/MSH from a normal pituitary responding to cortisol deficiency. COMLEX: Nelson syndrome is rarely tested on COMLEX but Cushing syndrome sequelae and adrenalectomy complications are fair game for management questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — GH excess before epiphyseal fusion causes gigantism; after fusion it causes acromegaly, distinguishing pediatric from adult-onset somatotroph PitNET","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"Sheehan syndrome results in ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary following severe peripartum hemorrhage, but the posterior pituitary is typically spared. Which anatomic difference in blood supply best explains this selective vulnerability of the anterior lobe?","choices":{"D":"The anterior pituitary receives blood exclusively through the low-pressure hypothalamic-hypophysial portal venous system; during severe postpartum hemorrhage, systemic hypotension and vasospasm preferentially compromise this portal circulation, causing anterior lobe ischemia; the posterior pituitary receives a direct arterial blood supply from the inferior hypophysial arteries and is therefore protected from portal system disruption","A":"The anterior pituitary is embryologically derived from oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch) and its cells require higher oxygen delivery than neural ectoderm-derived posterior pituitary cells; during peripartum hemorrhage, the higher metabolic rate of oral ectoderm-derived cells makes them selectively vulnerable to oxygen deficit","B":"The posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin in preformed peptide reserves within Herring bodies, enabling its cells to maintain viability during brief ischemic intervals; the anterior pituitary lacks equivalent preformed hormone storage and cannot sustain metabolic activity during even brief interruptions in blood flow","C":"The anterior pituitary undergoes twofold enlargement during pregnancy from estrogen-stimulated lactotroph hyperplasia; this increased mass physically compresses the portal vessels from within the rigid bony sella, creating a chronic ischemic state that culminates in frank infarction during any acute hypotensive episode","E":"The posterior pituitary undergoes compensatory hypertrophy during pregnancy to store oxytocin for parturition, which paradoxically increases its collateral arterial supply density; this pregnancy-induced vascular expansion provides protection against global ischemia while the less-vascularized anterior lobe remains vulnerable"},"explanation":"Sheehan syndrome mechanism: during pregnancy, the anterior pituitary doubles in size due to lactotroph hyperplasia (estrogen effect) without a proportional increase in portal blood supply. During peripartum hemorrhage → systemic hypotension → vascular spasm in the portal capillary system → anterior pituitary ischemia and necrosis. The key anatomic fact is the blood supply difference: anterior pituitary = portal venous only (low-pressure, vulnerable to spasm); posterior pituitary = direct inferior hypophysial arteries (arterial, maintained during portal disruption). The first clinical symptom is failure of lactation (prolactin deficiency); GH deficiency follows, then gonadotropin → TSH → ACTH deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sheehan syndrome = most common cause of hypopituitarism in the developing world. Classic first sign = failure to lactate postpartum. Lab pattern: low prolactin, low TSH + low T4, low FSH/LH, low ACTH + low cortisol. Posterior pituitary spared → no DI in classic Sheehan. Pituitary apoplexy is a separate diagnosis: hemorrhagic infarction of an existing adenoma → sudden onset. COMLEX: Sheehan syndrome is a CP3 named pituitary disorder; students must distinguish it from autoimmune hypophysitis (which has anti-pituitary antibodies) and pituitary apoplexy (which has hemorrhage on MRI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormones are synthesized in hypothalamic nuclei and stored in Herring bodies; Sheehan syndrome destroys the anterior lobe while sparing this arterially supplied posterior lobe","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"In patients with progressive hypopituitarism from a slowly enlarging pituitary adenoma, which anterior pituitary hormone axis is characteristically deficient first, and which is typically the last to become deficient as the adenoma continues to enlarge?","choices":{"A":"Growth hormone deficiency manifests earliest, reflecting the high sensitivity of GH secretion to even modest pituitary compression; gonadotropin deficiency typically follows; TSH and ACTH deficiency appear last because thyroid and adrenal function can be maintained with relatively small amounts of residual trophic stimulation","B":"TSH deficiency develops earliest because the thyroid gland has minimal intrinsic hormone reserve and depends entirely on continuous pituitary stimulation; GH deficiency follows; ACTH deficiency is last and requires almost complete anterior lobe destruction because the adrenal cortex maintains autonomous basal cortisol secretion","C":"ACTH deficiency develops earliest because cortisol is the critical hormone for basal cellular survival; TSH deficiency follows; gonadotropin deficiency appears last because FSH and LH have the lowest daily secretion rates and can be maintained with the smallest number of intact gonadotroph cells","D":"Prolactin deficiency manifests first because lactotrophs are the largest cell population and have the highest estrogen sensitivity, making them the most rapidly apoptotic during pituitary compression; FSH/LH deficiency appears last as gonadotrophs are most metabolically resistant to ischemia","E":"Gonadotropin deficiency develops first because FSH/LH-secreting cells have the highest metabolic rate and greatest oxygen demand; ACTH deficiency appears last because cortisol negative feedback sustains CRH-stimulated ACTH secretion from even a small residual population of corticotroph cells"},"explanation":"The sequence of hormonal loss in progressive hypopituitarism follows a predictable pattern: GH → FSH/LH → TSH → ACTH. Mnemonic: \"Go Find The Adrenal\" or GH, Gonadotropins, TSH, ACTH. GH is lost first because it requires the most intact somatotroph mass for adequate secretion and lacks a sensitive target organ to indicate deficiency early (unlike TSH/ACTH). Gonadotropins next (menstrual irregularity in women is often the presenting complaint). TSH and ACTH deficiencies are last because thyroid and adrenal function can be maintained with minimal trophic drive. Notably, prolactin rises (not falls) with progressive pituitary stalk compression because dopamine inhibition is lost before prolactin-secreting cells are destroyed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH → FSH/LH → TSH → ACTH sequence is a classic board question. Remember: in practice, GH deficiency often goes undiagnosed because it causes subtle symptoms in adults (fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, increased body fat). Gonadotropin deficiency is usually the presenting complaint in younger patients. COMLEX: Pituitary compression by adenoma is a cause of secondary hypothyroidism (low TSH, low T4); this is distinct from primary hypothyroidism (high TSH, low T4). Distinguishing primary from secondary endocrine insufficiency is high yield for COMLEX.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormones are synthesized in hypothalamic nuclei and stored in Herring bodies; Sheehan syndrome destroys the anterior lobe while sparing this arterially supplied posterior lobe","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"TSH and ACTH deficiencies are ","C":"TSH and ACTH deficiencies are ","D":"Notably, prolactin rises (not "}},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary empty sella syndrome is an incidental finding on MRI in which CSF fills the sella turcica and the pituitary appears thinned or absent. Which anatomic defect initiates this process, and what typically happens to pituitary function?","choices":{"B":"An incompetent or absent diaphragma sellae — the dural fold that normally covers the sella — allows CSF under normal intracranial pressure to herniate into the sella turcica through the diaphragmatic aperture, progressively compressing the pituitary against the sella floor; despite the dramatic MRI appearance, pituitary function is typically preserved because sufficient parenchyma remains attached to the stalk and intact blood supply","A":"A congenital absence of the pituitary stalk prevents adequate portal blood flow to the anterior lobe; the resulting chronic ischemia causes progressive cell loss and gland atrophy, eventually leaving a vacuum space within the rigid bony sella that draws in CSF from surrounding cisternal spaces through small dural defects","C":"Lactotroph hyperplasia during pregnancy ruptures the thin fibrous pituitary capsule; after delivery, the involuted gland shrinks away from the sella walls, creating a low-pressure space that draws in CSF through tears in the diaphragma sellae that opened under the pressure of the enlarged gland during gestation","D":"A Rathke cleft cyst expands within the sella over decades and gradually compresses normal pituitary cells into a thin peripheral rim; the cyst eventually communicates with the subarachnoid space through the diaphragmatic aperture and fills with CSF, producing the empty sella appearance on MRI","E":"Autoimmune lymphocytic hypophysitis destroys anterior pituitary cells and leaves a fibrotic contracted scar; the fibrous contraction pulls arachnoid tissue into the sella through the diaphragmatic aperture, and CSF follows the arachnoid herniation as a secondary event after the inflammatory destruction is complete"},"explanation":"Primary empty sella results from a deficient diaphragma sellae (the dural roof of the sella), allowing the arachnoid membrane and CSF to herniate in through the opening where the stalk passes. The pituitary is compressed flat against the floor but remains functional because it retains its blood supply and stalk attachment. Secondary empty sella follows destruction of pituitary tissue (surgery, radiation, infarction, Sheehan). Clinically: primary empty sella is most common in obese, hypertensive, multiparous women; pituitary function is usually normal; headache is the most frequent complaint (unrelated to pituitary); pseudotumor cerebri (idiopathic intracranial hypertension) can be an associated condition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Empty sella = herniation of CSF through deficient diaphragma sellae; pituitary function usually preserved; associated with pseudotumor cerebri. Distinguish from secondary empty sella (pituitary destruction). COMLEX: Pseudotumor cerebri is associated with obesity, OCP use, tetracycline, and vitamin A excess — all causes of elevated intracranial pressure that can force CSF into the sella. OMM relevance: craniosacral therapy and intracranial pressure dynamics are a COMLEX-specific topic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormones are synthesized in hypothalamic nuclei and stored in Herring bodies; Sheehan syndrome destroys the anterior lobe while sparing this arterially supplied posterior lobe","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":1,"stem":"In the syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, free water is retained and total body water rises, yet patients are clinically euvolemic and peripheral edema does not usually develop. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":{"E":"Water retention expands volume, which lowers aldosterone and raises ANP and BNP, so urinary sodium loss returns extracellular volume toward normal","A":"ADH constricts lymphatic vessels through V1 receptors, which keeps retained fluid out of the interstitium and prevents edema despite more body water","B":"ADH excess raises renin and aldosterone, so retained sodium and water stay within the vascular space while plasma oncotic pressure prevents edema","C":"Retained free water is lost through the lungs and skin at a higher rate, which keeps total extracellular volume constant without renal sodium loss","D":"ADH raises capillary protein permeability, which moves albumin into the interstitium and pulls the retained water back into the plasma space"},"explanation":"First Aid 2025 (p 342) explains that the body responds to the water retention of SIADH with decreased aldosterone and increased ANP and BNP, which increase urinary sodium excretion and normalize extracellular fluid volume, giving euvolemic hyponatremia with continued urinary sodium excretion and urine osmolality greater than serum osmolality. Robbins notes that although total body water is increased in SIADH, blood volume remains normal and peripheral edema does not generally develop; the clinical picture is dominated by hyponatremia, cerebral edema, and neurologic dysfunction. ADH does not constrict lymphatics to prevent edema. Renin and aldosterone fall, not rise, as volume expands. Insensible losses do not rise to offset the retained water. ADH does not increase capillary protein leak, and protein leak would promote edema rather than prevent it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH = euvolemic hyponatremia, urine osmolality > serum osmolality, continued urinary Na+ excretion; causes include CNS disorders, ectopic ADH from small cell lung cancer, and drugs such as SSRIs, carbamazepine, and cyclophosphamide (First Aid 2025 p 342). COMLEX: Fluid restriction is first-line, followed by salt tablets, hypertonic saline, or ADH antagonists such as tolvaptan, conivaptan, or demeclocycline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — ADH secretion is regulated by plasma osmolality and blood volume","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Renin and aldosterone fall, no","C":"Insensible losses do not rise ","D":"ADH does not increase capillar"}},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":1,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman with advanced dementia develops central diabetes insipidus after a hypothalamic stroke. She is bedbound and cannot ask for water, and over 3 days her serum sodium rises to 158 mEq/L. Her son asks why a neighbor who has had central DI for years keeps a normal serum sodium. Which best explains the difference?","choices":{"C":"Intact thirst and access to water let most patients replace urinary losses, while patients who cannot drink lose free water and become hypernatremic","A":"Hypernatremia develops when DI follows a stroke, because vascular injury also destroys the osmoreceptors, while tumors and trauma spare them","B":"Older adults lose collecting duct V2 receptors with age, so central DI in the elderly behaves like nephrogenic DI and resists any water replacement","D":"Bedbound patients secrete less aldosterone, so sodium is retained in the blood, while ambulatory patients excrete the excess sodium in their urine","E":"Dementia speeds breakdown of residual ADH by liver insulinase, so circulating ADH falls further than in other patients with the same hypothalamic lesion"},"explanation":"Robbins explains that in diabetes insipidus the excessive renal loss of free water can raise serum sodium and osmolality, which causes thirst and polydipsia. Patients who can drink water generally compensate for the urinary losses, but patients who are obtunded, bedridden, or otherwise limited in their ability to obtain water may develop life-threatening dehydration. The key difference is access to water and the ability to respond to thirst, not the cause of the lesion; the source lists head trauma, tumors, inflammatory disorders, and surgery as causes without linking hypernatremia to any one of them. Age-related loss of V2 receptors is not described, and replacing water corrects the problem. Less aldosterone would cause sodium loss rather than retention. Insulinase is the enzyme that degrades insulin, not ADH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central DI (AVP deficiency) and nephrogenic DI (AVP resistance) both produce large volumes of dilute urine; desmopressin corrects central DI (First Aid 2025 p 342). Causes of central DI: head trauma, tumors, inflammatory disorders such as sarcoidosis, and surgery (Robbins). COMLEX: After pituitary or hypothalamic injury in a patient who cannot drink, follow serum sodium and provide free water.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — diabetes insipidus represents ADH deficiency (central) or ADH resistance (nephrogenic)","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The key difference is access t","B":"Age-related loss of V2 recepto","D":"Less aldosterone would cause s","E":"Insulinase is the enzyme that "}},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":1,"stem":"X-linked nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is caused by a loss-of-function mutation in a specific gene. Which gene is mutated, and what is the precise downstream mechanism by which this mutation renders the collecting duct tubule impermeable to water despite markedly elevated circulating ADH?","choices":{"A":"X-linked nephrogenic DI is caused by mutations in AVPR2, encoding the V2 receptor on collecting duct principal cells; the nonfunctional receptor cannot couple to Gsα upon ADH binding, preventing cAMP production and PKA phosphorylation of aquaporin-2, so AQP2 channels remain in cytoplasmic vesicles despite high circulating ADH","B":"X-linked nephrogenic DI is caused by mutations in AVP, encoding the ADH prepropeptide itself; the mutant ADH cannot be cleaved from neurophysin by endopeptidases in the posterior pituitary, but the V2 receptor and downstream AQP2 insertion machinery remain completely intact and functional","C":"X-linked nephrogenic DI is caused by mutations in AQP2, encoding aquaporin-2 water channels on collecting duct principal cells; although AQP2 mutations cause autosomal recessive nephrogenic DI, the X-linked form requires regulatory mutations on the X chromosome that control AQP2 expression from a distance","D":"X-linked nephrogenic DI is caused by mutations in AQP4, encoding the aquaporin expressed in astrocytes and the basolateral membrane of collecting duct cells; loss of AQP4 prevents osmotic water movement from the medullary interstitium across the basolateral membrane into the tubular cell even when AQP2 is correctly inserted at the apical surface","E":"X-linked nephrogenic DI is caused by mutations in GNAS, encoding Gsα; without functional Gsα, V2 receptor activation cannot stimulate adenylyl cyclase, preventing cAMP production and AQP2 insertion; the same GNAS mutations at different codons are responsible for both nephrogenic DI and the café-au-lait spots of McCune-Albright syndrome"},"explanation":"Nephrogenic DI genetics: X-linked = AVPR2 mutations (V2 receptor); autosomal forms = AQP2 mutations. The AVPR2 mechanism: ADH cannot signal → Gsα not activated → adenylyl cyclase dormant → no cAMP → PKA inactive → AQP2 vesicles not phosphorylated → AQP2 stays in cytoplasm → tubule impermeable to water → dilute polyuria with high plasma ADH. The GNAS option is a classic trap: GNAS loss-of-function causes Albright hereditary osteodystrophy / Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a; GNAS gain-of-function causes McCune-Albright. Neither causes nephrogenic DI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: X-linked nephrogenic DI = AVPR2 mutation; autosomal = AQP2 mutation. Acquired nephrogenic DI causes: lithium (most common drug), demeclocycline, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, amyloidosis. Plasma ADH is high (body compensates → fails). Treatment: thiazide diuretics + low-sodium diet (paradoxically reduces urine volume by volume-depleting the patient → proximal tubule reabsorption of water increases). COMLEX: Understanding the V2/AQP2 signaling cascade is directly tested via questions about tolvaptan mechanism in SIADH treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — Diabetes insipidus represents ADH deficiency (central) or ADH resistance (nephrogenic), producing large volumes of dilute urine regardless of etiology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman presents 8 weeks after a complicated term delivery requiring transfusion of 6 units of packed red blood cells for hemorrhagic shock. She reports failure to produce breast milk, profound fatigue, cold intolerance, and loss of pubic and axillary hair. Labs: TSH 0.4 mU/L (low), free T4 0.5 ng/dL (low), FSH 1.8 IU/L (low), LH 1.6 IU/L (low), cortisol 5 mcg/dL (low), prolactin <2 ng/mL (undetectable), ADH axis intact. MRI shows a partially empty sella. Which interpretation of this hormonal pattern is most accurate?","choices":{"C":"Low target-gland hormones (T4, cortisol) with inappropriately low trophic hormones (TSH, FSH, LH) and undetectable prolactin indicate panhypopituitarism from Sheehan syndrome; the hemorrhage history, empty sella, and posterior pituitary sparing (intact ADH axis) point to ischemic anterior lobe necrosis","A":"The low TSH combined with low T4 most likely represents primary thyroid failure after autoimmune thyroiditis triggered by postpartum immune reconstitution; the low gonadotropins reflect nutritional suppression of the HPG axis from anemia and stress, and are not part of the primary pathologic process","B":"The pattern of low TSH and low T4 indicates an ectopic TSH-secreting tumor that is producing biologically inactive TSH; the low gonadotropins reflect compression of gonadotrophs by this functioning tumor, and the empty sella represents tumor-induced ischemic remodeling of the sella","D":"The hormonal pattern indicates primary hypothalamic failure rather than pituitary failure; the two conditions cannot be reliably distinguished on hormone levels alone, and TRH stimulation testing with simultaneous CRH, GnRH, and GHRH administration is required before initiating any hormone replacement","E":"The low cortisol with low ACTH indicates exogenous steroid use causing HPA axis suppression; the low gonadotropins represent stress-related GnRH suppression, and the undetectable prolactin suggests previous dopamine agonist use; the empty sella is an incidental finding unrelated to the peripartum hemorrhage"},"explanation":"Sheehan syndrome presents with the classic combination of: (1) massive postpartum hemorrhage history; (2) failure of lactation (first and most specific symptom — prolactin deficiency); (3) secondary hypothyroidism (low TSH + low T4); (4) secondary hypogonadism (low FSH/LH); (5) secondary adrenal insufficiency (low ACTH + low cortisol); (6) posterior pituitary spared (arterial supply preserved → no DI). The key distinguishing laboratory feature is low trophic hormones (TSH, LH, FSH) with low target hormones — this confirms anterior pituitary origin rather than primary target organ failure. Empty sella on MRI shows the infarcted anterior lobe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sheehan syndrome = failure to lactate + panhypopituitarism + history of postpartum hemorrhage. Failure of lactation is the earliest and most specific symptom. Management: corticosteroids first (life-threatening), then thyroid hormone, then sex hormones, then GH (least urgent). COMLEX: Sheehan syndrome is contrasted with lymphocytic hypophysitis in clinical presentation questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy presents with progressive headaches, bitemporal visual field loss, short stature, and diabetes insipidus. Head CT shows a calcified suprasellar cystic mass. MRI demonstrates a biloculated cyst with a solid component extending from the sella to the third ventricle. Surgical biopsy shows columnar epithelial cells with peripheral palisading around lobules, central pale \"ghost cells\" with lamellar keratin, and dystrophic calcification. Molecular testing reveals a CTNNB1 activating mutation with nuclear accumulation of beta-catenin. What is the diagnosis and its histologic classification?","choices":{"D":"Pituitary neuroendocrine tumor of null-cell type — null-cell PitNETs can present with DI and bitemporal hemianopia from suprasellar extension; CTNNB1 mutations have been reported in rare aggressive PitNETs, and dystrophic calcification is seen in macroadenomas that have undergone necrosis","A":"Papillary craniopharyngioma — the finding of ghost cells and dystrophic calcification in a child or adolescent specifically indicates the papillary variant, which carries CTNNB1 mutations and responds to BRAF inhibitor therapy; the adult-onset papillary variant instead carries BRAF V600E mutations and lacks ghost cell keratinization","B":"Rathke cleft cyst — this developmental lesion arises from the same anatomic location and produces a similar cystic sellar mass on imaging; histologically it is indistinguishable from adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma and the distinction is made only by CTNNB1 mutational analysis, which is positive in both entities","C":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma — squamoid basaloid epithelium with peripheral palisading, wet keratin ghost cells, dystrophic calcification, CTNNB1/beta-catenin mutation, and pediatric onset define this subtype, which arises from Rathke pouch remnants along the stalk","E":"Meningioma of the tuberculum sellae — this suprasellar meningioma can produce bitemporal hemianopia and calcification on CT; the squamoid histology represents metaplastic changes within the meningioma, and CTNNB1 mutations occur in a subset of intracranial meningiomas"},"explanation":"Craniopharyngioma is the most common suprasellar tumor in children (1–5% of intracranial tumors total). Adamantinomatous variant: peak age 5–15 years; CTNNB1 activating mutation → nuclear beta-catenin accumulation in whorled clusters; ghost cells (\"wet keratin\") = cells that retain their nuclear outlines after keratinizing; dystrophic calcification visible on CT (pathognomonic for craniopharyngioma in children); \"machine oil\" fluid from cholesterol-rich dark brown cyst contents. This variant is locally aggressive and recurs commonly, making gross total resection the goal. Papillary variant: adults, BRAF V600E, responds to BRAF inhibitors, no calcification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Suprasellar calcified cystic tumor in a child = craniopharyngioma.\" Classic presentation: bitemporal hemianopia (chiasm compression from below) + DI (infundibular stalk involvement) + growth failure (GH deficiency). Treat: surgery + radiation. COMLEX: Craniopharyngioma is in the CP3 differential for visual field defects in children; students are expected to recognize the CT calcification and suprasellar location.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss and explain the normal physiology and control of the pituitary gland with reference to alteration in function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with a known 2.8 cm pituitary macroadenoma presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset severe headache, acute loss of vision in the right eye, and right-sided ophthalmoplegia. She is afebrile and has no meningeal signs. MRI reveals hemorrhage within the sellar mass with suprasellar extension. Her cortisol level returns as critically low at 2 mcg/dL. What is the diagnosis and what is the single most immediately life-saving intervention?","choices":{"A":"Pituitary apoplexy — hemorrhagic infarction of a pituitary adenoma presenting as a neurosurgical emergency; the most immediately life-saving step is IV high-dose hydrocortisone (100 mg every 8 hours) to treat the adrenal crisis caused by sudden ACTH loss, followed by urgent neurosurgical evaluation for decompression of the sella","B":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured berry aneurysm — this is managed with neurosurgical clipping or endovascular coiling of the aneurysm; glucocorticoids are contraindicated because they worsen neurologic outcomes by increasing glucose and masking fever; lumbar puncture for xanthochromia is the confirmatory diagnostic step if CT does not show blood","C":"Bacterial meningitis with septic emboli to the pituitary — this requires immediate broad-spectrum antibiotics and dexamethasone; lumbar puncture before antibiotics is mandatory to identify the organism, and neurosurgical consultation would be needed only if an abscess develops or does not respond to antibiotics within 48 hours","D":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis complicating the pituitary adenoma — this requires systemic anticoagulation with intravenous heparin to prevent clot propagation and cranial nerve ischemia; glucocorticoids are used only as adjuncts for reducing orbital inflammation, and neurosurgical intervention is not indicated unless an abscess forms","E":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma precipitated by pupillary dilation from the pituitary mass compressing the ciliary ganglion — this ophthalmic emergency requires topical pilocarpine and intravenous acetazolamide to reduce intraocular pressure; the low cortisol is a coincidental finding from concurrent stress-induced HPA suppression"},"explanation":"Pituitary apoplexy = hemorrhage or infarction into a pituitary adenoma. Presents acutely: sudden severe headache (\"thunderclap\"), visual disturbance (chiasm → bitemporal hemianopia), ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI via cavernous sinus), and acute ACTH/cortisol deficiency → adrenal crisis. Adrenal crisis (hypotension, nausea, vomiting) is the immediate life-threatening complication. MRI: T1 hyperintense signal = hemorrhage within sellar mass. Management priority: IV hydrocortisone FIRST → then neurosurgery evaluation. Indications for urgent surgery: severe visual loss, declining consciousness. Many cases can be managed conservatively after steroid stabilization. Katzung Ch 39 gives hydrocortisone 100 mg IV every 8 hours until the patient is stable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pituitary apoplexy = sudden severe headache + visual field loss + ophthalmoplegia + low cortisol in a patient with known or incidental pituitary macroadenoma. Distinguish from SAH (CT angiogram shows aneurysm) and meningitis (fever, meningismus, CSF pleocytosis). Give hydrocortisone FIRST — acute ACTH deficiency can be fatal within hours. COMLEX: Pituitary apoplexy is in the CP3 differential for sudden headache with visual changes; recognize the clinical triad and the first treatment step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary cell types and primary target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":2,"stem":"A sellar mass was surgically removed from a 58-year-old man presenting with progressive loss of peripheral vision over 6 months, erectile dysfunction, fatigue, and a serum testosterone of 85 ng/dL (low). Grossly, the large tan-white sellar and suprasellar mass compressed the optic chiasm from below. Immunohistochemistry for GH, PRL, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, SF-1, POU1F1, and TBX19 is negative. Serum levels of all anterior pituitary hormones are low-normal or low. Which diagnosis and clinical syndrome best characterize this lesion?","choices":{"B":"Null cell pituitary neuroendocrine tumor — a clinically nonfunctioning macroadenoma that presents with mass effect (bitemporal hemianopia from chiasmal compression, hypopituitarism from gland compression, headache) and hormone deficiency rather than hypersecretion; null cell PitNETs lack hormonal IHC markers because the tumor cells are poorly differentiated","A":"Gonadotroph PitNET secreting supraphysiologic FSH and LH — the negative hormonal IHC reflects a technical error, as gonadotroph tumors uniformly express FSH, LH, and the shared glycoprotein alpha-subunit; the low testosterone is caused by end-organ desensitization from chronically elevated gonadotropins mimicking polycystic ovary syndrome physiology in the male","C":"Thyrotroph PitNET causing central hyperthyroidism — the negative IHC reflects TSH antigen denaturation during formalin fixation, and a repeat IHC panel targeting the TSH beta-subunit would be positive; serum TSH would be inappropriately normal or elevated despite low T4, and the correct workup requires a TRH stimulation test to confirm thyrotroph autonomy","D":"Metastatic carcinoma to the pituitary from an occult visceral primary — metastatic lesions are uniformly IHC-negative for pituitary hormones and transcription factors and should be the favored diagnosis over a primary PitNET whenever all pituitary IHC markers are negative; CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis is the next diagnostic step","E":"Craniopharyngioma of the papillary variant presenting in adulthood — papillary craniopharyngiomas are IHC-negative for pituitary hormones and arise in adults; they produce the same mass-effect symptoms as null-cell PitNET and are distinguished only by their predominantly suprasellar location, squamous histology on frozen section, and BRAF V600E mutation"},"explanation":"Null cell PitNETs express no hormonal or lineage markers and, like most gonadotroph PitNETs, are hormonally silent; symptoms arise entirely from mass effect: (1) bitemporal hemianopia from chiasm compression (optic chiasm sits directly above the sella); (2) hypopituitarism from compression of remaining normal pituitary; (3) headache from dural stretching; (4) cavernous sinus invasion causing cranial neuropathy (CN III, IV, VI). Unlike gonadotroph, somatotroph, or lactotroph PitNETs, null cells do not express recognizable hormonal markers by IHC. Treatment: transsphenoidal surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most common pituitary macroadenoma causing bitemporal hemianopia = nonfunctioning (null cell) PitNET or gonadotroph PitNET (both are \"clinically silent\"). The visual field defect starts superiorly (inferior nasal retina fibers cross first at chiasm) and progresses to complete bitemporal hemianopia. COMLEX: Pituitary macroadenoma compressing the chiasm is a classic visual field defect question; students must understand the anatomic explanation (nerve fibers from the nasal retina cross at the chiasm and are compressed first).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger pathway and its role in pituitary tumor pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy was obtained from a suprasellar mass in a 13-year-old girl presenting with growth arrest, bitemporal visual field loss, and polyuria. CT of the head showed a calcified cystic suprasellar lesion. The biopsy demonstrates lobules of basaloid squamoid epithelium with peripheral nuclear palisading, central ghost cells, lamellar keratin, and focal dystrophic calcification. Molecular analysis reveals nuclear beta-catenin accumulation. What molecular alteration drives this histologic pattern and what is its treatment implication?","choices":{"D":"Activating mutations in CTNNB1 encode a mutant beta-catenin that escapes APC-mediated proteasomal degradation and accumulates in the nucleus, driving WNT-pathway transcription that promotes squamous differentiation and wet keratin (ghost cell) formation; treatment relies on surgery and radiation because no approved targeted therapy exists","A":"BRAF V600E mutations cause constitutive MEK/ERK pathway activation in this adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma, promoting the squamoid and keratinizing differentiation seen; BRAF V600E is clinically actionable with dabrafenib/trametinib, and these targeted agents are approved as first-line therapy for recurrent pediatric craniopharyngioma","B":"USP8 deubiquitinase activating mutations prevent EGFR degradation in the adamantinomatous variant, maintaining constitutive EGFR signaling that drives squamous differentiation; EGFR-targeted therapy with erlotinib shows response rates exceeding 70% in pediatric adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma and is now preferred over radiation","C":"TP53 loss-of-function mutations cause genomic instability in this craniopharyngioma, with the resulting high tumor mutational burden producing the ghost cell keratinization seen; the elevated mutational burden is predictive of response to pembrolizumab, which is FDA-approved for TMB-high solid tumors including pediatric craniopharyngioma","E":"Activating GNAS mutations driving constitutive Gsα/cAMP signaling cause squamous metaplasia of the Rathke pouch epithelium in this tumor, explaining the keratinization and ghost cells; this is the same mutation found in somatotroph PitNETs, and somatostatin analogs therefore have demonstrated efficacy as adjuvant therapy after surgical resection"},"explanation":"Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma CTNNB1 mechanism: APC normally phosphorylates beta-catenin → ubiquitination → proteasomal degradation. CTNNB1 activating mutations render beta-catenin resistant to phosphorylation → nuclear accumulation → WNT target gene transcription → squamous differentiation and ghost cell keratinization. Nuclear beta-catenin IHC is the diagnostic marker. No CTNNB1 inhibitors are clinically approved; treatment = maximal safe surgical resection + radiation therapy. Contrast: Papillary craniopharyngioma has BRAF V600E → MEK/ERK activation → responds to BRAF inhibitors (vemurafenib, dabrafenib). Knowing which variant has which mutation predicts targeted therapy eligibility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adamantinomatous = CTNNB1/beta-catenin; papillary = BRAF V600E (and responds to BRAF inhibitors). The histology is classic First Aid: \"wet keratin, dystrophic calcification, machine oil fluid.\" COMLEX: Questions focus on clinical recognition; know that the calcified suprasellar cystic tumor in a child should prompt craniopharyngioma as the diagnosis and transsphenoidal/transcranial resection as the treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger pathway and contrast with CTNNB1/beta-catenin WNT pathway activation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply knowledge of the clinico-pathologic features of pituitary and suprasellar tumors and their management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman is evaluated for secondary amenorrhea and galactorrhea. MRI reveals a 7 mm pituitary microadenoma. Serum prolactin is 285 ng/mL (markedly elevated). Cabergoline is initiated. After 2 years, prolactin normalizes, MRI shows tumor reduction, and she wishes to conceive. She is counseled about prolactinoma behavior during pregnancy. Which of the following statements most accurately describes prolactinoma management in women planning pregnancy?","choices":{"E":"Transsphenoidal surgery should be performed before attempting conception to eliminate the risk of tumor growth; medical therapy alone is inadequate for pre-pregnancy management because dopamine agonists fail to prevent estrogen-driven tumor expansion in more than 60% of microadenomas when estrogen levels rise to pregnancy levels","A":"Cabergoline must be continued throughout pregnancy without dose reduction because discontinuation uniformly causes prolactin to rebound above pretreatment levels within 2 weeks, stimulating rapid microadenoma regrowth that may progress to chiasmal compression in the second trimester; the safety of cabergoline in pregnancy is confirmed by large registry data showing no teratogenicity","B":"Pregnancy is contraindicated in all patients with prolactinoma regardless of microadenoma or macroadenoma classification; placental estrogen reliably causes symptomatic tumor expansion in all prolactinomas, and the risk of pituitary apoplexy during the Valsalva maneuver of labor is high enough to recommend cesarean delivery","C":"Cabergoline should be switched to bromocriptine before attempting conception because bromocriptine has a longer pregnancy safety record; once conception is confirmed, both medications should be continued at reduced doses throughout the first and second trimester to prevent prolactin rebound and maintain tumor suppression","D":"Cabergoline is typically discontinued once pregnancy is confirmed in women with microadenomas, because the physiologic hyperprolactinemia of pregnancy rarely causes symptomatic microadenoma expansion; visual field monitoring is warranted if headaches or visual symptoms develop, and macroadenomas need closer surveillance because estrogen can enlarge them"},"explanation":"Prolactinoma management in pregnancy: For microadenomas, the risk of tumor growth during pregnancy is low (2.7% in Katzung Ch 37); the dopamine agonist is stopped upon conception. For macroadenomas, the risk of tumor progression is high (22.9%), so these patients need vigilance, often continue a dopamine agonist, and are monitored with visual field testing. The estrogen surge of pregnancy stimulates physiologic lactotroph hyperplasia, which can enlarge the underlying tumor. Bromocriptine has a longer safety record in pregnancy but cabergoline is increasingly used; both can be restarted if symptomatic expansion occurs. Breastfeeding is generally safe after prolactinoma treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dopamine agonists = first-line for prolactinoma; STOP in pregnancy if microadenoma (low expansion risk); MONITOR macroadenomas. Bromocriptine has longer pregnancy data; cabergoline is preferred outside pregnancy for tolerability. COMLEX: Prolactinoma causing infertility is a named CP3 endocrine cause of amenorrhea/galactorrhea; students are expected to recommend dopamine agonist as first-line and recognize that surgical referral is reserved for medication failures or giant adenomas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger pathway and dopamine D2 receptor Gi-mediated inhibition of lactotroph cAMP","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A biopsy with H&E and growth hormone immunohistochemistry was taken from a sellar mass in a 42-year-old man presenting with progressive coarsening of facial features, enlargement of hands and feet over 5 years, a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, and hypertension. Serum IGF-1 is 3.4 times the upper limit of normal. GH fails to suppress on OGTT. The tumor shows strong diffuse cytoplasmic GH immunoreactivity (densely granulated morphology). After surgical resection achieves only partial debulking, which first-line adjuvant medical therapy most directly targets the pathophysiology of residual disease?","choices":{"C":"Somatostatin analogs (octreotide LAR, lanreotide autogel) — these agents bind SSTR2 and SSTR5 receptors on somatotroph PitNET cells, activating Gi protein signaling that reduces cAMP and suppresses GH secretion; they lower GH and IGF-1 and can reduce residual tumor volume, with densely granulated tumors responding better than sparsely granulated ones","A":"Dopamine agonists (cabergoline) — these represent the most effective adjuvant medical therapy for acromegaly because D2 receptor activation suppresses GH secretion from the tumor cells more potently than somatostatin analogs; clinical response rates exceeding 85% are achieved in densely granulated somatotroph PitNETs and the treatment is preferred over somatostatin analogs for this histologic subtype","B":"Pegvisomant monotherapy — this pegylated GH receptor antagonist is the most appropriate first-line adjuvant after incomplete surgery because it directly blocks GH signaling at peripheral tissues, reducing IGF-1 levels in >95% of patients regardless of tumor histologic subtype; it does not require intact tumor SSTR2 expression and is therefore superior to somatostatin analogs as first-line adjuvant therapy","D":"Levothyroxine supplementation — normalizing any coexisting secondary hypothyroidism from pituitary compression paradoxically reduces GH secretion by increasing hypothalamic somatostatin tone; this represents the most direct pharmacologic intervention for the residual acromegaly and should be initiated before considering tumor-targeted agents","E":"GnRH analogs (leuprolide) — these suppress gonadotropin and gonadal steroid secretion; reduced estrogen lowers hepatic IGF-1 synthesis by reducing IGF-1 gene transcription in hepatocytes; this represents a validated indirect treatment strategy that normalizes IGF-1 levels in the majority of patients without requiring direct pituitary tumor-targeted pharmacotherapy"},"explanation":"The IHC shows densely granulated somatotroph PitNET — strong diffuse cytoplasmic GH staining. Treatment algorithm: (1) Transsphenoidal surgery = first line; (2) If incomplete resection: somatostatin analogs (SSA) = first-line adjuvant for residual disease; (3) If SSA fail: GH receptor antagonist (pegvisomant) ± combination; (4) Radiation for refractory cases. Densely granulated tumors respond better to SSAs because they express more SSTR2. Sparsely granulated tumors (fibrous body by cytokeratin) respond less well to SSAs and may need pegvisomant. Cabergoline is a second-line adjunct: acromegaly needs higher dopamine agonist doses and seldom responds adequately to dopamine agonist alone unless the tumor also secretes prolactin (Katzung Ch 37).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acromegaly treatment: surgery first, then somatostatin analogs (octreotide), then pegvisomant if needed. Somatostatin analogs also shrink the tumor. Pegvisomant = IGF-1 falls, GH rises (blocks feedback). Don't confuse treatment of acromegaly (octreotide) with carcinoid syndrome (also octreotide) — mechanism is the same but different indication. COMLEX: Physical exam findings of acromegaly are explicitly listed in CP3; carpal tunnel syndrome from GH excess soft tissue changes is a named PE finding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Gigantism and acromegaly as the clinical disorders of GH excess","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy presents with accelerated linear height velocity (growing 14 cm in the past year, currently at the 99th height percentile for age), progressive enlargement of hands and feet, coarsening facial features including frontal bossing, and a recently documented bone age of 14 years. Serum GH is persistently elevated and fails to suppress on OGTT. IGF-1 is 4 times the upper limit of normal. An MRI reveals a sellar mass. If this condition is left untreated in this child, what ultimate phenotype would be expected, and how does this differ from the same tumor occurring in a 45-year-old adult?","choices":{"E":"In a child with open growth plates, chronic GH excess drives long bone lengthening, producing gigantism — proportionate linear overgrowth with heights that can exceed 7 feet; in an adult with fused plates, the same excess causes acromegaly — periosteal bone apposition with enlarged hands and feet, prognathism, and frontal bossing but no height gain","A":"In a prepubertal child, chronic GH excess causes the same acromegalic periosteal bone changes as in adults because GH acts predominantly on periosteal osteoblasts regardless of epiphyseal plate status; gigantism is an archaic clinical term referring to severely advanced bone age from IGF-1-driven epiphyseal acceleration, not a distinct endocrinopathy from acromegaly","B":"In a prepubertal child, chronic GH excess causes Marfan-like habitus because elevated IGF-1 stimulates fibrillin-1 synthesis and disrupts TGF-beta sequestration in connective tissue; this connective tissue overgrowth produces tall stature with arachnodactyly and joint laxity that is distinct from both gigantism and adult acromegaly","C":"In a prepubertal child, chronic GH excess causes premature epiphyseal closure because the markedly advanced bone age accelerates epiphyseal senescence; paradoxically, the child ultimately has shorter adult height than predicted, and acromegalic periosteal changes develop only after the growth plates fuse in mid-adolescence","D":"In a prepubertal child, chronic GH excess preferentially stimulates IGF-2 rather than IGF-1 because the liver expresses predominantly IGF-2 receptors before puberty; IGF-2-driven symmetrical organomegaly occurs without linear bone growth, and the acromegalic phenotype only emerges after puberty triggers a shift to IGF-1 predominance"},"explanation":"The key distinction is epiphyseal status: open (child) → GH drives chondrocyte proliferation at growth plates → linear bone elongation → gigantism; closed (adult) → GH drives periosteal osteoblast activity only → cortical thickening without lengthening → acromegaly. IGF-1 mediates both effects. In gigantism, insulin resistance, organomegaly, and the acromegalic features of soft tissue hypertrophy and facial changes co-occur. Untreated gigantism leads to cardiovascular morbidity, sleep apnea, diabetes, and markedly reduced lifespan. Historical cases of gigantism are attributable to somatotroph PitNETs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gigantism (epiphyses open) vs. acromegaly (epiphyses fused) = the classic test-worthy distinction. Both = elevated GH + IGF-1 + failure to suppress on OGTT. Both treated the same: surgery first, then octreotide, then pegvisomant. COMLEX: Gigantism causes cardiovascular complications (cardiomegaly, hypertension), diabetes from GH insulin-antagonism, and joint destruction — recognizing these in a pediatric patient is a clinical integration question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Gigantism and acromegaly: clinical disorders of GH excess before and after epiphyseal fusion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hyperpituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with Cushing disease undergoes transsphenoidal resection of a 6-mm pituitary microadenoma. On H&E the tumor cells are basophilic, they stain strongly with periodic acid-Schiff, and nuclear TPIT is positive. Which best explains the periodic acid-Schiff positivity of these tumor cells?","choices":{"B":"Carbohydrate in pro-opiomelanocortin, the glycosylated precursor of corticotropin stored in the secretory granules of the tumor cells","A":"Glycogen accumulation in the cytoplasm, driven by cortisol-induced gluconeogenesis and glycogen storage within the tumor cells themselves","C":"Mucin secreted by squamous nests derived from Rathke's pouch remnants that become trapped within the growing corticotroph tumor","D":"Lipid droplets accumulated from cholesterol uptake for steroid synthesis, like those in the adrenal cortical cells the tumor stimulates","E":"Ringlike cytokeratin filaments, the Crooke change that develops in corticotroph tumor cells exposed to persistently high cortisol"},"explanation":"Robbins describes corticotroph PitNETs as usually small at diagnosis and most often basophilic (densely granulated), occasionally chromophobic (sparsely granulated); both variants stain positively with periodic acid-Schiff because of the carbohydrate in pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC), the precursor of corticotropin. Nuclear TPIT is positive, consistent with corticotroph lineage. The PAS reaction in these cells reflects the glycosylated prohormone, not stored glycogen. Squamous nests are a feature of craniopharyngioma, not of corticotroph tumors. Lipid droplets of steroid-producing cells are a feature of the adrenal cortex, and lipid is not what PAS detects. Ringlike cytokeratin deposition (Crooke change) defines the Crooke cell variant, an aggressive subtype, and is not the source of PAS staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Basophils = FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH; corticotroph tumors are PAS-positive from glycosylated POMC (Robbins). Cushing disease = ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor; after bilateral adrenalectomy the tumor can enlarge (Nelson syndrome). COMLEX: The Crooke cell PitNET, with ringlike cytokeratin, is an aggressive corticotroph variant (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — corticotropes make ACTH from the POMC precursor","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The PAS reaction in these cell","C":"Squamous nests are a feature o"}},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man presents with progressive fatigue, decreased libido, infertility (the couple's infertility workup found his testosterone to be low), and loss of pubic and axillary hair over 4 years. History is notable for a motor vehicle accident 5 years ago requiring craniotomy. Labs: prolactin 72 ng/mL (elevated), FSH and LH both at the lower limit of normal, free T4 low, TSH low-normal, serum cortisol low. MRI shows an interrupted pituitary stalk. What is the mechanism of the paradoxically elevated prolactin in the context of global hypopituitarism?","choices":{"D":"Prolactin is unique among anterior pituitary hormones in being under predominant tonic inhibitory control by hypothalamic dopamine delivered via the portal system; stalk interruption physically prevents dopamine from reaching lactotroph cells, eliminating D2 inhibition so prolactin rises, while all other hormones fall without their hypothalamic releasing hormones","A":"Stalk interruption causes prolactin to accumulate in the hypothalamic-pituitary portal blood because the prolactin-degrading enzyme (prolyl endopeptidase) is located exclusively in the median eminence; without axonal transport to the posterior pituitary, this enzyme is lost, and prolactin builds up in portal blood and spills into the systemic circulation at elevated concentrations","B":"Stalk interruption triggers compensatory lactotroph hyperplasia in the anterior pituitary because FSH and LH deficiency removes gonadotroph competition for portal blood nutrients; the enlarged lactotroph mass secretes prolactin at rates that exceed the capacity of residual dopamine to suppress it, resulting in hyperprolactinemia","C":"Stalk interruption damages the posterior pituitary ADH storage capacity, causing transient SIADH from ADH leakage into the systemic circulation; the elevated systemic ADH cross-activates oxytocin receptors on lactotroph cells, which share structural homology with the oxytocin receptor and drive prolactin gene transcription","E":"Stalk interruption activates the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus to secrete excess TRH directly into the systemic circulation rather than the portal system; systemic TRH then reaches the anterior pituitary lactotrophs via the inferior hypophysial artery, stimulating prolactin release through a non-portal pathway that bypasses dopaminergic inhibition"},"explanation":"The \"stalk effect\" is a classic board concept: any process that disrupts the pituitary stalk (trauma, compression by a tumor, inflammation) causes: (1) elevated prolactin — because dopamine (the tonic inhibitor) cannot reach lactotrophs; (2) decreased all other anterior pituitary hormones — because TRH, CRH, GHRH, and GnRH cannot reach their respective target cells. This is why a pituitary mass can cause paradoxically elevated prolactin even if it does not arise from lactotroph cells. The stalk effect is often called \"disconnection hyperprolactinemia\" and is an important differential for elevated prolactin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stalk effect = elevated prolactin + pan-hypopituitarism. Key differential: true prolactinoma (often very high prolactin) vs. stalk effect (milder elevation). Dopamine agonists work for both but surgery is needed for the tumor causing stalk compression. COMLEX: Pituitary stalk injury from head trauma is a cause of hypopituitarism and elevated prolactin; the OMM approach to evaluation of post-traumatic cervical and cranial somatic dysfunction is a COMLEX-specific clinical context.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormone synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei and storage in the neurohypophysis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman presents 10 weeks after a term delivery complicated by placenta previa requiring transfusion of 5 units of packed red blood cells. She reports complete failure of lactation, profound fatigue, cold intolerance, and loss of body hair. Labs show undetectable prolactin, low free T4 with low TSH, low FSH, low LH, and low cortisol with low ACTH. MRI demonstrates a partially empty sella. Autoimmune workup is ordered. Which finding would most specifically support Sheehan syndrome over autoimmune lymphocytic hypophysitis as the etiology?","choices":{"C":"Lactation failure from the day of delivery after a transfusion-requiring hemorrhage, with a partially empty sella, most specifically supports Sheehan syndrome, because ischemic necrosis of the pregnancy-enlarged gland leaves a shrunken, scarred pituitary rather than an inflamed, enlarged one","A":"Positive serum anti-pituitary antibody titers most specifically support Sheehan syndrome as the etiology, because ischemic necrosis releases intracellular pituitary antigens that stimulate an autoimmune response; high anti-pituitary antibody titers therefore confirm the ischemic mechanism and rule out primary autoimmune hypophysitis","B":"Elevated serum ACTH combined with low cortisol most specifically supports Sheehan syndrome, because ischemic necrosis of the pituitary paradoxically activates residual corticotrophs through a stress-response mechanism; in autoimmune hypophysitis, ACTH is uniformly low because corticotrophs are the first cell type targeted by lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Normal posterior pituitary bright spot on MRI most specifically supports Sheehan syndrome, because the posterior pituitary is preferentially spared by its arterial blood supply in ischemic disease but is the first structure destroyed in autoimmune hypophysitis due to its exposed location at the infundibular stalk","E":"Low serum IGF-1 with absent GH response to insulin-induced hypoglycemia most specifically supports Sheehan syndrome over autoimmune hypophysitis, because somatotroph cells are the first to be destroyed by ischemic injury while autoimmune hypophysitis preferentially targets corticotroph and lactotroph cells, sparing somatotrophs until late disease"},"explanation":"Sheehan syndrome is ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary after severe obstetric hemorrhage: the gland enlarges during pregnancy from lactotroph hyperplasia, hypotension starves its low-pressure portal supply, and the necrotic tissue shrinks into a small or partially empty sella. Failure to lactate from delivery onward is the earliest clue, and the link to the hemorrhage plus the empty sella point to ischemia; lymphocytic hypophysitis instead typically produces an enlarged, enhancing gland with a thickened stalk. Anti-pituitary antibodies point toward an autoimmune process, so they cannot confirm Sheehan syndrome. ACTH is low, not high, once corticotrophs are destroyed. A preserved posterior bright spot is expected in both conditions, and GH deficiency is nonspecific because either process can destroy somatotrophs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sheehan vs. lymphocytic hypophysitis: Sheehan = postpartum hemorrhage + ischemic necrosis; lymphocytic = autoimmune + anti-pituitary antibodies + often presents in late pregnancy or postpartum; may show \"pituitary stalk thickening\" on MRI as early finding. COMLEX: Autoimmune causes of hypopituitarism are becoming more recognized as checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy (anti-PD-1, anti-CTLA-4) can cause hypophysitis as an immune-related adverse event — high yield for modern pharmacology questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormone synthesis in hypothalamic nuclei; posterior pituitary spared in Sheehan due to arterial blood supply","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"ACTH is low, not high, once co","E":"A preserved posterior bright s"}},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology and clinical presentation of hypopituitarism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old obese woman (BMI 36 kg/m²) is referred after an incidental finding of empty sella on brain MRI performed for chronic headaches. She has no visual symptoms. Serum TSH, free T4, cortisol (AM), FSH, LH, and prolactin are all within normal limits. Serum IGF-1 is also normal for her age. Visual field testing is normal. She asks about the significance of this finding and what should be done. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"No hormonal replacement or surgical intervention is required; primary empty sella with normal pituitary function requires only reassurance and periodic clinical monitoring because the compressed pituitary rim retains enough viable parenchyma and blood supply to maintain all hormonal axes, and the headaches should be evaluated independently","B":"Empirical hormone replacement therapy should be initiated immediately because primary empty sella syndrome invariably progresses to complete panhypopituitarism within 5 to 10 years; early hormone replacement prevents adrenal crisis and myxedema coma that occur when subclinical deficits become clinically manifest during physiologic stress","C":"Transsphenoidal surgical packing of the sella with fat or muscle is indicated even in asymptomatic patients with normal pituitary function to prevent progressive pituitary compression from ongoing CSF herniation; delaying surgery until symptoms develop risks sudden catastrophic hypopituitarism from acute compression during a Valsalva maneuver","D":"Urgent MRI of the entire neuraxis is indicated to evaluate for a thoracic or cervical syrinx as the primary driver of elevated intracranial pressure causing the CSF herniation into the sella; untreated syrinx causing secondary empty sella requires spine surgery rather than pituitary-directed management to halt progressive pituitary compression","E":"Measurement of cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure by lumbar puncture is mandatory because primary empty sella in an obese woman is always caused by idiopathic intracranial hypertension; if CSF pressure exceeds 25 cm H2O, acetazolamide must be started immediately to prevent progressive pituitary compression and subsequent hypopituitarism"},"explanation":"Primary empty sella with normal hormonal function requires watchful waiting only. The key reassuring features are: normal all pituitary hormone axes, normal visual fields, and asymptomatic finding. There is no evidence that primary empty sella with preserved function will progress to hypopituitarism. Periodic clinical monitoring (annual labs) is appropriate. The headaches are almost certainly unrelated — primary empty sella is not a cause of headache despite being a common coincidental finding. Note: if this patient had been hypertensive and had signs of elevated ICP, evaluation for pseudotumor cerebri would be warranted separately.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Empty sella + normal pituitary function = reassure and monitor.\" This question tests over-testing — recognizing when findings are incidental and not requiring intervention. Distinguish from secondary empty sella (prior surgery, radiation, infarction), which does require hormonal evaluation. COMLEX: Empty sella associated with pseudotumor cerebri is seen in obese women; weight loss is the primary intervention for pseudotumor cerebri, with acetazolamide as adjunct therapy — this is the targeted management if ICP elevation is confirmed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — Posterior pituitary hormone synthesis; contrast with anterior pituitary vulnerability in empty sella syndrome","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"There is no evidence that prim"}},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with non-small cell lung carcinoma currently receiving cyclophosphamide-based chemotherapy presents with progressive confusion and nausea. Labs: serum Na+ 116 mEq/L, serum osmolality 241 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 510 mOsm/kg, urine Na+ 48 mEq/L. He is euvolemic on examination with no edema or jugular venous distension. Serum TSH, cortisol, creatinine, and blood glucose are all normal. Which mechanism of SIADH is most likely in this patient?","choices":{"B":"Syndrome of cerebral salt wasting from paraneoplastic neural injury causing renal sodium wasting with secondary water loss; cerebral salt wasting produces hyponatremia with concentrated urine and elevated urine sodium identical to SIADH, but these patients are hypovolemic rather than euvolemic, and the absence of volume depletion in this patient argues against this mechanism","A":"Ectopic ADH production by the non-small cell lung carcinoma — while ectopic ADH causes the majority of paraneoplastic SIADH cases in lung cancer, ectopic ADH production is far more characteristic of small cell lung carcinoma (neuroendocrine origin); non-small cell carcinoma causes ectopic ADH production rarely, making drug-induced SIADH from cyclophosphamide the more likely mechanism here","C":"Nephrotoxic tubular injury from cyclophosphamide causing primary failure of the diluting segment in the thick ascending limb, impairing free water excretion; this mechanism would produce urine osmolality and sodium identical to SIADH but would also be accompanied by elevated serum creatinine and reduced GFR, which are absent in this patient","D":"CNS metastases from the lung carcinoma causing hypothalamic compression and stimulating inappropriate ADH secretion from the supraoptic nucleus; this mechanism would be expected to produce additional neurologic signs and symptoms, and distinguishing it from drug-induced SIADH requires brain MRI rather than just biochemical evaluation","E":"Cyclophosphamide-induced SIADH — this alkylating agent potentiates ADH action in the renal collecting duct by sensitizing V2 receptors or directly upregulating AQP2 channel expression, and at high doses can stimulate hypothalamic ADH secretion directly; the result is inappropriate water retention with euvolemic hyponatremia, concentrated urine, and elevated urine sodium"},"explanation":"Drugs are one arm of the FA SIADH mnemonic (HEELD-up water), with SSRIs, carbamazepine and cyclophosphamide as the named examples. Cyclophosphamide causes SIADH, especially at high doses used in conditioning regimens. The euvolemia and normal creatinine distinguish drug-induced SIADH from nephrotoxic tubular injury (elevated creatinine) and cerebral salt wasting (volume-depleted). Ectopic ADH from NSCLC is possible but far less common than from SCLC — SCLC is the neuroendocrine tumor classic for ectopic ADH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drug-induced SIADH common causes: cyclophosphamide, carbamazepine, SSRIs, NSAIDs, chlorpropamide, vincristine, desmopressin. Treatment: discontinue offending drug + water restriction. Severe symptomatic hyponatremia (confusion, seizures): 3% hypertonic saline carefully (correct chronic hyponatremia by less than 10–12 mmol/L in 24 h, Guyton Ch 25, to prevent osmotic demyelination). COMLEX: Distinguishing SIADH from cerebral salt wasting by volume status (euvolemic vs. hypovolemic) is a high-yield COMLEX clinical correlation question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency; contrast with SIADH as ADH excess from ectopic or drug-induced secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"The euvolemia and normal creat"}},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to clinic for recurrent urinary tract infections and excessive urination. Serum sodium is 149 mEq/L, serum osmolality is 310 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality is 82 mOsm/kg despite adequate hydration. Intranasal desmopressin produces no measurable change in urine osmolality (remains 86 mOsm/kg after administration). Plasma ADH is markedly elevated at 22 pg/mL. Genetic testing reveals a hemizygous missense mutation in AVPR2. What is the mechanism by which this mutation prevents urinary concentration despite markedly elevated circulating ADH?","choices":{"E":"The hemizygous AVPR2 mutation produces a nonfunctional V2 receptor that cannot transduce ADH binding into Gsα activation; adenylyl cyclase is not stimulated, cAMP is not produced, and PKA cannot phosphorylate aquaporin-2 vesicle proteins, so AQP2 channels stay in cytoplasmic vesicles and the tubule remains water-impermeable despite massive ADH elevation","A":"The AVPR2 mutation causes constitutive V2 receptor internalization after ADH binding, trapping the receptor-ligand complex in endosomes and preventing receptor recycling; the progressive depletion of surface V2 receptors from repeated internalization leaves collecting duct cells with insufficient receptor density to generate a cAMP signal even when ADH concentrations are maximally elevated","B":"The AVPR2 mutation produces a receptor with enhanced affinity for the V2 antagonist endozepine secreted by astrocytes; endozepine normally fine-tunes water reabsorption, but the mutant receptor binds it so tightly that even extremely high circulating ADH concentrations cannot competitively displace the endogenous V2 antagonist from the active receptor site","C":"The AVPR2 mutation disrupts the V2 receptor transmembrane domains such that the receptor is expressed on the basolateral membrane of collecting duct cells rather than the apical membrane; the mislocalized receptor cannot couple to the Gsα-adenylyl cyclase system located on the basolateral side, preventing cAMP production despite adequate ADH binding","D":"The AVPR2 mutation encodes a receptor with normal Gsα coupling and normal cAMP production, but introduces a truncation in the cytoplasmic tail that prevents PKA-mediated phosphorylation of the receptor itself; without receptor phosphorylation, beta-arrestin recruitment and downstream MAPK signaling that is required for AQP2 vesicle fusion cannot occur"},"explanation":"X-linked nephrogenic DI mechanism: Normal pathway: ADH → V2 receptor → Gsα-GTP → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylation of AQP2 → AQP2 vesicle fusion to apical membrane → water enters cell → exits basolaterally via AQP3/4 → water is reabsorbed. AVPR2 mutation blocks the first step (receptor signaling to Gsα), so the entire downstream cascade fails. High plasma ADH is a compensatory response by the hypothalamus sensing persistent hypertonicity — it confirms the defect is at the kidney (nephrogenic) rather than ADH deficiency (central). Treatment: paradoxical use of thiazides + low-salt diet → mild volume depletion → increased proximal tubule reabsorption (bypasses ADH-dependent collecting duct).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nephrogenic DI = high ADH + fails to respond to desmopressin. X-linked = AVPR2 mutation (V2 receptor). Acquired causes: lithium, demeclocycline, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia. Treatment: thiazide + low-salt diet (paradoxical); NSAIDs (reduce prostaglandin-mediated ADH antagonism); amiloride (for lithium-induced specifically, blocks ENaC that lithium enters through). COMLEX: Connecting V2 receptor signal transduction to AQP2 insertion is high yield for understanding both DI and SIADH pharmacology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — Diabetes insipidus as ADH deficiency (central) or ADH resistance (nephrogenic) with distinct desmopressin responses","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply knowledge of the pathogenesis, pathology, clinical presentation and management of diabetes insipidus and SIADH.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with confirmed SIADH secondary to a small cell lung carcinoma presents with worsening confusion and a serum Na+ of 115 mEq/L. He was started on water restriction 3 days ago but sodium has only risen from 113 to 115 mEq/L, which is an inadequate response. Serum cortisol and TSH are normal. He has no evidence of adrenal or thyroid disease. The team decides to add pharmacologic therapy that specifically antagonizes the mechanism driving his hyponatremia. Which agent most directly targets the receptor mediating ADH-driven water retention in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Tolvaptan — an oral selective V2 receptor antagonist (aquaretic) that competitively blocks ADH binding to V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, preventing cAMP production, AQP2 trafficking, and water reabsorption; this produces free water excretion (aquaresis) without urinary sodium loss, directly counteracting the ectopic ADH-V2 receptor interaction responsible for the euvolemic hyponatremia","B":"Furosemide monotherapy — this loop diuretic inhibits the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of Henle, reducing the medullary concentration gradient needed for ADH-driven water reabsorption in the collecting duct; furosemide is often combined with oral salt supplementation in SIADH but does not directly block V2 receptors and produces isotonic fluid loss rather than selective free water excretion","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide — this thiazide diuretic blocks NCC in the distal convoluted tubule and is paradoxically used in nephrogenic DI to reduce urine volume through volume depletion; it is not indicated for SIADH management and would worsen hyponatremia by its direct effect of increasing ADH sensitivity in the collecting duct","D":"Demeclocycline — this tetracycline antibiotic induces acquired nephrogenic DI by interfering with ADH-stimulated cAMP generation in collecting duct cells, producing ADH resistance as a side effect; while it has been used historically for chronic SIADH, it acts nonspecifically within collecting duct cells and carries significant nephrotoxic risk compared to targeted V2 receptor antagonism","E":"Fludrocortisone — a synthetic aldosterone analog that acts on mineralocorticoid receptors in collecting duct principal cells to increase sodium reabsorption; it is used for mineralocorticoid replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency, does not bind V2 receptors, and its fluid-retaining action would not block the ADH-driven water retention causing this patient's hyponatremia"},"explanation":"Vaptans (vasopressin receptor antagonists) are the most mechanistically specific pharmacologic treatment for SIADH because they directly block the V2 receptor responsible for ADH-driven water retention. Tolvaptan (oral, V2-selective) and conivaptan (IV, V1a/V2 dual) are both FDA-approved for SIADH. Mechanism: V2 antagonism → no cAMP → no AQP2 trafficking → water stays in tubule → aquaresis (free water loss without salt loss) → serum Na+ rises. Important safety: correction must be monitored carefully; avoid overcorrection (>10–12 mEq/L in 24h or >18 mEq/L in 48h) to prevent osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). Conivaptan, the other vaptan, is not among the choices. Fludrocortisone is a synthetic aldosterone analog used for mineralocorticoid replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency; it acts on mineralocorticoid receptors, not V2 receptors, and its salt and fluid retention would not counter ADH-driven water retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vaptans = V2 antagonists for euvolemic and hypervolemic hyponatremia (SIADH, heart failure, cirrhosis). Mechanism: aquaresis = free water loss without sodium loss. Tolvaptan = oral V2-selective; conivaptan = IV V1a+V2. Risk: osmotic demyelination from too-rapid correction. Contraindicated in patients with anuria or volume depletion. COMLEX: Understanding vaptan mechanism from V2 receptor all the way to AQP2 is tested in pharmacology; the clinical indication (euvolemic hyponatremia + water restriction failure) represents the most common clinical question format.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — ADH mechanism of action via V2 receptors and aquaporins; V2 antagonism reverses this pathway in SIADH","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"}],"13":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major constituent of the colloid stored within thyroid follicles, and what is the approximate intracellular-to-blood iodide concentration ratio maintained by a normally functioning thyroid gland?","choices":{"B":"The major colloid constituent is thyroglobulin, a glycoprotein of approximately 335,000 molecular weight synthesized by follicular cells; the thyroid concentrates iodide to about 30 times blood concentration, a ratio that can rise to 250 times under maximal TSH stimulation","A":"The major colloid constituent is albumin, a serum protein actively transported into follicles by receptor-mediated endocytosis; the normally functioning thyroid maintains an iodide concentration ratio of approximately 10 times blood concentration, rising to only 50 times under maximal TSH stimulation","C":"The major colloid constituent is thyroglobulin; however, the iodide concentration gradient across the basolateral membrane is only 2 to 3 times blood concentration under normal resting conditions because the sodium-iodide symporter operates near its maximal capacity at baseline TSH levels","D":"The major colloid constituent is a lipid-protein complex called thyrolipid, which serves as the scaffold for iodination; the iodide concentration ratio is approximately 30 times blood under normal conditions and can rise to 100 times with maximal TSH stimulation","E":"The major colloid constituent is iodinated lipoprotein precursors stored as inactive thyroid hormone intermediates; the normally functioning thyroid concentrates iodide to approximately 30 times blood concentration, but this ratio falls rather than rises when TSH levels are chronically elevated"},"explanation":"Thyroid follicles are the functional unit of the thyroid gland: closed spheres of cuboidal epithelial cells lined around a central space filled with colloid. Colloid is composed predominantly of thyroglobulin, a 335-kDa glycoprotein that contains approximately 70 tyrosine residues serving as the scaffold for thyroid hormone synthesis. The NIS on the basolateral membrane concentrates iodide to ~30× blood under normal conditions and up to 250× when maximally active. TSH is the primary positive regulator of NIS activity; hypophysectomy nearly eliminates iodide trapping. The C cells (parafollicular cells) interspersed between follicles produce calcitonin — a separate function unrelated to thyroid hormone synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyroid follicle anatomy is the structural basis for all synthesis questions: follicular cells → colloid (thyroglobulin) → iodide trapping (NIS). Key fact: thyroid has highest blood flow per gram of any organ except adrenal cortex. Normal iodine requirement is 50 mg/year = 1 mg/week. COMLEX: Thyroid anatomy is a CP3 named clinical presentation; thyroglobulin as a tumor marker (elevated in differentiated thyroid carcinoma) connects synthesis physiology to oncology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — TSH activates the sodium-iodide symporter via Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling, coupling pituitary regulation to iodide trapping in thyroid follicular cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"The NIS on the basolateral mem"}},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells transports iodide against its electrochemical gradient. What is the correct stoichiometry of ion co-transport, and what is the thermodynamic driving force that powers this secondary active transport process?","choices":{"E":"NIS co-transports 3 sodium ions with each iodide ion; the driving force is the potassium concentration gradient established by the Na+/K+-ATPase, with potassium efflux generating a large electrical potential that pulls iodide into the cell through a voltage-sensitive NIS conformation","A":"NIS co-transports 1 sodium ion with each iodide ion in a 1:1 electroneutral exchange; the driving force is the mitochondrial proton gradient generated by oxidative phosphorylation, which directly couples cellular respiration to iodide import via an electrochemical linkage across the inner mitochondrial membrane","B":"NIS co-transports 2 sodium ions with each iodide ion across the basolateral membrane; the driving force is the low intracellular sodium concentration maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase, which creates the inward sodium electrochemical gradient that energizes secondary active iodide transport","C":"NIS co-transports 2 sodium ions with each iodide ion; the primary driving force is direct ATP hydrolysis by the NIS transporter itself, which contains an intrinsic ATPase domain that uses chemical energy to move iodide against both its chemical and electrical gradients simultaneously","D":"NIS co-transports 1 sodium ion with each iodide ion; the driving force is the chloride gradient across the basolateral membrane, which generates a sustained electrical potential difference that indirectly facilitates electrogenic sodium-iodide co-entry independent of Na+/K+-ATPase activity"},"explanation":"NIS stoichiometry: 2 Na+ : 1 I⁻ per transport cycle, making transport electrogenic (net positive charge enters with each cycle). Energy source: the Na+/K+-ATPase on the basolateral membrane pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP hydrolyzed, maintaining low intracellular Na+. The resulting Na+ electrochemical gradient drives NIS to co-transport Na+ down its gradient, simultaneously pulling I⁻ into the cell against its concentration gradient — this is secondary active transport. Clinical relevance: NIS is the pharmacologic target of radioactive iodine (I-131) therapy for hyperthyroidism and thyroid cancer; NIS is also inhibited by perchlorate, thiocyanate, and pertechnetate (all competitive inhibitors). TSH upregulates NIS expression via cAMP/PKA, linking TSH receptor signaling directly to iodide uptake capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NIS = secondary active transport (powered by Na+ gradient), NOT ATPase itself. Stoichiometry: 2 Na+ : 1 I⁻. NIS blockers: perchlorate (competitive inhibitor; used in the perchlorate discharge test) and thiocyanate (from cyanogenic foods); excess iodide acts mainly by blocking organification (Wolff-Chaikoff), with escape via NIS down-regulation. NIS is exploited therapeutically: I-131 concentrates in thyroid via NIS and destroys follicular cells. COMLEX: NIS dysfunction is the basis for congenital hypothyroidism from iodide trapping defects; goiter results from TSH-driven compensatory hyperplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — TSH activates the sodium-iodide symporter via Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling, coupling pituitary regulation to iodide trapping in thyroid follicular cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"After iodide enters the follicular cell via NIS on the basolateral membrane, it must be transported across the apical membrane into the follicular lumen before organification can occur. Which transporter moves iodide from the cytoplasm into the follicular lumen, and what is the ion it exchanges?","choices":{"C":"Pendrin is the apical transporter responsible for iodide efflux into the follicular lumen; it is a chloride-iodide counter-transporter that exchanges iodide for chloride, delivering iodide to the apical surface where thyroid peroxidase oxidizes it for its organification onto thyroglobulin","A":"NIS also functions on the apical membrane in reverse mode to export iodide into the follicular lumen; the reversed electrochemical gradient across the apical membrane is sufficient to drive NIS in the outward direction without any additional energy input or counter-ion exchange mechanism","B":"Thyroid peroxidase itself functions simultaneously as an iodide transporter and oxidizing enzyme on the apical membrane; the peroxidase couples iodide transmembrane translocation directly to its oxidation, ensuring spatial coordination of transport and chemistry at a single enzymatic complex","D":"Megalin on the apical membrane serves as the primary iodide exporter; this same multiligand receptor simultaneously retrieves thyroglobulin from the follicular lumen for intracellular digestion, making it a bidirectional transporter that links iodide delivery to thyroglobulin recycling","E":"A voltage-gated iodide channel on the apical membrane allows passive iodide diffusion down its electrochemical gradient into the follicular lumen; this channel is regulated by TSH via a cAMP-independent pathway involving direct protein kinase C phosphorylation of the channel pore domain"},"explanation":"Iodide transport is bidirectional across the follicular cell: in via NIS on the basolateral membrane (secondary active, 2Na+:1I⁻), and out via pendrin on the apical membrane (chloride-iodide antiporter, electroneutral). Pendrin (SLC26A4) is encoded by the PDS gene; Pendred syndrome = biallelic PDS mutations causing goiter, congenital hypothyroidism (variable), and sensorineural hearing loss (cochlear endolymph iodide transport also impaired). After iodide exits into the follicular lumen, thyroid peroxidase with H₂O₂ as oxidant converts I⁻ to a reactive nascent iodine (I⁰ or I₃⁻) that then attacks tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin to form MIT and DIT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pendrin mutations cause Pendred syndrome (goiter + deafness). The two-step iodide transport: basolateral NIS (in) → apical pendrin (out to lumen). Peroxidase then oxidizes the lumenal iodide immediately at the apical surface. The Wolff-Chaikoff effect (excess iodide temporarily blocks organification) and its escape (NIS downregulation reduces iodide entry) are pharmacologically important. COMLEX: Pendred syndrome is a named autosomal recessive cause of congenital hypothyroidism with goiter; recognizing the goiter + hearing loss combination distinguishes it from other congenital hypothyroid causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — TSH activates the sodium-iodide symporter via Gsα/cAMP second messenger signaling, coupling pituitary regulation to iodide trapping in thyroid follicular cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the coupling reactions that produce T4 and T3 from iodinated tyrosine precursors within the thyroglobulin molecule, including the specific tyrosine combinations that form each hormone?","choices":{"D":"T4 (thyroxine) is formed by coupling two diiodotyrosine (DIT) molecules; T3 (triiodothyronine) is formed by coupling one monoiodotyrosine (MIT) with one DIT; both reactions are catalyzed by thyroid peroxidase with H2O2 at the apical membrane-follicle interface, within the intact thyroglobulin molecule","A":"T4 is formed by the coupling of one MIT and one DIT; T3 is formed by the coupling of two MIT molecules; the coupling reactions are spontaneous oxidative dimerizations that do not require enzymatic catalysis, and they occur in the follicular lumen after thyroglobulin has been completely released from the cell membrane","B":"T4 is formed by the coupling of one MIT and three iodotyrosine residues via a sequential iodine-addition mechanism; T3 is formed by coupling two DIT molecules; the major secretory product of the thyroid is T3 because the DIT-DIT reaction is kinetically favored under normal physiologic TSH levels","C":"T4 is formed by coupling two DIT molecules and T3 by coupling MIT with DIT; however, these reactions occur only after thyroglobulin has been completely cleaved by lysosomal proteases, so coupling takes place on free MIT and DIT molecules in the cytoplasm rather than within the intact thyroglobulin molecule","E":"T4 is formed by oxidative dimerization of two T3 molecules via an additional fourth-iodine addition step catalyzed by a separate thyroid oxidase; T3 is the primary thyroid secretory product and T4 is formed only when T3 synthesis exceeds the binding capacity of nuclear thyroid hormone receptors in peripheral tissues"},"explanation":"Coupling reactions occur within the intact thyroglobulin molecule while it is in the follicular colloid. The key combinations are: MIT + DIT → T3 (3,5,3-triiodothyronine, the active hormone) and DIT + DIT → T4 (thyroxine, the prohormone). T4 is the major product (~93% of secreted hormone) because DIT is more abundantly formed than MIT when iodide is adequate. Small amounts of reverse T3 (RT3 = coupling of DIT onto MIT in the reverse orientation) are formed but are metabolically inactive. Thyroid peroxidase with H₂O₂ catalyzes both the initial oxidation and the coupling step. When iodide is limited, MIT accumulates relative to DIT, and the MIT:DIT ratio increases — more T3 relative to T4 is formed, which is the adaptive response in iodine deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Coupling: MIT + DIT = T3; DIT + DIT = T4. T4 is the major secretory product (93% of output). T3 is 4x more potent than T4. All reactions need thyroid peroxidase + H2O2. Thionamides (PTU, methimazole) block peroxidase → block organification and coupling. COMLEX: Understanding the coupling chemistry explains why PTU and methimazole take 4-8 weeks to lower hormone levels fully (stored T3/T4 must be depleted first); PTU also blocks peripheral T4→T3 conversion (extra advantage in thyroid storm).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — TSH from the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis drives thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification and coupling reactions that form T3 and T4 on thyroglobulin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":1,"stem":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized by thyroid follicular cells and serves as the scaffold for thyroid hormone formation. Which combination of features correctly identifies where thyroglobulin is synthesized, how many tyrosine residues it contains, and how long stored thyroid hormones within thyroglobulin can supply normal body requirements if new synthesis ceases entirely?","choices":{"B":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized in the rough endoplasmic reticulum and processed by the Golgi apparatus before secretion into the follicular lumen; each molecule contains approximately 70 tyrosine amino acids; thyroid hormones stored within thyroglobulin in the follicular colloid can supply normal body requirements for approximately 2 to 3 months","A":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized on free ribosomes in the cytoplasm and transported to the follicular lumen via a signal peptide-independent targeting mechanism; each molecule contains approximately 100 tyrosine amino acids; stored thyroid hormones can meet normal requirements for approximately 1 week before functional deficiency becomes clinically apparent","C":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum and directly inserted into the apical membrane without Golgi processing; each molecule contains approximately 30 tyrosine amino acids that are all iodinated; stored thyroid hormones can supply the body for approximately 2 to 3 weeks before depletion","D":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized in the Golgi apparatus itself and secreted in vesicles without prior endoplasmic reticulum processing; each molecule contains approximately 700 tyrosine amino acids; stored thyroid hormones can supply normal requirements for 5 to 7 years due to the extraordinary colloid storage capacity of the thyroid gland","E":"Thyroglobulin is synthesized by mitochondria of follicular cells and exported to the follicular lumen via a specialized mitochondrial outer membrane channel; each molecule contains approximately 70 tyrosine amino acids; stored thyroid hormones can supply the body for approximately 2 to 3 months as in other protein hormone systems"},"explanation":"Thyroglobulin synthesis follows the standard secretory protein pathway: rough ER → Golgi → secretory vesicles → apical exocytosis into the follicular lumen. MW ~335,000 Da; ~70 tyrosine residues per molecule, of which only about 1/6 (approximately 8-12) are iodinated under normal conditions. After synthesis, each thyroglobulin molecule can contain up to 30 T4 molecules plus a few T3 molecules. The 2-3 month storage buffer is unique to the thyroid — other endocrine glands cannot store hormone reserves lasting more than days to weeks. This explains why iodine deficiency manifests slowly, why antithyroid drugs require weeks to normalize hormone levels, and why thyroid hormone replacement doses can be titrated gradually.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyroid = only endocrine gland with months-long hormone storage. Thyroglobulin itself is not released into blood under normal conditions; only T3 and T4 are released. However, serum thyroglobulin is a useful tumor marker for differentiated thyroid carcinoma (papillary and follicular) because tumor cells release it. COMLEX: The prolonged storage capacity is why clinical hypothyroidism develops insidiously over months (Hashimoto) rather than acutely — the colloid buffer delays symptom onset even as synthesis is progressively destroyed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — TSH from the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis drives thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification and coupling reactions that form T3 and T4 on thyroglobulin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":1,"stem":"During thyroid hormone synthesis, oxidation of iodide ions is an essential prerequisite for organification. Which enzyme catalyzes the oxidation of iodide to its reactive form, what cofactor serves as the oxidant in this reaction, and what happens to thyroid hormone synthesis if this enzyme is pharmacologically blocked or congenitally absent?","choices":{"A":"Thyroid peroxidase oxidizes iodide using hydrogen peroxide as the electron acceptor, generating reactive iodine at the apical membrane; if it is blocked by thionamides such as methimazole or propylthiouracil, or congenitally absent, organification fails, no MIT or DIT forms, and T3 and T4 synthesis stops","B":"Thyroid oxidase generates reactive iodine using molecular oxygen as the direct electron acceptor without requiring hydrogen peroxide; if this enzyme is inhibited, organification is reduced but not eliminated because a minor peroxide-independent iodination pathway remains active through the pendrin transporter at the apical membrane","C":"Thyroid peroxidase reduces hydrogen peroxide while simultaneously oxidizing iodide; if the enzyme is blocked, iodide accumulates in the follicular lumen and undergoes spontaneous non-enzymatic oxidation at physiologic pH, sustaining a low level of organification sufficient to prevent complete hormone synthesis failure","D":"A selenium-dependent deiodinase in the follicular lumen oxidizes iodide using reduced glutathione as the cofactor; if blocked, reverse T3 accumulates because the deiodinase simultaneously prevents aberrant coupling of DIT molecules in the wrong orientation","E":"NADPH oxidase in the apical membrane generates hydrogen peroxide, which then spontaneously oxidizes iodide without enzymatic catalysis; thyroid peroxidase functions only as a coupling enzyme and does not participate in the initial iodide oxidation step; its inhibition reduces T3 and T4 formation but MIT and DIT continue to accumulate"},"explanation":"Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) is a heme-containing enzyme on the apical membrane that performs two sequential functions: (1) oxidizes I⁻ to reactive nascent iodine using H₂O₂ as oxidant; (2) catalyzes organification — the covalent attachment of reactive iodine to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin. Dual inhibition of both steps by thionamides explains their efficacy. DUOX1 and DUOX2 (dual oxidases) generate H₂O₂ at the apical membrane, providing the oxidant for TPO. Congenital TPO deficiency is the most common cause of dyshormonogenetic goiter. Clinical trap: antithyroid drugs do not affect stored T3/T4, explaining the 4-8 week lag before clinical response — the gland must deplete its thyroglobulin-stored hormone reserve first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TPO = dual function: oxidize I⁻ AND organify (iodinate tyrosines). H2O2 is the oxidant. Thionamides (PTU, methimazole) block TPO. PTU also blocks peripheral T4→T3 conversion (5-deiodinase type 1), making it preferred in thyroid storm. Methimazole: once-daily dosing, preferred in pregnancy except first trimester. Both drugs have 4-8 week lag before T4 normalizes (stored hormone must deplete). COMLEX: PTU-specific toxicities: hepatotoxicity (black box warning), ANCA-positive vasculitis. Methimazole: teratogenicity (aplasia cutis) — avoid in first trimester.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — TSH from the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis drives thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification and coupling reactions that form T3 and T4 on thyroglobulin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":1,"stem":"After proteolytic digestion of thyroglobulin releases T3 and T4, approximately 75% of the iodinated tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin are not incorporated into T3 or T4 but instead remain as monoiodotyrosine and diiodotyrosine. What happens to the iodine in these unused residues, and what is the clinical consequence of a congenital defect in this process?","choices":{"C":"The unused MIT and DIT residues are released from thyroglobulin but are immediately re-iodinated by thyroid peroxidase in a reverse organification reaction before they can exit the cell; this futile cycling prevents acute iodine excess from driving uncontrolled thyroid hormone synthesis and contributes to the Wolff-Chaikoff effect","A":"The unused MIT and DIT residues are secreted into the bloodstream along with T3 and T4; the iodine on these circulating MIT and DIT is then deiodinated by hepatic enzymes, and the released iodine enters a shared pool with dietary iodine available for NIS-mediated uptake on the thyroid gland","B":"The unused MIT and DIT residues are stored in the colloid as secondary hormone precursors; when thyroid hormone demand increases, thyroid peroxidase retroactively couples these stored residues into T3 and T4, acting as an emergency hormone reserve beyond the T3 and T4 already stored in intact thyroglobulin molecules","D":"The unused MIT and DIT residues are oxidized to iodinated fatty acids by a thyroid-specific cyclooxygenase enzyme; these iodinated lipids are incorporated into follicular cell membranes where they modulate NIS activity and serve as allosteric feedback inhibitors of iodide trapping when hormone stores are sufficient","E":"A deiodinase within follicular cells cleaves iodine from the free MIT and DIT released during thyroglobulin digestion, recycling nearly all of it for new hormone synthesis; congenital absence of this enzyme causes iodine deficiency and goiter despite adequate dietary iodine because MIT and DIT are lost in urine"},"explanation":"Iodine economy is critical: ~75% of iodinated tyrosines in thyroglobulin (MIT and DIT) never couple into T3 or T4. After lysosomal proteolysis of thyroglobulin releases free MIT and DIT, a cytoplasmic iodotyrosine dehalogenase (DEHAL1 enzyme) cleaves the iodine from these residues, returning iodide to the intracellular pool for re-use. This recycling is essential because the daily dietary iodine requirement is only ~1 mg/week, far less than what would be needed without recycling. Congenital DEHAL1 deficiency: free MIT and DIT leak into the bloodstream and are cleared by kidneys, causing urinary iodine loss, compensatory goiter, and hypothyroidism despite normal dietary iodine. This is a classic cause of dyshormonogenetic goiter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dehalogenase recycles ~75% of iodinated tyrosines within the thyroid. Loss = urinary MIT/DIT loss = goiter + hypothyroidism despite adequate dietary iodine. Distinguish from dietary iodine deficiency (endemic goiter, low urinary inorganic iodine, responds to iodine supplementation). COMLEX: Dyshormonogenetic goiter vs. endemic iodine-deficiency goiter: both have elevated TSH + goiter, but the dehalogenase deficiency shows elevated urinary iodinated amino acids and is unresponsive to iodine supplementation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Thyroid hormones are lipophilic iodothyronines that require carrier proteins for aqueous blood transport; only the free hormone fraction is biologically active","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":1,"stem":"Upon entering the bloodstream, more than 99% of T4 and T3 are immediately bound to plasma carrier proteins. Which primary binding protein and its relative binding affinities for T4 versus T3 best explain the striking difference in half-life between the two hormones in blood?","choices":{"E":"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), synthesized by the liver, is the primary carrier of both hormones; TBG has higher affinity for T4 than for T3, so T4 is released to tissues much more slowly (about half every 6 days versus about 1 day for T3), explaining T4's longer half-life and its role as a circulating prohormone reservoir","A":"Albumin is the primary thyroid hormone carrier, binding the majority of both T4 and T3; albumin has identical high affinity for both hormones, resulting in similar half-lives of approximately 2 to 3 days; changes in serum albumin from hepatic disease proportionally and immediately alter both total and free thyroid hormone levels with direct functional consequences","B":"Thyroxine-binding globulin is the primary carrier; however, TBG has a lower affinity for T4 than for T3, so T3 is bound more tightly and has a longer half-life than T4; this is why T3 is preferred for long-term replacement therapy in patients requiring a stable and slowly released thyroid hormone preparation","C":"Thyroxine-binding prealbumin selectively binds T4 but does not bind T3 at all; because T3 is entirely unbound, it circulates as free hormone at high concentration and accounts for most acute thyroid hormone effects, while TBG plays only a minor buffering role for T4 levels in the blood","D":"All three plasma carrier proteins (TBG, transthyretin, and albumin) have identical binding affinities for T4 and T3, so both hormones have the same circulating half-life; the longer biological duration of T4 action is explained by tighter binding to intracellular nuclear receptors in target cells, not by differential protein binding in blood"},"explanation":"Three plasma proteins bind thyroid hormones: TBG (highest affinity, the main carrier), transthyretin or TBPA (intermediate affinity), and albumin (lowest affinity, high capacity). T4 has higher affinity for TBG than T3; therefore T4 is released very slowly (t½ ~6-7 days for T4 vs. ~1 day for T3). This creates the T4 buffer pool: >99% of circulating T4 is protein-bound and inactive, serving as a long-acting reservoir that is converted to T3 in peripheral tissues by type 1 and 2 deiodinases. Free fraction = biologically active fraction. Only free T4 and free T3 cross cell membranes, enter the nucleus, and activate thyroid hormone receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TBG binds T4 > T3, so T4 has longer half-life. Free hormone = active. TBG increased by: estrogen/OCP, pregnancy, tamoxifen, hepatitis, heroin (increases estrogen). TBG decreased by: androgens, glucocorticoids, nephrotic syndrome (protein loss), liver disease, phenytoin (displaces binding). Free T4 and TSH are unaffected by TBG changes (equilibrium maintained). COMLEX: Elevated total T4 with normal TSH and normal free T4 in a woman on oral contraceptives is classic — recognize as TBG elevation, not hyperthyroidism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Thyroid hormones are lipophilic iodothyronines that require carrier proteins for aqueous blood transport; only the free hormone fraction is biologically active","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"T4 has higher affinity for TBG"}},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman is started on an oral contraceptive containing ethinyl estradiol. A thyroid function panel obtained 3 months later shows: total T4 22 μg/dL (normal 4.8–10.4), TSH 1.8 mU/L (normal), free T4 1.1 ng/dL (normal). She is clinically asymptomatic. Which mechanism best explains this laboratory pattern?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen stimulates hepatic synthesis of TBG, increasing total binding capacity in plasma; the extra TBG transiently binds more T4 and lowers free T4, the pituitary senses this and slightly increases TSH, and thyroid output rises until free T4 returns to normal at a new equilibrium with higher total T4 but normal free T4 and TSH","B":"Estrogen directly stimulates thyroid follicular cells via nuclear estrogen receptors, increasing T4 synthesis and secretion rate; the thyroid gland hypertrophies in response, and total T4 rises while free T4 also rises proportionally; the normal TSH indicates that the elevated T4 level is appropriate for her new metabolic demands during hormonal therapy","C":"Ethinyl estradiol competitively displaces T4 from TBG, releasing more T4 from protein binding and raising free T4; the elevated free T4 suppresses TSH via hypothalamic-pituitary negative feedback, reducing thyroid hormone synthesis; total T4 rises paradoxically because released-bound T4 overwhelms the suppressed new synthesis rate","D":"Ethinyl estradiol is an iodinated compound that competes with iodide at the NIS transporter, partially reducing iodide trapping and selectively impairing T3 synthesis relative to T4; the selective T3 reduction reduces TBG occupancy by T3, leaving more TBG available to bind T4, thereby elevating total T4 without changing free T4","E":"Oral contraceptives increase hepatic synthesis of transthyretin rather than TBG; transthyretin has higher affinity for T4 than TBG, so even a modest rise in transthyretin dramatically elevates total T4; TBG remains unchanged, and the free T4 is unaffected because the normal TBG equilibrium is not disturbed by the transthyretin increase"},"explanation":"This is a classic board scenario: elevated total T4 + normal free T4 + normal TSH + exogenous estrogen = elevated TBG, not hyperthyroidism. Mechanism: estrogen increases hepatic TBG synthesis → more TBG in blood → transiently more T4 bound → free T4 falls transiently → TSH rises transiently → thyroid produces more T4 until free T4 normalizes → new steady state with higher total T4 but normal free T4 and TSH. Other states that raise TBG: pregnancy, tamoxifen, acute hepatitis, heroin. States that lower TBG: androgens, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, phenytoin, carbamazepine, salicylates. The key diagnostic question is always: measure free T4 (not total T4) and TSH to assess actual thyroid status.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen raises TBG → raises total T4 → normal free T4 → euthyroid. This is the classic OCP trap. Never assess thyroid status with total T4 alone. Free T4 + TSH = gold standard. Pregnancy raises TBG similarly; total T4 upper limit of normal is 50% higher in pregnancy (use trimester-specific TSH ranges). COMLEX: TBG changes are a common cause of abnormal total thyroid hormone results in outpatient settings; recognizing this pattern prevents unnecessary radioactive iodine uptake or biopsy workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Thyroid hormones are lipophilic iodothyronines that require carrier proteins for aqueous blood transport; only the free hormone fraction is biologically active","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman who works at a fertilizer manufacturing plant presents with fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain over 8 months. TSH is elevated (12 mU/L) and free T4 is low (0.5 ng/dL). Radioactive iodine uptake scan shows markedly reduced uptake despite normal serum iodine levels. TSH receptor antibodies are absent. Occupational toxicology review identifies prolonged perchlorate exposure, a known competitive inhibitor of NIS. Which step in thyroid hormone synthesis downstream of NIS inhibition is most directly impaired as the proximate cause of hormone deficiency?","choices":{"D":"Perchlorate blocks iodide entry via NIS, depleting intracellular iodide; less iodide is then delivered by pendrin to the follicular lumen, and thyroid peroxidase cannot oxidize enough iodide for organification onto thyroglobulin; the proximate consequence is impaired formation of MIT and DIT, the obligate precursors of T3 and T4","A":"Perchlorate inhibits NIS and simultaneously allosterically activates thyroid peroxidase by binding to its heme iron active site; the paradoxical peroxidase activation increases organification efficiency per iodide molecule, partially compensating for decreased total iodide delivery and limiting the degree of hypothyroidism","B":"Perchlorate blocks iodide import via NIS but has no effect on the follicular lumen iodide concentration because pendrin functions bidirectionally; when intracellular iodide falls, pendrin reverses direction and imports iodide from the lumen into the cell, maintaining organification independently of NIS activity","C":"Perchlorate blocks NIS-mediated iodide import; however, because the thyroid dehalogenase enzyme increases its activity in response to iodide deficit, recycled iodine from MIT and DIT becomes the primary iodide source; iodine recycling fully compensates for impaired dietary iodide uptake and prevents hypothyroidism","E":"Perchlorate inhibits iodide entry; the impaired iodide delivery causes thyroglobulin secretion into the follicular lumen to increase compensatorily, providing more tyrosine substrate per available iodide molecule; this increase in thyroglobulin concentration maintains T3 and T4 formation at normal rates despite reduced total iodide availability"},"explanation":"NIS is the rate-limiting step in thyroid hormone synthesis. Without adequate intracellular iodide: (1) pendrin has no iodide to deliver to the follicular lumen; (2) thyroid peroxidase has no substrate to oxidize; (3) organification fails; (4) MIT and DIT do not form; (5) no coupling can occur; (6) T3 and T4 synthesis drops. The TSH elevation is a compensatory response: low free T4 → reduced negative feedback → TSH rises → NIS expression is upregulated; but perchlorate blocks the upregulated NIS, trapping the thyroid in a state where it cannot compensate. Perchlorate discharge test: pre-exposure to radioiodine followed by perchlorate challenge; a large positive discharge indicates failure of organification, as unorganified iodide washes out when NIS is blocked.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Perchlorate = competitive NIS inhibitor. Used diagnostically in the perchlorate discharge test to identify organification defects. Also causes thyroid dysfunction with chronic occupational or environmental exposure. TSH rises → gland enlarges → compensatory goiter develops. Contrast with radioactive iodine: I-131 enters via NIS and destroys follicular cells (therapeutic). COMLEX: Occupational thyroid toxins include perchlorate (fertilizer/rocket fuel), thiocyanate (cigarette smoke, cassava), and excess iodine (Wolff-Chaikoff); recognizing these causes is part of the environmental/occupational history in hypothyroid workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gsα/cAMP second messenger pathway activated by TSH receptor to drive NIS expression and iodide trapping","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old infant is brought to clinic for poor feeding, prolonged jaundice, hypotonia, and an abnormally large tongue. Thyroid function panel shows T4 of 1.2 μg/dL (critically low) and TSH of 320 mU/L (markedly elevated). Thyroid radionuclide scan shows no uptake in the thyroid bed or elsewhere. Genetic testing confirms homozygous loss-of-function mutations in SLC5A5, encoding the sodium-iodide symporter. What is the cascade of consequences from absent NIS function that explains this clinical picture?","choices":{"C":"Absent NIS function eliminates iodide trapping; without intracellular iodide, pendrin cannot deliver iodide to the follicular lumen, thyroid peroxidase cannot oxidize or organify iodide onto thyroglobulin tyrosines, and no MIT or DIT forms; complete absence of T3 and T4 production removes all negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, causing marked TSH elevation, and the infant develops cretinism with irreversible intellectual disability and growth retardation if not treated with levothyroxine within days to weeks of birth","A":"Absent NIS function eliminates dietary iodide trapping; however, the thyroid can partially compensate via a megalin-mediated alternative pathway that captures iodide-rich proteins from the bloodstream; the compensated iodide intake is sufficient to synthesize enough T4 to prevent severe neurologic impairment, producing only mild subclinical hypothyroidism","B":"Absent NIS function eliminates iodide trapping; T4 synthesis is completely abolished, but T3 synthesis is preserved because T3 can be formed from coupling of two MIT molecules, and MIT iodination does not require NIS-mediated intracellular iodide for the peroxidase reaction at the apical membrane","D":"Absent NIS function severely impairs iodide trapping; the elevated TSH stimulates follicular cell hyperplasia and hypertrophy that eventually activates expression of an alternative iodide transporter with NIS-like function encoded on chromosome 3, providing partial compensation for NIS loss and producing variable phenotypic severity","E":"Absent NIS function causes iodide trapping failure; however, the thyroid dehalogenase enzyme increases activity markedly in response and recycles iodine from MIT and DIT with greater efficiency; the recycled iodine pool supports approximately 20% of normal T4 synthesis, sufficient to prevent severe cretinism but producing mild intellectual disability"},"explanation":"Congenital NIS loss-of-function is a rare cause of dyshormonogenetic goiter and hypothyroidism (paradoxically without goiter when the gland cannot grow due to absent stimulation — though TSH can still drive some hyperplasia even with iodide deficiency). Key features: (1) near-zero radioiodine uptake (confirms NIS loss); (2) massively elevated TSH (no negative feedback); (3) critically low or absent T4 and T3. The clinical urgency is neurologic: thyroid hormone is absolutely essential for fetal and neonatal brain development. The neonatal brain requires T3 for neuronal migration, myelination, and synaptogenesis. Treatment must begin within days of birth; delayed treatment causes irreversible cretinism (intellectual disability, deafness, short stature). This is why universal newborn thyroid screening was introduced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Congenital hypothyroidism — most common preventable cause of intellectual disability worldwide. Causes: thyroid aplasia/ectopy (most common), dyshormonogenesis (NIS, TPO, thyroglobulin mutations, dehalogenase deficiency), iodine deficiency (endemic). Newborn screening: TSH and T4 on filter paper (Guthrie card). Treatment: levothyroxine ASAP. COMLEX: Congenital hypothyroidism is a CP3 named diagnosis. Newborn screening connects to population health and preventive medicine competencies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — TSH receptor Gsα/cAMP signaling drives NIS expression; absent NIS creates unresponsive hypothyroidism despite compensatory TSH elevation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the anatomy of thyroid follicles and explain the role of iodide trapping via the sodium-iodide symporter in thyroid hormone synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Graves disease has markedly elevated free T4 (4.2 ng/dL) and suppressed TSH (<0.01 mU/L). Radioactive iodine uptake at 24 hours is 85% (normal 5–35%). She is started on methimazole. After 6 weeks of confirmed adherence, her free T4 is still 2.8 ng/dL (elevated) and TSH remains suppressed. Which property of thyroid hormone physiology most directly accounts for the delayed normalization of free T4 despite inhibited synthesis?","choices":{"A":"The thyroid gland stores preformed T3 and T4 within thyroglobulin in the follicular colloid in amounts sufficient to supply normal hormonal requirements for 2 to 3 months; methimazole blocks new thyroid hormone synthesis by inhibiting thyroid peroxidase, but cannot mobilize or destroy already-synthesized hormones stored in colloid; the gland continues releasing T4 and T3 from its existing thyroglobulin reservoir until those stores are depleted, explaining the weeks-long lag before circulating hormone levels normalize","B":"Methimazole is a prodrug that requires hepatic activation by CYP3A4 to its active carbimazole metabolite; the 6-week delay reflects the time needed for cytochrome P450-mediated bioactivation in the liver; once activated, the drug simultaneously blocks new synthesis and destroys stored hormone within 48 hours of reaching therapeutic hepatic concentrations","C":"Methimazole blocks thyroid peroxidase but simultaneously stimulates TSH receptor internalization, reducing TSH-driven thyroid stimulation in Graves disease; the net effect is partial rather than complete hormone suppression, and TSH receptor antibodies in Graves disease competitively block methimazole binding to the receptor","D":"Methimazole blocks new thyroid hormone synthesis but also inhibits peripheral type 2 deiodinase, which normally converts T4 to T3 in the liver and brain; the accumulation of unconverted T4 prolongs hyperthyroid symptoms because total free T4 remains elevated even as the more potent T3 levels normalize","E":"Methimazole reduces thyroid hormone synthesis but is competitively overcome by the very high radioiodine uptake in Graves disease; the elevated NIS activity driven by TSH receptor antibodies continues to import iodide faster than methimazole can block peroxidase, sustaining organification at a rate that offsets the pharmacologic inhibition"},"explanation":"The 2-3 month thyroid hormone storage buffer is clinically important: antithyroid drugs block synthesis but do not accelerate depletion of stored hormone. Normalization of free T4 on methimazole typically takes 4-8 weeks in most patients and up to 3 months in patients with large goiters or high pretreatment hormone levels. During this lag, beta-blockers (propranolol, atenolol) are used to control adrenergic symptoms (tachycardia, tremor, anxiety) because they work within minutes-hours rather than weeks. This also explains why propylthiouracil (PTU) has the added advantage of blocking peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion (inhibits type 1 deiodinase) — in thyroid storm, where rapid lowering of the most active hormone (T3) is needed, PTU is preferred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Methimazole (preferred for most) blocks TPO, takes 4-8 weeks to normalize T4. PTU: also blocks T4→T3 conversion (type 1 deiodinase), use in thyroid storm or first trimester. Add beta-blockers for immediate symptom control. Both drugs: agranulocytosis risk (0.1–0.5%, Katzung Ch 38), rash. PTU-specific: hepatotoxicity (black box), ANCA vasculitis. Methimazole-specific: teratogenicity (avoid in T1). COMLEX: Beta-blockers for thyrotoxicosis symptom control (not the underlying mechanism) is a CP3 named treatment; the mechanism distinction between symptom control and hormone-level correction is high yield.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — TSH receptor Gsα/cAMP signaling drives follicular cell growth and NIS upregulation; TSH receptor antibodies in Graves mimic this signal constitutively","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"This also explains why propylt"}},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl from a mountainous landlocked region is brought to a traveling clinic with a large visible anterior neck mass, slow mentation, fatigue, and failure to progress in school. TSH is markedly elevated and T4 is critically low. Neck ultrasound shows a massively enlarged thyroid with large colloid-filled follicles. Urinary iodide excretion is extremely low. Which step in thyroid hormone synthesis is most directly impaired by iodine deficiency, and through which mechanism does chronic TSH elevation produce goiter in this setting?","choices":{"E":"Iodine deficiency directly impairs organification because thyroid peroxidase cannot iodinate thyroglobulin tyrosines without adequate iodide substrate; reduced T3 and T4 synthesis removes HPT negative feedback, TSH rises chronically, and TSH-driven Gsα/cAMP signaling increases NIS expression and causes follicular hyperplasia and hypertrophy, producing goiter","A":"Iodine deficiency directly impairs the coupling step rather than organification; MIT and DIT form normally from residual dietary iodide because organification requires less iodide than coupling; uncoupled MIT and DIT accumulate on thyroglobulin and cause osmotic swelling of the follicular colloid, mechanically driving gland enlargement without TSH-mediated cellular hyperplasia","B":"Iodine deficiency primarily impairs thyroglobulin synthesis rather than iodine organification; without adequate iodine incorporated into the thyroglobulin protein backbone, the misfolded thyroglobulin is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and activates an unfolded protein response that stimulates follicular cell proliferation via stress-activated kinases","C":"Iodine deficiency impairs the pinocytosis-mediated release step rather than synthesis; T4 and T3 are successfully formed on thyroglobulin, but without iodide available as a cofactor for lysosomal protease activation, the hormones cannot be cleaved from stored thyroglobulin, causing progressive colloid accumulation and mechanical gland enlargement","D":"Iodine deficiency impairs the coupling step selectively; the MIT-DIT coupling to form T3 is more iodide-sensitive than the DIT-DIT coupling to form T4 because MIT has lower iodide incorporation efficiency; therefore T3 deficiency develops before T4 deficiency, producing a T3-deficient hypothyroid state with paradoxically normal or elevated T4"},"explanation":"Iodine deficiency goiter — the most common cause of goiter worldwide: insufficient dietary iodide → reduced organification and coupling → low T4 and T3 → loss of negative feedback → elevated TSH → TSH-driven follicular hyperplasia, hypertrophy, NIS upregulation → goiter. The adaptive response attempts to maximize T3 synthesis relative to T4 (MIT:DIT ratio increases as MIT is more efficiently formed with limited iodide, favoring MIT+DIT coupling to T3). Prevention: iodized salt (1:100,000 ratio). Treatment: iodine supplementation (prevents and partly reverses the goiter in early stages); levothyroxine if symptomatic hypothyroidism present. Goiter itself can cause compressive symptoms: dysphagia, hoarseness, stridor (tracheal compression), superior vena cava obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iodine deficiency = most common cause of preventable intellectual disability and goiter worldwide. Cretinism = congenital hypothyroidism from maternal iodine deficiency during pregnancy. Goiter mechanism: low T4 → high TSH → follicular hyperplasia. In pregnancy, the recommended iodide intake rises from 150 to 200 mcg/day (Katzung Ch 38). COMLEX: Maternal iodine deficiency in first trimester causes fetal brain damage even before the fetal thyroid is functional (Robbins Ch 24); this is a named CP3 developmental disorder (cretinism) requiring prenatal supplementation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — HPT axis: low T4 removes negative feedback, TSH rises and drives thyroid hyperplasia; iodine deficiency blocks the synthesis that TSH is trying to stimulate","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with persistent atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone (40% iodine by weight). Three months later, thyroid function shows: TSH 8.4 mU/L (elevated), free T4 0.6 ng/dL (low), free T3 1.8 pmol/L (markedly low). The disproportionate T3 reduction relative to T4 prompts investigation. Which combination of mechanisms most specifically explains why T3 is reduced to a greater degree than T4 in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"B":"Amiodarone selectively blocks pendrin-mediated iodide export across the apical membrane without inhibiting basolateral NIS, causing iodide to accumulate intracellularly; the excess intracellular iodide competitively inhibits the MIT-DIT coupling step while paradoxically stimulating the DIT-DIT coupling step, producing relatively more T4 and less T3","A":"Amiodarone preferentially inhibits the MIT-DIT coupling reaction over the DIT-DIT coupling reaction because of its structural resemblance to monoiodotyrosine; since MIT-DIT coupling forms T3 and DIT-DIT coupling forms T4, T3 synthesis is selectively abolished at the organification-coupling step while T4 formation continues relatively normally, directly producing the disproportionate T3 reduction","C":"Amiodarone and desethylamiodarone inhibit type 1 and type 2 deiodinases in liver, muscle, and other tissues; because about 80% of circulating T3 comes from peripheral 5′-deiodination of T4, T3 falls more than T4, while the iodine load can block organification (Wolff-Chaikoff) and lower T4 synthesis","D":"Amiodarone triggers the Wolff-Chaikoff effect due to its iodine load; the Wolff-Chaikoff effect preferentially suppresses T3 organification before T4 organification because T3-generating organification requires a lower local iodide concentration threshold; when this threshold is exceeded, T3 synthesis fails while T4 synthesis is maintained at the higher iodide concentration","E":"Amiodarone inhibits the lysosomal protease that specifically cleaves T3 residues from thyroglobulin without affecting T4 cleavage sites; consequently, stored T3 accumulates in the thyroglobulin-containing colloid rather than being secreted into the blood, directly causing low serum T3 despite normal intraglandular T3 synthesis and T4 secretion"},"explanation":"Amiodarone (40% iodine by weight per FA) affects the thyroid in two main ways here. First, it and its metabolite desethylamiodarone inhibit type 1 and type 2 5′-deiodinases, so T4 is not converted to T3; because about 80% of circulating T3 is generated peripherally, T3 falls far more than T4, and reverse T3 accumulates. Second, its iodine load can block organification (Wolff-Chaikoff effect), and a gland that fails to escape makes less T4. Here TSH is high and free T4 low, so this patient has amiodarone-induced hypothyroidism (failure to escape Wolff-Chaikoff) superimposed on the deiodinase block that makes T3 fall most. Amiodarone does not selectively block MIT-DIT coupling, pendrin, or cleavage of T3 from thyroglobulin, and the Wolff-Chaikoff effect does not spare T4 organification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Amiodarone effects: contains 40% iodine by weight; inhibits T4→T3 conversion (deiodinase); can cause amiodarone-induced hypothyroidism (in iodine-sufficient areas, due to Wolff-Chaikoff failure to escape) or amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis type 1 (iodine-excess driven synthesis, iodine-deficient areas) or type 2 (destructive thyroiditis). Classic pattern: elevated T4, elevated rT3, low T3. COMLEX: Amiodarone thyrotoxicosis is a named cardiac emergency; treatment involves antithyroid drugs (type 1) or glucocorticoids (type 2); rarely, amiodarone must be discontinued despite arrhythmia risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — HPT axis: T3 (not T4) is the primary hormone activating negative feedback at the pituitary level; peripheral T4 to T3 conversion is essential for normal HPT axis homeostasis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Amiodarone does not selectivel","E":"Amiodarone does not selectivel"}},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the process of thyroid hormone formation including iodination of tyrosine, coupling reactions to form T3 and T4, and storage in thyroglobulin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old child is found to have hypothyroidism with markedly elevated TSH, low T4, and a visible goiter. Dietary iodine is adequate. A perchlorate discharge test is performed: radioactive iodine (I-131) is administered, uptake is measured at 2 hours, then potassium perchlorate is given. Repeat measurement shows 42% of the accumulated I-131 is released after perchlorate administration. Genetic testing confirms homozygous loss-of-function mutations in the TPO gene encoding thyroid peroxidase. Which step in thyroid hormone formation is blocked, and why does the perchlorate discharge test yield a large positive result in this patient?","choices":{"C":"TPO mutations block both oxidation of iodide and its organification onto thyroglobulin tyrosines; iodide enters the cell via NIS and is transported to the follicular lumen via pendrin, but cannot be covalently bound to thyroglobulin; this unorganified free iodide is released from the follicular lumen and cell when NIS is blocked by perchlorate, yielding a large positive discharge of the accumulated radioiodine because the iodide is not covalently fixed to thyroglobulin","A":"TPO mutations block only the coupling step, not organification; MIT and DIT form normally but cannot be joined into T3 and T4; the perchlorate discharge test is negative because MIT and DIT are covalently bound to thyroglobulin and cannot be washed out when NIS is blocked, distinguishing this from organification defects that give a positive discharge","B":"TPO mutations block only iodide oxidation but not coupling; MIT and DIT do not form, but any free iodide that spontaneously oxidizes at physiologic pH is efficiently coupled into T4; the perchlorate discharge test shows a small positive result because only the fraction of iodide that never spontaneously oxidized is released after NIS blockade","D":"TPO mutations block the pinocytosis step by which follicular cells engulf colloid; stored thyroglobulin with T3 and T4 cannot be retrieved from the lumen, causing colloid to accumulate; the perchlorate discharge test releases the large pool of covalently bound T4 and T3 from the lumen, giving a large positive discharge as hormone leaves the follicle","E":"TPO mutations block NIS-mediated iodide entry rather than organification; the perchlorate discharge test would show a false-positive result because the residual uptake seen at 2 hours reflects non-specific follicular iodide binding rather than NIS-mediated transport; true NIS-based iodide is not discharged because it was never taken up by the mutant cells"},"explanation":"The perchlorate discharge test is a functional assay for organification: (1) administer radioiodine; (2) measure thyroid uptake (reflects iodide trapping via NIS); (3) give perchlorate to block NIS; (4) measure again. If iodide has been organified (covalently bound to tyrosines on thyroglobulin), it cannot be discharged — perchlorate blocking NIS has no effect on covalently incorporated iodide. If iodide is unorganified (free in the lumen or cytoplasm), it diffuses out when NIS is blocked — large positive discharge. Positive discharge test diagnoses: TPO deficiency (most common), thyroglobulin mutations (abnormal T scaffold), pendrin deficiency (iodide never reaches the lumen efficiently). Negative test: normal organification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Perchlorate discharge test: positive (large discharge) = organification defect. Causes: TPO mutation (most common), pendrin deficiency (Pendred syndrome), thyroglobulin mutation, severe iodine deficiency. Normal result: organification intact. COMLEX: Dyshormonogenetic goiter (enzymatic defects) vs. endemic goiter (iodine deficiency) — both cause goiter + elevated TSH + low T4, but perchlorate test positive only in organification defects; treatment differs (enzyme-related requires lifelong levothyroxine; dietary requires iodine supplementation).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — TSH drives organification via cAMP signaling; absent organification from TPO loss is not correctible by TSH-mediated upregulation of the synthetic machinery","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with confirmed primary hypothyroidism is started on levothyroxine (T4 only). She asks her physician why she receives only T4 when she has read that T3 is four times more potent and that most thyroid hormone receptor binding involves T3. The physician explains the physiologic rationale. Which explanation most accurately describes why T4 monotherapy is appropriate for most patients with hypothyroidism?","choices":{"A":"T4 functions as a prohormone converted to T3 in peripheral tissues by type 1 and type 2 deiodinases; about 80% of circulating T3 derives from this conversion, and T4's longer half-life from higher TBG affinity provides stable, sustained T3 delivery without the peaks and troughs of direct T3 administration","B":"T4 is used because it has higher nuclear thyroid hormone receptor affinity than T3; the thyroid hormone receptor requires the four-iodine configuration for full activation; peripheral deiodinases strip one iodine after gene transcription is complete, inactivating T4 to T3 as a post-receptor termination step that limits transcriptional overstimulation","C":"T4 is used because direct T3 administration suppresses TSH below the threshold needed to maintain NIS expression in residual thyroid tissue; without adequate TSH stimulation, NIS is downregulated and the thyroid loses its ability to trap any residual dietary iodine, accelerating gland atrophy beyond what replacement therapy alone would cause","D":"T4 is preferred because T3 cannot be reliably absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract; the three-iodine configuration makes T3 susceptible to intestinal iodoperoxidases that degrade it before mucosal uptake, so oral T3 bioavailability is consistently below 5% regardless of dose or formulation","E":"T4 is preferred because levothyroxine competitively inhibits TSH receptor internalization in residual thyroid tissue; this preserved TSH receptor signaling maintains follicular cell viability and prevents accelerated thyroid atrophy, which would make future dose reductions impossible and eliminate any chance of disease remission"},"explanation":"T4-to-T3 conversion: ~80% of circulating T3 comes from peripheral 5-deiodination of T4 (type 1 deiodinase in liver, type 2 in brain/pituitary/skeletal muscle). Direct thyroid T3 secretion accounts for only ~20% of circulating T3. Since T4 is the dominant product of the thyroid and is converted to active T3 in target tissues, levothyroxine (synthetic T4) effectively replaces both T4 and T3. The longer half-life of T4 (~7 days vs. ~1 day for T3) means once-daily dosing provides stable hormone levels. If T3 (liothyronine) is given orally, it is rapidly absorbed and causes short-duration peaks in T3 — the pharmacokinetics are less physiologic. Some patients with persistent symptoms on T4 monotherapy may benefit from combination T4+T3 therapy, but this remains debated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 = prohormone; T3 = active hormone. ~80% of T3 comes from peripheral conversion. T4 half-life = 7 days (once-daily levothyroxine is appropriate). T3 half-life = 1 day (peaks and troughs with direct T3 administration). Levothyroxine absorption: best on empty stomach, 30-60 min before food; impaired by calcium, iron supplements, proton pump inhibitors, sucralfate. Check TSH at 6 weeks after dose change. COMLEX: Levothyroxine dosing and absorption interactions are a named CP3 pharmacology topic; calcium and iron timing is the classic board trap.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Thyroid hormones are lipophilic amines transported on carrier proteins; T4 higher protein binding explains its longer duration of action compared to T3","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"If T3 (liothyronine) is given "}},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with primary hypothyroidism has been on levothyroxine for 6 months but TSH remains persistently elevated at 18 mU/L despite dose increases. He states he takes his medication every morning. A detailed medication reconciliation reveals he takes levothyroxine with his morning coffee and immediately follows it with calcium carbonate for acid reflux. Free T4 is consistently low. Which mechanism best explains his persistent hypothyroidism despite adherence?","choices":{"B":"Calcium carbonate forms insoluble complexes with levothyroxine in the intestinal lumen, substantially reducing its bioavailability; coffee impairs mucosal levothyroxine absorption through chlorogenic acids and possibly increased intestinal motility; together they substantially reduce the fraction of the oral dose reaching systemic circulation, producing low free T4 despite adherence","A":"Calcium carbonate increases gastric and intestinal pH, which converts levothyroxine from its absorbed ionized form to an unabsorbable neutral form; the neutralized drug precipitates in the intestine rather than crossing the mucosal epithelium; coffee has no effect on absorption but does increase renal clearance of levothyroxine metabolites by upregulating CYP1A2 in proximal tubular cells","C":"Coffee contains high concentrations of iodine that competitively inhibit levothyroxine from binding to TBG in the portal blood; the unbound levothyroxine is immediately degraded by hepatic first-pass metabolism before reaching systemic circulation; calcium carbonate is entirely absorbed before levothyroxine is administered and has no direct interaction with the drug in the intestinal lumen","D":"Calcium carbonate alkalinizes the duodenum, activating calcium-dependent peptidase enzymes that cleave the iodine from levothyroxine before absorption; once deiodinated, the residual tyrosine backbone is absorbed but has no hormonal activity; coffee has no direct effect on absorption but induces CYP3A4 in the duodenal mucosa, further accelerating levothyroxine degradation","E":"The persistent TSH elevation is unrelated to absorption; both calcium carbonate and coffee are pharmacologically inert with respect to levothyroxine; the true cause of the persistent hypothyroidism is that he has autoimmune polyglandular syndrome with simultaneous adrenal insufficiency causing cortisol deficiency that mimics levothyroxine resistance at the pituitary level"},"explanation":"Levothyroxine absorption requires: empty stomach, low gastric acidity, no competing complexing agents. Agents that impair absorption by complex formation or other mechanisms: calcium carbonate, calcium acetate, ferrous sulfate (iron), sucralfate, aluminum hydroxide, PPIs and H2 blockers (raise gastric pH, reducing solubilization), cholestyramine, colestipol. Coffee also impairs absorption (Katzung Ch 38 lists it with bran and soy). Timing solution: take levothyroxine 30-60 minutes before meals and separate it from calcium by at least 4 hours (Katzung Ch 38). Alternative: some patients do better with bedtime dosing (stomach emptier at night). Liquid formulations may bypass some absorption interactions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Levothyroxine absorption impaired by: calcium, iron, antacids (aluminum, calcium), cholestyramine, sucralfate, coffee. Take on empty stomach, 30-60 min before food/other meds. Drugs that increase T4 metabolism (inducers): rifampin, phenytoin, carbamazepine (increase CYP enzymes → faster T4 degradation). COMLEX: Levothyroxine-drug interactions are a named pharmacology competency; the calcium/iron interaction is the most commonly tested. In patients with persistent elevated TSH despite claimed adherence, always ask about timing and co-administration with food/other medications before increasing the dose.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Lipophilic hormones require careful consideration of bioavailability; levothyroxine absorption from the gut depends on physicochemical conditions that determine its solubility and mucosal transport","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Coffee also impairs absorption","C":"Agents that impair absorption ","D":"Coffee also impairs absorption","E":"Agents that impair absorption "}},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the release of thyroid hormones from thyroglobulin and explain thyroid hormone transport in blood bound to thyroid-binding proteins.","batch":2,"stem":"Thyroid follicular cells take up colloid at their apical surface, and lysosomal proteases cleave T4 and T3 from thyroglobulin so that the free hormones diffuse into the surrounding capillaries. Most thyroglobulin is therefore never released into the blood, yet a small amount of intact thyroglobulin normally enters the circulation. Which mechanism accounts for this?","choices":{"D":"Some colloid thyroglobulin binds megalin on the apical membrane and is carried across the cell by transcytosis to the basolateral membrane","A":"Thyroglobulin leaks between follicular cells through tight junctions that open briefly when TSH stimulates pseudopod formation at the apical surface","B":"Lysosomes that fail to fuse with colloid vesicles release undigested thyroglobulin through the basal membrane by constitutive exocytosis","C":"Newly made thyroglobulin leaves the Golgi in both directions, with a fixed fraction secreted at the basolateral membrane before it is iodinated","E":"Thyroglobulin is cleaved to free T4 and T3 within the follicular lumen by colloid proteases, and the protein fragments diffuse out between cells"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 77) describes release: the apical surface sends out pseudopods that form pinocytic vesicles of colloid, lysosomes fuse with them, and proteases digest thyroglobulin to free T4 and T3, which diffuse through the base of the cell into capillaries. In addition, some colloid thyroglobulin enters the cell by endocytosis after binding megalin, a protein on the luminal membrane; the megalin-thyroglobulin complex is carried by transcytosis to the basolateral membrane, where a portion of the megalin remains bound to thyroglobulin and is released into the capillary blood. Leakage through tight junctions is not the route described. Lysosomal digestion releases free hormones, not intact thyroglobulin, through the base of the cell. Newly synthesized thyroglobulin is secreted into the follicle lumen for iodination rather than into the blood. Thyroglobulin is digested inside the cell, not in the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Release: pinocytosis of colloid → lysosomal proteolysis of thyroglobulin → free T4 (about 93%) and T3 (about 7%) diffuse into capillaries; MIT and DIT are deiodinated and their iodine recycled (Guyton Ch 77). COMLEX: More than 99% of released T4 and T3 binds plasma proteins made by the liver, mainly thyroxine-binding globulin (Guyton Ch 77).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — TSH increases proteolysis of thyroglobulin and release of T4 and T3","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Leakage through tight junction","C":"Newly synthesized thyroglobuli"}}],"14":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary molecular mechanism by which thyroid hormones exert their metabolic effects on target cells?","choices":{"E":"T4 is converted intracellularly to T3 by deiodinases; T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors that heterodimerize with retinoid X receptors at response elements to activate gene transcription.","A":"T4 binds directly to plasma membrane G protein-coupled receptors on target cells, activating adenylyl cyclase to increase intracellular cAMP, which phosphorylates metabolic enzymes to increase basal metabolic rate.","B":"T3 binds to cytoplasmic receptor proteins that are subsequently phosphorylated and translocated to the nucleus, where they activate the STAT5 transcription factor independently of any DNA response element.","C":"T4 binds to transmembrane tyrosine kinase receptors and triggers receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation, activating the RAS-MAPK signaling cascade to stimulate mitochondrial biogenesis.","D":"T3 diffuses across the nuclear membrane and binds to homodimeric receptors at glucocorticoid response elements, increasing transcription of genes encoding enzymes of oxidative phosphorylation."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormones are lipophilic and enter cells by carrier-mediated transport. Intracellular type 2 deiodinase converts T4 to T3, the active ligand. T3 binds the thyroid hormone receptor (THR), which forms a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor (RXR) at specific thyroid hormone response elements on DNA, initiating gene transcription and synthesis of new proteins that elevate basal metabolic rate. The other options describe GPCR, JAK-STAT, tyrosine kinase, and glucocorticoid receptor mechanisms, none of which apply to thyroid hormone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T3 is the active intracellular ligand; T4 is a prohormone. THR-RXR heterodimer = classic nuclear receptor concept. COMLEX: Thyroid hormones are the premier lipophilic nuclear receptor example; understanding this mechanism is foundational for interpreting lab findings and drug interactions such as amiodarone inhibiting T4-to-T3 conversion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Lipophilic hormones use intracellular nuclear receptors; T3 binding to the THR-RXR heterodimer at thyroid hormone response elements is the canonical example of this receptor class.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The other options describe GPC","B":"The other options describe GPC","C":"The other options describe GPC","D":"The other options describe GPC"}},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher injects a large single dose of thyroxine (T4) intravenously into a healthy volunteer. The basal metabolic rate (BMR) is monitored every 12 hours. No measurable change in BMR is detected for the first 2 to 3 days after injection. Which of the following best explains this latent period?","choices":{"A":"The delay reflects time for intracellular T4-to-T3 conversion, receptor binding, gene transcription, translation, and accumulation of enough new metabolic proteins to measurably raise BMR.","B":"T4 undergoes extensive first-pass hepatic glucuronidation that temporarily inactivates it; the latent period represents the time for hepatic enzymes to be saturated and allow free T4 to reach peripheral tissues.","C":"Injected T4 must first stimulate hypothalamic TRH release, which activates anterior pituitary TSH secretion; only after TSH reaches the thyroid gland does cellular metabolic activation begin.","D":"T4 is sequestered in erythrocytes for the first 48 hours after injection; gradual release from red blood cell binding proteins limits initial bioavailability and delays metabolic effects.","E":"The anterior pituitary must first reduce TSH secretion in response to exogenous T4; this fall in TSH is required before target-organ thyroid hormone receptors become upregulated and responsive."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormones act primarily through nuclear receptor-mediated gene transcription, which is an inherently slow process. T4 must first be converted to T3 by intracellular deiodinases, T3 must bind THR-RXR heterodimers at TREs, gene transcription must occur, mRNA must be translated, and new proteins must accumulate to sufficient levels to alter metabolic rate. The maximal metabolic effect of T4 occurs at 10-12 days; T3 acts faster with a latent period of only 6-12 hours. The other options describe plausible-sounding but incorrect mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 latent period = 2-3 days, max effect at 10-12 days; T3 latent period = 6-12 hours, max at 2-3 days. This is tested in pharmacokinetics of levothyroxine therapy. COMLEX: In hypothyroid crisis, T3 (liothyronine) may be preferred over T4 for faster effect given its shorter latent period.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Lipophilic hormones use intracellular nuclear receptors; T3 binding to the THR-RXR heterodimer at thyroid hormone response elements is the canonical example of this receptor class.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":1,"stem":"Thyroid hormones activate the transcription of hundreds of genes in virtually every cell of the body. Which of the following best describes the overall cellular consequence of this widespread genomic activation?","choices":{"D":"Increased synthesis of metabolic enzymes, mitochondrial proteins, Na+/K+-ATPase, and transport proteins throughout the body, producing a generalized rise in basal metabolic rate and functional activity.","A":"Selective upregulation of glycolytic enzymes in skeletal muscle and adipocytes with simultaneous suppression of hepatic and cardiac oxidative phosphorylation, redistributing energy production away from aerobic metabolism.","B":"Direct stimulation of target endocrine glands including the adrenal cortex and gonads through thyroid hormone receptor activation in those glands, causing a positive cascade of hormonal output across multiple axes.","C":"Downregulation of mitochondrial electron transport chain genes to reduce reactive oxygen species production, shifting cellular energetics toward anaerobic glycolysis and lactate-based ATP generation.","E":"Primary induction of insulin and glucagon gene expression in pancreatic islets through thyroid hormone receptor activation, driving secondary effects on carbohydrate metabolism in peripheral tissues."},"explanation":"The net result of thyroid hormone nuclear receptor activation is a generalized increase in functional activity throughout the body. This is accomplished by synthesizing hundreds of new proteins including metabolic enzymes, mitochondrial components (increasing number and size of mitochondria), Na+/K+-ATPase (increasing membrane ion transport and heat production), and structural proteins. The basal metabolic rate can rise 60-100% above normal with excess thyroid hormone and fall 40-50% with total deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key downstream targets include mitochondria (number and size), Na+/K+-ATPase (heat generation), and enzymes of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. These explain why hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance, weight loss, and increased O2 consumption. COMLEX: Thyroid hormone is the primary regulator of BMR; changes in thyroid status alter tissue texture and metabolic examination findings relevant to osteopathic assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Lipophilic hormones use intracellular nuclear receptors; T3 binding to the THR-RXR heterodimer at thyroid hormone response elements is the canonical example of this receptor class.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following cardiovascular changes is the most direct consequence of elevated thyroid hormone levels?","choices":{"C":"Increased cardiac output and heart rate from peripheral vasodilation driven by elevated tissue metabolism and a direct excitatory effect of thyroid hormone on cardiac pacemaker activity, with output sometimes 60% above normal.","A":"Decreased heart rate and increased stroke volume caused by thyroid hormone activation of parasympathetic tone through direct stimulation of hypothalamic vagal nuclei and upregulation of muscarinic receptor density on the sinoatrial node.","B":"Increased mean arterial pressure from arteriolar vasoconstriction triggered by thyroid hormone upregulation of vascular alpha-1 adrenergic receptors in peripheral resistance vessels throughout the body.","D":"Decreased cardiac output with reflex increase in systemic vascular resistance, caused by direct myocardial depression from excess thyroid hormone binding to mitochondrial receptors in ventricular cardiomyocytes.","E":"Proportional increases in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure caused by co-secretion of aldosterone stimulated by elevated TSH levels and subsequent sodium and water retention by the renal collecting duct."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormone increases tissue metabolism, generating vasodilatory metabolic byproducts in peripheral tissues. This peripheral vasodilation lowers total peripheral resistance and increases venous return, driving up cardiac output. Thyroid hormone also directly increases heart rate and cardiac excitability. The pulse pressure is widened (systolic rises 10-15 mmHg, diastolic falls), but mean arterial pressure remains approximately normal. Cardiac output can fall to 50% of normal in severe hypothyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperthyroidism = high-output state; increased HR, increased CO, widened pulse pressure. Severe hyperthyroidism can cause cardiac decompensation from myocardial protein catabolism. COMLEX: Atrial fibrillation is a classic complication of hyperthyroidism; thyroid function should always be checked in new-onset atrial fibrillation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone and thyroid hormone both increase protein synthesis and fat utilization; comparing their metabolic profiles clarifies how different hormone classes regulate whole-body energy balance.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"E":"The pulse pressure is widened "}},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with newly diagnosed primary hypothyroidism has a fasting total cholesterol of 318 mg/dL and is at high cardiovascular risk. Which mechanism most directly explains her hypercholesterolemia?","choices":{"A":"Elevated TSH directly activates HMG-CoA reductase in hepatocytes through TSH receptor-mediated cAMP signaling, driving excess de novo cholesterol synthesis independent of any change in peripheral thyroid hormone levels.","B":"Low thyroid hormone reduces hepatic LDL receptor expression and cholesterol secretion into bile, impairing the two main routes of hepatic cholesterol clearance and raising LDL and total cholesterol.","C":"Reduced thyroid hormone increases pancreatic glucagon secretion, which redirects hepatic acetyl-CoA away from ketogenesis toward cholesterol biosynthesis, selectively increasing VLDL and LDL production.","D":"Hypothyroidism causes upregulation of the intestinal NPC1L1 cholesterol transporter, dramatically increasing dietary cholesterol absorption and overwhelming normal hepatic clearance capacity.","E":"Decreased thyroid hormone reduces lipoprotein lipase activity in adipose and muscle tissue, preventing hydrolysis of VLDL triglycerides and secondarily elevating LDL cholesterol through metabolic conversion pathways."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormone normally increases cholesterol secretion into bile and upregulates hepatic LDL receptor expression, both of which clear cholesterol from plasma. When thyroid hormone is deficient, these clearance mechanisms are diminished, causing plasma cholesterol, phospholipids, and triglycerides to accumulate. This hypercholesterolemia is strongly associated with accelerated atherosclerosis and coronary artery disease in hypothyroid patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroidism causes hypercholesterolemia via decreased hepatic LDL receptors and reduced bile cholesterol excretion. This is a classic cause of secondary dyslipidemia. COMLEX: Always evaluate thyroid function before attributing hypercholesterolemia to primary hyperlipidemia; treating hypothyroidism alone can normalize cholesterol without statins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone and thyroid hormone both increase protein synthesis and fat utilization; comparing their metabolic profiles clarifies how different hormone classes regulate whole-body energy balance.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following pairings of neurological manifestations most accurately contrasts hyperthyroidism with hypothyroidism?","choices":{"B":"Hyperthyroidism produces a fine, high-frequency tremor (10 to 15 per second), anxiety, insomnia, and brisk tendon reflexes; hypothyroidism produces mental sluggishness, somnolence up to 14 hours a day, and slow-relaxing reflexes.","A":"Hyperthyroidism produces a coarse resting tremor resembling Parkinson disease, bradyphrenia, and hypersomnia; hypothyroidism produces an intention tremor, emotional lability, and reduced sleep requirement from CNS stimulation.","C":"Hyperthyroidism produces seizures, decreased cerebration, and profound fatigue from cortical suppression; hypothyroidism produces psychomotor agitation, pressured speech, and reduced sleep requirement from cortical overstimulation.","D":"Hyperthyroidism produces peripheral neuropathy with distal sensory loss due to axonal protein catabolism; hypothyroidism produces central demyelination with optic neuritis and cerebellar ataxia from myelin glycoprotein deficiency.","E":"Hyperthyroidism produces delirium with visual hallucinations caused by excessive cortical firing; hypothyroidism produces focal neurological deficits from cerebellar infarction due to reduced cardiac output and hypoperfusion."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormone increases the rapidity of cerebration; hyperthyroidism causes CNS overstimulation manifesting as anxiety, nervousness, paranoia, and insomnia. The fine tremor of hyperthyroidism is a high-frequency oscillation (10-15 Hz) caused by increased synaptic reactivity in spinal cord circuits; this is distinctly different from the coarse resting tremor of Parkinson disease (3 to 6 cycles/sec in Guyton Ch 57). Hypothyroidism reduces cerebration, causing mental sluggishness and extreme somnolence. Reflexes are brisk in hyperthyroidism and characteristically slow-relaxing in hypothyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Slow-relaxing (hung-up) deep tendon reflexes are pathognomonic of hypothyroidism. Fine high-frequency tremor distinguishes hyperthyroid tremor from Parkinson (resting) and cerebellar (intention) tremors. COMLEX: Both boards test this contrast; fine tremor of hyperthyroidism is observed by placing paper on an outstretched hand.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone and thyroid hormone both increase protein synthesis and fat utilization; comparing their metabolic profiles clarifies how different hormone classes regulate whole-body energy balance.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism by which thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) stimulates TSH secretion from anterior pituitary thyrotroph cells?","choices":{"C":"TRH binds to a Gi-coupled receptor that inhibits adenylyl cyclase, reducing cAMP levels; this fall in cAMP releases an inhibitory constraint on TSH granule fusion proteins, allowing constitutive secretion to proceed.","A":"TRH crosses the lipid bilayer of thyrotroph cells and binds directly to nuclear receptors, activating the JAK-STAT5 signaling pathway to increase TSH gene transcription and subsequent secretion over a period of hours.","B":"TRH binds to a receptor tyrosine kinase on the thyrotroph surface, triggering receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation of intracellular tyrosine residues, which activates the RAS-MAPK cascade to release stored TSH granules.","D":"TRH binds its G protein-coupled receptor on thyrotrophs, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG from PIP2; IP3 releases intracellular calcium and DAG activates protein kinase C, driving TSH exocytosis.","E":"TRH is taken up into thyrotrophs by megalin-mediated endocytosis, where it allosterically activates proprotein convertases that process pro-TSH into mature TSH in the Golgi apparatus before secretion."},"explanation":"TRH is a tripeptide amide (pyroglutamyl-histidyl-proline-amide) synthesized in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus that travels via portal blood to the anterior pituitary. TRH binds a Gq-coupled receptor on thyrotroph cell membranes, activating phospholipase C. Phospholipase C cleaves PIP2 into IP3 (which releases Ca2+ from the endoplasmic reticulum) and DAG (which activates protein kinase C). This second messenger cascade drives TSH vesicle exocytosis. This is distinct from the Gs/cAMP pathway used by TSH at the thyroid gland.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TRH uses the phospholipase C (IP3/DAG/Ca2+) pathway, not cAMP. TSH uses a Gs/cAMP mechanism at the thyroid cell. Knowing which second messenger each hormone uses is a classic high-yield distinction. COMLEX: TRH is a tripeptide amide that acts rapidly and is cleared quickly, making it useful as a diagnostic TRH stimulation test to assess pituitary TSH reserve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones (TRH) drive anterior pituitary hormone (TSH) secretion which in turn regulates the thyroid target gland, exemplifying the classic three-tier hypothalamic-pituitary-target axis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":1,"stem":"TSH is administered intravenously to a patient during a diagnostic test. Of the following actions of TSH on the thyroid gland, which occurs FIRST and can be detected within 30 minutes of administration?","choices":{"D":"Increased proteolysis of thyroglobulin stored in follicular colloid by lysosomal proteases, releasing T4 and T3 directly into the circulation within approximately 30 minutes of TSH exposure.","A":"Rapid mitotic division of thyroid follicular cells transforming cuboidal epithelium into tall columnar cells with extensive apical membrane infolding, detectable within the first 30 minutes of TSH stimulation.","B":"Immediate upregulation of new thyroglobulin synthesis in the endoplasmic reticulum, producing measurable increases in thyroglobulin precursor protein within 30 minutes of TSH binding to its receptor.","C":"Rapid doubling of sodium-iodide symporter transport capacity at the basolateral membrane, causing an 8-fold increase in intracellular iodide concentration within the first 15 minutes of TSH exposure.","E":"Immediate activation of thyroid peroxidase enzyme at the apical membrane to oxidize iodide to reactive iodine, with measurable increases in organification occurring within 5 minutes of TSH administration."},"explanation":"TSH exerts multiple effects on the thyroid gland that differ in time course. The most important early effect is stimulation of proteolysis of stored thyroglobulin by lysosomal proteases, releasing T4 and T3 into the blood within 30 minutes. The thyroid stores 2-3 months of hormone in follicular colloid, and TSH rapidly mobilizes this reserve. Other TSH effects (increased NIS activity, increased thyroid peroxidase activity, increased cell size and number) require hours, days, or even weeks, as they depend on new gene transcription and protein synthesis downstream of the cAMP second messenger cascade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TSH early effect = proteolysis of thyroglobulin (within 30 min). TSH late effects = increased cell size, cell number, NIS activity, and organification (hours to weeks). This temporal hierarchy is tested in questions about why thyroid enlargement takes time to develop. COMLEX: TSH receptor is a Gs-coupled GPCR; TSH increases cAMP, activating protein kinase A which phosphorylates multiple downstream targets in the thyroid cell.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones (TRH) drive anterior pituitary hormone (TSH) secretion which in turn regulates the thyroid target gland, exemplifying the classic three-tier hypothalamic-pituitary-target axis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Other TSH effects (increased N","C":"Other TSH effects (increased N","E":"Other TSH effects (increased N"}},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":1,"stem":"In the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis, thyroid hormone negative feedback operates primarily at which anatomical site and through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"T3 and T4 act mainly at the anterior pituitary to inhibit thyrotroph TSH secretion, with a secondary effect at the hypothalamus reducing TRH; when T4 reaches about 1.75 times normal, TSH secretion falls to nearly zero.","B":"T3 and T4 act exclusively at the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus to suppress TRH gene transcription; the anterior pituitary thyrotroph cells are unable to directly sense circulating thyroid hormone levels.","C":"T3 and T4 stimulate hypothalamic somatostatin neurons to release somatostatin into the portal blood, where it travels to anterior pituitary thyrotrophs and inhibits TSH secretion by activating Gi-coupled somatostatin receptors.","D":"T3 and T4 upregulate TSH receptor expression on thyroid follicular cells, causing end-organ desensitization to TSH that reduces thyroid hormone output without any direct inhibitory effect on hypothalamic or pituitary cells.","E":"T3 and T4 activate inhibitory interneurons in the hypothalamic median eminence that physically disrupt portal blood flow from the hypothalamus, preventing TRH from reaching the anterior pituitary."},"explanation":"The primary site of negative feedback in the HPT axis is the anterior pituitary itself: T3 and T4 directly inhibit thyrotroph cell TSH secretion. This is demonstrated by the fact that the feedback effect persists even after the hypothalamus has been separated from the pituitary. A secondary feedback effect also suppresses hypothalamic TRH synthesis and release. When circulating thyroid hormone rises to about 1.75 times normal, TSH secretion falls essentially to zero. This dual-level feedback provides very tight control that keeps free thyroid hormone levels remarkably constant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HPT axis negative feedback: T3/T4 suppress TSH (primary) and TRH (secondary). The pituitary is the dominant feedback site. This explains why Graves disease (high T4) shows near-zero TSH. COMLEX: Elevated TSH with low T4 = primary hypothyroidism; low TSH with low T4 = secondary or tertiary hypothyroidism; these patterns guide TRH stimulation testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones (TRH) drive anterior pituitary hormone (TSH) secretion which in turn regulates the thyroid target gland, exemplifying the classic three-tier hypothalamic-pituitary-target axis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"This is demonstrated by the fa"}},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with Graves disease develops progressive bilateral protrusion of the eyeballs with retraction of the superior eyelids. She reports that her eyelids do not fully close when she sleeps, causing eye irritation and tearing. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of her exophthalmos?","choices":{"E":"Immunoglobulins reactive with extraocular muscle proteins accumulate in the retro-orbital space and cause edematous swelling and degenerative changes in the extraocular muscles; this autoimmune process correlates with thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin levels and is usually ameliorated when hyperthyroidism is treated.","A":"TSH directly stimulates retro-orbital adipocyte proliferation through TSH receptors in orbital fat tissue, and the accumulating adipose tissue mechanically displaces the eyeball anteriorly independent of any autoimmune mechanism.","B":"Sympathetic nervous system overstimulation from excess thyroid hormone tonically contracts the superior tarsal muscle and Muller muscle, pulling the upper eyelid superiorly and mechanically stretching the orbit over time.","C":"Increased cardiac output in Graves disease causes sustained venous hypertension in the ophthalmic veins, leading to transudation of fluid into the retro-orbital space that passively pushes the globe forward.","D":"Excess T3 upregulates connective tissue growth factor in orbital fibroblasts through thyroid hormone receptor activation, stimulating type I collagen deposition in the retro-orbital space that physically displaces the eyeball."},"explanation":"Exophthalmos in Graves disease is caused by edematous swelling of retro-orbital tissues and degenerative changes in the extraocular muscles due to immunoglobulins that react with eye muscle antigens. Like the thyroid gland itself, the orbit is targeted by the same autoimmune process. The concentration of these immunoglobulins is highest in patients with elevated TSI levels, confirming the autoimmune etiology. Exophthalmos can persist even after thyroid treatment and is usually ameliorated when the hyperthyroidism is controlled. Lid retraction is a separate finding caused by sympathetic stimulation of the superior tarsal muscle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exophthalmos in Graves = retro-orbital autoimmune infiltration with immunoglobulins reactive to extraocular muscles. TSI levels correlate with severity. Proptosis can stretch the optic nerve causing vision loss. COMLEX: Exophthalmos is a CP3 physical examination finding for thyroid disorders; lid lag and lid retraction are assessed separately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Thyroid storm and myxedema follow the same excess-deficiency clinical framework used for GH disorders, reinforcing recognition of hormone-specific high-output and low-output syndromes.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Lid retraction is a separate f"}},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with longstanding untreated hypothyroidism presents with a doughy, nonpitting swelling of the face, tongue, and extremities. His TSH is critically elevated and free T4 is undetectable. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of his nonpitting edema?","choices":{"C":"Hyaluronic acid and chondroitin sulfate bound to protein accumulate in the interstitium, forming a hydrophilic gel that traps fluid and produces nonpitting edema because the gel is not displaced by pressure.","A":"Severely reduced cardiac output in this patient decreases venous return from peripheral tissues, raising hydrostatic capillary pressure and causing protein-rich fluid to transude from the intravascular compartment into peripheral interstitial spaces.","B":"Decreased hepatic albumin synthesis resulting from thyroid hormone deficiency reduces plasma oncotic pressure below 15 mmHg, causing net fluid movement from the vasculature into the interstitium throughout the body.","D":"Elevated TSH directly stimulates glycosaminoglycan synthesis in dermal fibroblasts through fibroblast TSH receptor signaling, depositing mucopolysaccharides in the dermis independent of thyroid hormone deficiency.","E":"Hypothyroidism impairs lymphatic smooth muscle Na+/K+-ATPase activity, reducing intrinsic lymphatic contractility and preventing normal lymph drainage from peripheral tissues, causing lymphedema."},"explanation":"Myxedema is the term for severe hypothyroidism with characteristic nonpitting edema. The mechanism is accumulation of hyaluronic acid and chondroitin sulfate bound to protein in the interstitial spaces. These large hydrophilic glycosaminoglycan molecules trap water and form an immobile gel, causing total interstitial fluid volume to increase. Because the gel is essentially immobile, finger pressure does not displace it, producing the characteristic nonpitting quality. This is distinct from pitting edema of heart failure or hypoalbuminemia, where interstitial fluid is freely mobile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myxedema = nonpitting edema from glycosaminoglycan (hyaluronic acid, chondroitin sulfate) accumulation. Contrasts with pitting edema from heart failure or hypoalbuminemia. COMLEX: Myxedema coma includes hypothermia, bradycardia, and altered mental status; requires urgent T3 and T4 replacement plus supportive care including corticosteroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Thyroid storm and myxedema follow the same excess-deficiency clinical framework used for GH disorders, reinforcing recognition of hormone-specific high-output and low-output syndromes.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"This is distinct from pitting ","B":"This is distinct from pitting "}},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":1,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with Graves disease has a serum TSH of less than 0.01 mIU/L (reference 0.45-4.12) with markedly elevated free T4 and free T3. Which of the following best explains why her TSH is nearly undetectable?","choices":{"B":"TSI directly bind TSH receptors on anterior pituitary thyrotroph cells and allosterically block the TRH receptor, preventing TRH from stimulating TSH synthesis and release from the pituitary gland.","A":"Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) continuously activate cAMP signaling through TSH receptors on follicular cells, and the resulting high T3 and T4 suppress pituitary thyrotroph TSH secretion to near zero by negative feedback.","C":"Elevated T3 levels in this patient stimulate hypothalamic somatostatin neurons to tonically release somatostatin into the portal blood, which travels to pituitary thyrotrophs and completely blocks their TSH secretory capacity.","D":"Autoantibodies in Graves disease target and destroy thyrotroph cells in the anterior pituitary, eliminating their structural capacity to synthesize and store TSH for secretion in response to TRH stimulation.","E":"TSI competes with endogenous TSH for binding to thyroid follicular cell receptors, reducing the effective TSH concentration at the gland and triggering a compensatory reduction in pituitary TSH production by a short-loop feedback mechanism."},"explanation":"In Graves disease, TSI are IgG autoantibodies that mimic TSH by binding the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells and persistently activating the Gs/cAMP cascade. Unlike TSH (which lasts about 1 hour), TSI stimulation lasts up to 12 hours. This chronic stimulation produces continuously elevated T3 and T4, which exert maximal negative feedback on anterior pituitary thyrotrophs, suppressing TSH secretion to near zero. A near-zero TSH with elevated free T4/T3 is the diagnostic laboratory signature of Graves disease and all other causes of primary hyperthyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TSH near-zero + elevated free T4/T3 = primary hyperthyroidism. TSI persists 12 hours vs TSH 1 hour. Serum TSH is the most sensitive test for thyroid dysfunction. COMLEX: Graves disease is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in pregnancy; preferred treatment in first trimester is PTU due to methimazole teratogenicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — Thyroid storm and myxedema follow the same excess-deficiency clinical framework used for GH disorders, reinforcing recognition of hormone-specific high-output and low-output syndromes.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman presents with fatigue, cold intolerance, and weight gain. TSH is mildly elevated at 5.8 mIU/L, free T4 is high-normal, and free T3 is low. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in intracellular type 2 deiodinase. Which of the following best explains the molecular basis of her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Without functional type 2 deiodinase, T4 cannot be converted to T3 in target cells, so T3-preferring nuclear receptors are underactivated despite normal T4, and TSH rises because thyrotrophs also rely on D2 to sense T4.","B":"The mutation prevents T4 from binding to thyroxine-binding globulin in the plasma, reducing total T4 transport to peripheral tissues while free T4 remains elevated at toxic levels inside target cells causing receptor downregulation.","C":"Loss of type 2 deiodinase eliminates the only pathway for TSH synthesis in anterior pituitary thyrotrophs, reducing TSH-driven thyroid stimulation and explaining her hypothyroid symptoms from reduced thyroid hormone output.","D":"Type 2 deiodinase normally inactivates T3 to reverse T3 at the cell membrane; loss of this enzyme causes T3 to accumulate to toxic levels that paradoxically downregulate thyroid hormone receptors in peripheral tissues.","E":"The mutation prevents deiodination of T3 to T2 in peripheral tissues, causing T3 to accumulate in the nucleus and saturate thyroid hormone receptors, triggering receptor internalization and transcriptional silencing."},"explanation":"T4 is the primary secretory product of the thyroid gland (93% of secretion), but it is a prohormone. Intracellular type 2 deiodinase converts T4 to T3 within target cells; because more than 90% of thyroid hormone that binds nuclear receptors is T3, this conversion step is essential. Without it, T4 cannot generate sufficient intracellular T3 to activate the THR-RXR heterodimer at thyroid hormone response elements. The result is tissue-level hypothyroidism despite a normal-to-high circulating T4. Because pituitary thyrotrophs also use D2 to convert T4 to T3 when sensing feedback, D2 loss blunts T4 feedback, so TSH rises even though circulating T4 is high-normal, a pattern standard labs can easily misread. D2 does not govern TBG binding or TSH synthesis, and it activates T4 rather than inactivating T3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type 2 deiodinase converts T4 to T3 intracellularly; >90% of nuclear receptor-bound thyroid hormone is T3. Selenium deficiency reduces deiodinase activity. COMLEX: Some patients on levothyroxine have residual symptoms despite normal TSH and T4 due to suboptimal T4-to-T3 conversion; adding low-dose liothyronine (T3) is a proposed treatment strategy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Release and Transport of Thyroid Hormones — peripheral deiodination of T4 to T3 accounts for approximately 80% of circulating T3; this step is entirely dependent on tissue deiodinase activity and is the rate-limiting step for intracellular thyroid hormone action.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"D2 does not govern TBG binding","C":"D2 does not govern TBG binding","D":"D2 does not govern TBG binding"}},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with a large toxic thyroid adenoma is admitted with palpitations and dyspnea. His ECG shows atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular rate; free T4 is 4.2 ng/dL (reference 0.8-1.4). His cardiologist notes that some cardiac effects of thyroid hormone occur within minutes of hormone exposure, too rapidly to be explained by gene transcription and protein synthesis. Which mechanism best accounts for these rapid cardiac effects?","choices":{"E":"Nongenomic thyroid hormone actions at the plasma membrane and cytoplasm regulate ion channels and activate second messengers such as cAMP and protein kinases, rapidly changing pacemaker activity and contractility without new protein synthesis.","A":"T3 rapidly enters cardiac nuclei and activates a subset of thyroid hormone response elements with unusually fast transcription rates, producing new ion channel proteins within minutes through an accelerated ribosomal translation mechanism unique to cardiomyocytes.","B":"T4 at high plasma concentrations directly binds and activates beta-1 adrenergic receptors on cardiac pacemaker cells without requiring conversion to T3, mimicking catecholamine signaling to increase heart rate immediately.","C":"High serum T4 levels stimulate immediate catecholamine release from adrenal chromaffin cells by activating thyroid hormone receptors on adrenal medullary cells, and this catecholamine surge produces the rapid cardiac effects.","D":"Excess circulating T4 directly inhibits phosphodiesterase in cardiac pacemaker cells, preventing cAMP breakdown and maintaining persistent PKA activation that drives chronotropic and dromotropic effects within minutes of T4 exposure."},"explanation":"In addition to their genomic actions (which take hours to days), thyroid hormones have nongenomic effects that occur within minutes. These effects are independent of gene transcription and translation and are not blocked by transcription inhibitors. The sites of nongenomic action include the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and organelles such as mitochondria. Nongenomic effects include regulation of ion channel conductance (including K+ and Na+ channels in cardiomyocytes), activation of intracellular cAMP generation, and protein kinase signaling cascades. These rapid effects on cardiac automaticity and conduction contribute to the increased heart rate and susceptibility to arrhythmias seen in hyperthyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyroid hormone has both genomic (slow, hours-days, nuclear receptor) and nongenomic (rapid, minutes, plasma membrane/cytoplasm) effects. COMLEX: Beta-blockers (propranolol) are used acutely in thyroid storm to block the adrenergic amplification of thyroid hormone cardiac effects; they do not lower thyroid hormone levels but rapidly control heart rate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — G Protein-Coupled Receptor Signaling and Second Messengers — nongenomic thyroid hormone actions converge on the same cAMP and protein kinase A cascades used by classical GPCR hormones, explaining the overlapping cardiovascular presentations of hyperthyroidism and adrenergic excess.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain thyroid hormone’s mechanism of action via nuclear receptors leading to increased gene transcription and increased metabolic rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with newly diagnosed Hashimoto hypothyroidism takes part in a physiology study and receives a single large injected dose of thyroxine (T4). Her plasma T4 rises promptly, but her basal metabolic rate shows essentially no change for 2 to 3 days and then rises progressively to a maximum at 10 to 12 days. Which pharmacokinetic and physiological property of thyroxine best explains why the metabolic effect lags behind the rise in plasma T4?","choices":{"D":"T4 has a plasma half-life of about 6 days from extensive TBG binding, and conversion to T3, gene transcription, and new protein synthesis add further lag, so the metabolic effect of one dose peaks only 10 to 12 days later.","A":"Levothyroxine must first accumulate in the thyroid follicular colloid before being secreted into the blood as T3; because thyroid stores are depleted in Hashimoto thyroiditis, this colloid reloading phase takes 1 to 2 weeks.","B":"T4 requires hydroxylation by hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes before it can bind thyroid hormone receptors; saturation of these enzymes with the new oral dose creates a 1 to 2 week delay before peripheral tissues can respond.","C":"The pituitary TSH must first fall to the normal range before peripheral thyroid hormone receptors are upregulated by receptor dephosphorylation, and this receptor activation process takes 1 to 2 weeks to produce measurable metabolic changes.","E":"Levothyroxine undergoes enterohepatic recycling via gut bacterial deconjugation; dysbiosis in autoimmune thyroid disease delays this reabsorption step by 1 to 2 weeks before steady-state plasma levels are achieved."},"explanation":"T4 (thyroxine) has a long plasma half-life (half released to tissues about every 6 days in Guyton Ch 77; half-life 7 days in Katzung Ch 38) because more than 99% is bound to plasma proteins, predominantly TBG, which slows distribution and clearance. Even after plasma T4 rises, metabolic effects are delayed because: T4 must be converted to T3 by deiodinase; T3 must bind nuclear receptors and activate gene transcription; new proteins must be synthesized and accumulate; and existing dysfunctional proteins must be turned over. After a single large injected dose of thyroxine, Guyton Ch 77 (course SDL 13–14 PDF p9) describes essentially no metabolic effect for 2 to 3 days, a maximum at 10 to 12 days, and some activity persisting for 6 to 8 weeks. Daily oral therapy is a different question: Katzung Ch 38 (course SDL 15 reading) gives steady-state T4 levels and maximum effect only after 6 to 8 weeks of a consistent dose, which is why TSH is rechecked at 6 to 8 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 t1/2 = ~6-7 days (extensive protein binding). Peak metabolic effect 10-12 days after a single injected dose (Guyton); on daily therapy, steady state and maximum effect take 6-8 weeks (Katzung). TSH should be rechecked 6-8 weeks after any levothyroxine dose change (Katzung Ch 38). T3 t1/2 = ~1 day; much faster onset. COMLEX: Calcium, iron, antacids, and PPIs reduce levothyroxine absorption; patients should take it on empty stomach 30-60 minutes before food or other medications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — Thyroid Hormone Transport — TBG binds more than 99% of circulating T4, creating the buffer reservoir that determines the long half-life and slow onset of T4 action; understanding protein binding explains why total T4 changes slowly compared to free T4.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with a 3-month history of heat intolerance, frequent loose stools, 15-pound unintentional weight loss despite increased appetite, and inability to sleep until 3 AM. Examination reveals HR 116 bpm, warm moist skin, fine hand tremor, and brisk deep-tendon reflexes. TSH is 0.02 mIU/L and free T4 is 4.0 ng/dL. Which of the following best explains the unified mechanism connecting her elevated thyroid hormones to both her cardiovascular and gastrointestinal symptoms?","choices":{"B":"Excess thyroid hormone activates nuclear receptors to upregulate metabolic enzymes and Na+/K+-ATPase throughout all body cells, increasing O2 consumption and heat production, which drives peripheral vasodilation, increases cardiac output and heart rate, increases GI smooth muscle contractility and peristalsis, and accelerates all tissue metabolic processes simultaneously.","A":"Excess thyroid hormone directly binds and activates beta-2 adrenergic receptors in the heart and GI smooth muscle, bypassing the second messenger system and producing catecholamine-independent tachycardia and diarrhea through receptor cross-activation.","C":"Thyroid hormone excess stimulates sympathetic ganglia directly through thyroid hormone response elements in sympathetic neurons, producing a generalized sympathetic surge that raises heart rate and increases GI motility through adrenergic pathways.","D":"Elevated T3 causes uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation in intestinal enterocytes, reducing ATP production and forcing enterocytes into osmotic secretory diarrhea while simultaneously depleting cardiac myocyte energy stores and causing tachycardia.","E":"High thyroid hormone suppresses TSH to near zero, and the absence of TSH removes a tonic suppressive signal on cardiac pacemaker cells and enteric neurons, releasing them from inhibition and causing tachycardia and diarrhea."},"explanation":"The diverse systemic effects of thyroid hormone all trace to a common upstream cause: activation of nuclear receptors that increase transcription of genes encoding metabolic enzymes, structural proteins, and transport proteins (especially Na+/K+-ATPase) in virtually every cell type. In the cardiovascular system, increased cellular metabolism generates vasodilatory metabolites, lowers peripheral resistance, increases venous return, and raises cardiac output; thyroid hormone also directly increases cardiac pacemaker automaticity. In the GI tract, thyroid hormone increases smooth muscle contractility and digestive juice secretion, accelerating transit and causing diarrhea in hyperthyroidism. Heat intolerance arises from excess heat production by elevated Na+/K+-ATPase and mitochondrial activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The broad systemic effects of thyroid hormone (heat intolerance, tachycardia, weight loss, diarrhea, tremor, insomnia, brisk reflexes) all stem from the same genomic mechanism: upregulation of metabolic enzymes and Na+/K+-ATPase. COMLEX: Fine tremor, heat intolerance, and weight loss with increased appetite is the classic triad of hyperthyroidism tested on COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Second Messenger Systems — thyroid hormone gene transcription produces Na+/K+-ATPase as one of its key effector proteins, directly connecting nuclear receptor signaling to the membrane ion transport that drives heat production and metabolic rate elevation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with untreated Graves disease presents for a pre-surgical evaluation. His blood pressure is 154/66 mmHg on repeated measurements. He reports palpitations and is mildly short of breath at rest. His resting heart rate is 110 bpm. Which hemodynamic mechanism best explains his specific blood pressure pattern?","choices":{"C":"Excess thyroid hormone increases tissue metabolism, causing peripheral vasodilation and lower diastolic pressure; increased cardiac output and heart rate raise systolic pressure by 10 to 15 mmHg, widening the pulse pressure while mean arterial pressure stays near normal.","A":"Excess thyroid hormone directly activates renin secretion from juxtaglomerular cells by binding thyroid hormone receptors in the kidney, triggering the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone cascade that selectively raises diastolic pressure through aldosterone-mediated sodium retention.","B":"Thyroid hormone stimulates adrenal medullary catecholamine secretion by activating thyroid hormone receptors on chromaffin cells, causing both systolic and diastolic blood pressure to rise proportionally through alpha-1 adrenergic vasoconstriction.","D":"Elevated thyroid hormone causes ventricular hypertrophy that increases stroke volume without changing heart rate; this high stroke volume selectively raises systolic pressure while diastolic pressure is maintained by compensatory increase in total peripheral resistance.","E":"Increased cardiac output in hyperthyroidism raises hydrostatic pressure throughout the arterial tree proportionally, elevating both systolic and diastolic components equally while the pulse pressure remains unchanged from baseline."},"explanation":"In hyperthyroidism, increased tissue metabolism generates vasodilatory metabolites in peripheral tissues, reducing total peripheral resistance and lowering diastolic blood pressure. Simultaneously, the elevated metabolic rate increases O2 consumption and CO2 production, leading to increased venous return and cardiac output, which raises systolic pressure. The net effect is a characteristically widened pulse pressure with the systolic pressure elevated by approximately 10-15 mmHg and diastolic pressure reduced by a corresponding amount. Mean arterial pressure remains approximately normal. This widened pulse pressure pattern is a distinctive cardiovascular sign of hyperthyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperthyroidism = widened pulse pressure (systolic up, diastolic down, mean AP normal). This is opposite to aortic stenosis (narrow pulse pressure). COMLEX: Checking for wide pulse pressure is part of the cardiovascular exam in suspected hyperthyroidism and helps distinguish thyroid-mediated tachycardia from primary cardiac arrhythmia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth Hormone Metabolic Effects — both GH and thyroid hormone increase protein synthesis and cellular metabolism; unlike GH which raises mean arterial pressure, thyroid hormone maintains normal MAP by balancing increased cardiac output against decreased peripheral resistance through metabolic vasodilation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"The net effect is a characteri","E":"The net effect is a characteri"}},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the major physiological effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism, cardiovascular system, and nervous system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with a 12-year history of untreated primary hypothyroidism presents with exertional chest pain and dyspnea. Echocardiogram reveals cardiac output of 2.9 L/min (normal about 5 L/min) and heart rate of 48 bpm. Lipid panel shows total cholesterol 364 mg/dL and LDL 290 mg/dL. Which mechanism most directly links her thyroid hormone deficiency to both her cardiac findings and her lipid abnormalities?","choices":{"D":"Absent thyroid hormone fails to upregulate metabolic enzymes, Na+/K+-ATPase, and mitochondrial biogenesis; lower O2 consumption reduces the vasodilatory stimulus, cardiac output, and heart rate, while reduced hepatic LDL receptor expression and bile cholesterol secretion cause hypercholesterolemia.","A":"Elevated TSH in primary hypothyroidism activates TSH receptors on cardiac myocytes and hepatocytes, directly suppressing cardiac contractility through Gi-mediated cAMP reduction and upregulating HMG-CoA reductase through a TSH-specific hepatic second messenger pathway.","B":"Myxedematous edema fluid accumulates in the pericardial space, compressing the cardiac chambers and reducing stroke volume by a restrictive physiology, while glycosaminoglycans released from pericardial fluid activate hepatic cholesterol synthesis through a TLR4-dependent inflammatory mechanism.","C":"Severely reduced basal metabolic rate causes skeletal muscle catabolism that releases amino acids into the circulation; the liver converts excess amino acid substrates into cholesterol via de novo lipogenesis while reduced peripheral O2 demand causes cardiac output to fall reflexively.","E":"Hypothyroid patients develop anemia from reduced erythropoietin synthesis, and the resulting tissue hypoxia triggers compensatory cardiac hypertrophy that paradoxically reduces cardiac output; hypoxia also upregulates PCSK9 expression in the liver, reducing LDL receptor recycling and elevating LDL."},"explanation":"Thyroid hormone normally activates nuclear transcription of genes encoding metabolic enzymes, Na+/K+-ATPase, and mitochondrial proteins in every cell including cardiomyocytes. Without thyroid hormone, cellular metabolism falls, tissue O2 demand decreases, vasodilatory signals diminish, total peripheral resistance rises, venous return decreases, and cardiac output can fall to 50% of normal. Heart rate slows because thyroid hormone has a direct excitatory effect on cardiac pacemaker cells. Simultaneously, reduced thyroid hormone decreases hepatic LDL receptor expression and bile cholesterol secretion, causing cholesterol to accumulate in plasma and drive atherosclerosis, explaining the exertional chest pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroidism = low-output state + hypercholesterolemia + atherosclerosis. Cardiac output can fall to 50% of normal. Hypothyroid cardiomyopathy is reversible with levothyroxine. COMLEX: Diastolic dysfunction is common in hypothyroidism; testing TSH is recommended in all new heart failure evaluations before attributing symptoms to primary cardiac disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — Aldosterone and Renal Effects — thyroid hormone deficiency reduces cardiac output and renal perfusion, which can secondarily activate RAAS; both aldosterone excess and thyroid deficiency impair cardiac performance through convergent but mechanistically distinct pathways.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old research assistant spends 8 weeks at an Antarctic field station. At week 6, her TSH is 6.8 mIU/L (reference 0.45-4.12) and free T4 is 1.3 ng/dL (reference 0.8-1.4). She feels well with normal activity level and no symptoms of thyroid dysfunction. Which physiological pathway best explains her elevated TSH?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged cold exposure activates hypothalamic thermoregulatory centers, increasing TRH secretion from the paraventricular nucleus into portal blood, which drives anterior pituitary TSH release and thyroid secretion of T3 and T4 to raise basal metabolic rate.","B":"Cold exposure activates the sympathetic nervous system, which directly stimulates anterior pituitary thyrotrophs through norepinephrine binding to beta-2 adrenergic receptors, increasing TSH synthesis and secretion independent of any hypothalamic TRH signal.","C":"Cold-induced peripheral vasoconstriction reduces thyroid gland blood flow, decreasing the delivery of iodide and TSH to the thyroid; the feedback system detects reduced thyroid hormone output and reflexively increases TSH from the anterior pituitary to compensate.","D":"Low environmental temperature reduces the rate of T4-to-T3 conversion by peripheral deiodinase enzymes; the resulting lower free T3 reduces negative feedback on the pituitary, causing TSH to rise to restore T3 levels to normal.","E":"Shivering thermogenesis in cold environments consumes large amounts of T3 at the skeletal muscle level, depleting circulating T3 below the normal range; this T3 deficit removes inhibitory feedback on the anterior pituitary, causing compensatory TSH elevation."},"explanation":"Cold exposure is one of the most potent physiological stimuli for the HPT axis. Cold activates hypothalamic temperature control centers that project to TRH neurons in the paraventricular nucleus, increasing TRH synthesis and secretion into the portal blood. Elevated TRH then drives anterior pituitary TSH secretion, which stimulates the thyroid to increase T3 and T4 production. The elevated thyroid hormones increase basal metabolic rate and thermogenesis to counteract heat loss. Rats exposed to severe cold for several weeks raise their basal metabolic rate as much as 50%, and people moving to Arctic regions have basal metabolic rates 15% to 20% above normal (Guyton Ch 77).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cold exposure is a TRH stimulus through hypothalamic thermoregulatory pathways. This explains why patients in cold environments may have transiently elevated TSH values without hypothyroidism. COMLEX: In this Antarctic researcher, free T4 is at the upper limit of normal confirming the gland is responding appropriately; this is a physiological adaptation, not disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hypothalamic Control of Anterior Pituitary — TRH from the paraventricular nucleus travels via the median eminence portal blood to anterior pituitary thyrotrophs; cold is the most studied neurogenic stimulus that drives this axis upward to meet increased thermogenic demand.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old male competitive cyclist undergoes a 5-day caloric restriction protocol for racing weight reduction. By day 5, serum leptin is markedly low, TSH is 0.8 mIU/L (low-normal), free T4 is normal, and free T3 is below the reference range. He feels fatigued and cold. Which central mechanism best explains his suppressed thyroid hormone axis?","choices":{"B":"Low leptin reduces POMC neuron activity and increases NPY/AGRP activity in the arcuate nucleus; these circuits converge on paraventricular TRH neurons, lowering TRH and TSH and reducing T4-to-T3 conversion to conserve energy.","A":"Caloric restriction depletes hepatic glycogen stores required as a cofactor for type 2 deiodinase enzyme activity, directly impairing peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion without any change in the hypothalamic TRH neuron firing rate.","C":"Low leptin directly binds TSH receptors on thyroid follicular cells and acts as a competitive antagonist, reducing TSH-stimulated thyroid hormone synthesis independent of any central hypothalamic or pituitary effect.","D":"Ketone bodies produced during caloric restriction bind to thyroid hormone receptors in the anterior pituitary and displace T3, blocking negative feedback and paradoxically reducing TSH secretion through a false saturation mechanism.","E":"Caloric restriction increases cortisol secretion from the adrenal glands through HPA axis activation, and elevated cortisol directly suppresses TRH gene transcription in the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus to reduce thyroid axis activity."},"explanation":"Leptin is an adipokine that signals nutritional status to the hypothalamus. During caloric restriction, leptin falls, which has two convergent effects on TRH-producing neurons in the paraventricular nucleus: reduced leptin decreases anorexigenic POMC neuron activity (which normally stimulates TRH neurons), and increased NPY/AGRP activity directly inhibits TRH neurons. The net result is reduced TRH secretion, reduced pituitary TSH secretion, and decreased thyroid hormone production. Additionally, type 1 deiodinase (which converts T4 to T3 peripherally) is also reduced during caloric restriction. This constellation reduces T3 and metabolic rate, conserving energy during food scarcity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leptin links energy stores to the HPT axis. Fasting lowers leptin, lowers TRH, lowers TSH, and lowers T3 (euthyroid sick syndrome or nonthyroidal illness pattern). COMLEX: This energy conservation response explains why severely malnourished patients have low T3 and resting bradycardia; treatment is refeeding, not levothyroxine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — JAK-STAT Signaling via Leptin Receptor — leptin receptor on hypothalamic POMC/AGRP neurons uses JAK2-STAT3 signaling to regulate neuropeptide expression; when leptin falls, this pathway reduces POMC and permits NPY/AGRP to suppress TRH neurons and downregulate the entire HPT axis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis including the roles of TRH and TSH and negative feedback regulation by T3 and T4.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with infertility and menstrual irregularities is found to have a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma. Her TSH is 52 mIU/L, free T4 is 4.8 ng/dL, and free T3 is critically elevated. Thyroid ultrasound shows the gland is nearly twice its normal volume. Which of the following best explains why her thyroid gland is both oversecreting hormones AND physically enlarged?","choices":{"E":"TSH binds Gs-coupled receptors on follicular cells, raising cAMP and PKA activity, which drives both acute hormone release (thyroglobulin proteolysis) and trophic growth (larger, more numerous, columnar cells), causing hypersecretion and goiter.","A":"High TSH directly activates thyroid follicular cell mTOR signaling through a cAMP-independent pathway, selectively driving ribosomal biogenesis and cell hypertrophy without affecting thyroid hormone synthesis or secretion rate.","B":"Excess T4 and T3 from the overactive thyroid gland feed back positively onto TSH receptors in the thyroid, activating an autocrine growth loop in follicular cells that causes both hypersecretion and progressive glandular enlargement.","C":"High circulating TSH activates insulin-like growth factor-1 receptors on thyroid cells through molecular mimicry of IGF-1 structure, and this receptor cross-activation drives thyroid cell proliferation and hormone secretion by IGF-1 downstream pathways.","D":"Elevated TSH stimulates calcitonin-producing C cells to secrete calcitonin in large quantities, and calcitonin acts as a thyroid-specific growth factor that stimulates follicular cell hyperplasia alongside TSH-driven hormone hypersecretion."},"explanation":"TSH binds a Gs protein-coupled receptor on the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells, activating adenylyl cyclase to produce cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates multiple downstream effectors. Early effects (within 30 minutes) include increased proteolysis of thyroglobulin with T3/T4 release. Long-term effects (hours to days) include: increased NIS activity, increased iodide organification, increased cell size, transformation from cuboidal to columnar cells, increased epithelial infolding into follicles, and increased thyroid cell number. All of these trophic effects together explain the enlarged thyroid gland (goiter) seen whenever TSH is chronically elevated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TSH receptor uses Gs/cAMP mechanism. TSH is both a secretagogue (short-term, proteolysis) AND a trophic factor (long-term, cell growth). Chronically elevated TSH causes goiter regardless of cause. COMLEX: Distinguishing TSH-secreting adenoma (high TSH + high T4 = central hyperthyroidism) from primary hyperthyroidism (low TSH + high T4) is critical for correct diagnosis and treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior Pituitary Hormones and Cell Types — TSH is produced by thyrotroph cells and is the only anterior pituitary hormone that is both a secretagogue and a trophic factor for its target gland; the trophic effect accounts for goiter in all causes of TSH excess including primary hypothyroidism and TSH-producing adenomas.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with poorly controlled Graves disease undergoes emergency laparoscopic appendectomy. Six hours postoperatively she develops fever to 40.8 degrees C, heart rate of 152 bpm, agitation, confusion, and vomiting. Her free T4 is 6.2 ng/dL and free T3 is critically elevated. The thyroid storm management protocol includes high-dose intravenous iodide after thionamides. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which high-dose iodide acutely reduces thyroid hormone release?","choices":{"B":"High concentrations of inorganic iodide reduce iodide trapping and organification (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect) and, most importantly, paralyze the endocytosis of colloid by thyroid glandular cells, rapidly shutting down thyroid hormone secretion into the blood.","A":"High-dose iodide directly inactivates thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins by iodinating their antigen-binding regions, eliminating the autoimmune drive for thyroid hormone overproduction and allowing TSH-based negative feedback to normalize thyroid output.","C":"Excess iodide competitively inhibits thyroxine-binding globulin in the plasma, displacing T4 and T3 from TBG and causing rapid renal filtration and urinary excretion of unbound thyroid hormones within the first few hours of administration.","D":"High iodide concentrations activate Gi-coupled iodide-sensing receptors on thyroid follicular cells that inhibit adenylyl cyclase and reduce cAMP, blocking the TSH-mediated secretory pathway and causing immediate retention of thyroid hormones within thyroglobulin.","E":"Iodide loading rapidly saturates the pendrin antiporter on the apical membrane of thyroid follicular cells, preventing all iodide efflux into the follicular lumen and halting new thyroid hormone synthesis without affecting existing stored hormone secretion."},"explanation":"When iodides are present in blood at approximately 100 times the normal plasma level, the rate of iodide trapping and iodination of tyrosine (organification) decreases (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect), and, most critically, the normal endocytosis of colloid droplets from the follicular lumen by thyroid glandular cells is paralyzed. Because endocytosis is the first step in releasing thyroid hormones from stored thyroglobulin, its inhibition rapidly shuts down T3 and T4 secretion into the blood. This is why high-dose iodide is administered in thyroid storm and preoperatively before thyroidectomy. The effect lasts only days to a few weeks before the gland escapes by downregulating NIS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-dose iodide blocks organification (Wolff-Chaikoff effect) and, acutely, inhibits colloid endocytosis and hormone release. Used preoperatively and in thyroid storm. The gland escapes after days-weeks by downregulating NIS. COMLEX: Thyroid storm management sequence: propylthiouracil or methimazole first (blocks synthesis), then iodide (given AFTER thionamide so iodide is not used to make new hormone), plus beta-blockers and steroids.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — Thyroid Hormone Formation via Thyroid Peroxidase — organification is the step blocked by the Wolff-Chaikoff effect; high iodide concentrations suppress the peroxidase-mediated iodination of tyrosine residues within thyroglobulin that is required for T3 and T4 formation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn screening program identifies an infant with undetectable free T4 and TSH of 210 mIU/L on day 2 of life. The infant appears clinically normal with appropriate reflexes and muscle tone. The neonatologist initiates levothyroxine replacement immediately and counsels the family that without prompt treatment the consequences are permanent and irreversible. Which of the following best explains why treatment within the first few weeks of life is critical to prevent permanent disability?","choices":{"D":"Skeletal growth plates require thyroid hormone for normal enchondral ossification; if untreated beyond the first month, premature bilateral growth plate fusion occurs and the resulting short stature cannot be corrected by later hormonal supplementation.","A":"Congenital hypothyroidism causes immediate cardiac dysfunction from bradycardia and reduced cardiac output; if uncorrected for more than a few weeks, the myocardium undergoes irreversible fibrotic remodeling that persists despite subsequent thyroid hormone normalization.","B":"Thyroid hormone is required for pancreatic beta-cell maturation; prolonged neonatal hypothyroidism causes permanent loss of beta-cell mass and lifelong insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus that cannot be reversed by thyroid replacement therapy.","C":"Thyroid hormone is essential for normal neuronal growth, dendritic and axonal branching, and myelination of central nervous system neurons during a critical perinatal developmental window; if hypothyroidism is not corrected within the first few weeks of life, retardation of brain growth and myelination causes permanent intellectual disability that cannot be reversed by later thyroid replacement.","E":"Neonatal hypothyroidism causes irreversible adrenal cortex atrophy from reduced ACTH secretion within the first weeks of life, producing permanent glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid deficiency that requires lifelong steroid replacement in addition to thyroid treatment."},"explanation":"Congenital hypothyroidism carries the risk of permanent intellectual disability if not treated promptly. Thyroid hormone is critical for neuronal dendrite and axon growth, and for myelination of CNS neurons during a critical perinatal developmental window. If hypothyroidism is corrected within a few weeks of birth, normal physical growth is restored and brain development can proceed. However, if hypothyroidism persists beyond this critical window, the growth, branching, and myelination of CNS neurons are permanently retarded, and the resulting intellectual disability cannot be reversed by later thyroid treatment. This is why universal newborn screening for congenital hypothyroidism exists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cretinism = congenital hypothyroidism; irreversible intellectual disability if untreated. Physical signs (short stature, stocky habitus, tongue enlargement) can be corrected with treatment, but neurological damage is permanent if treatment is delayed beyond the first few weeks. COMLEX: Maternal hypothyroidism during the first trimester also impairs fetal brain development; optimal maternal thyroid function is critical throughout pregnancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Clinical Features of GH Deficiency — both GH deficiency and thyroid hormone deficiency in childhood cause growth failure; however, only thyroid hormone deficiency causes irreversible neurological impairment because thyroid hormone is uniquely required for CNS myelination during the critical perinatal window.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the common manifestations and mechanisms of over or under exposure to thyroid hormones such as thyroid storm, exophthalmos, and myxedema.","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old woman from a landlocked mountainous region with no access to iodized salt presents with a markedly enlarged, diffusely nontender thyroid gland. She reports fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance, and weight gain. Her TSH is 32 mIU/L, free T4 is undetectable, and radioactive iodine uptake is markedly elevated at 90% (normal 5-35%). Which pathophysiological sequence best explains the development of her thyroid enlargement?","choices":{"C":"Dietary iodine deficiency prevents thyroid hormone synthesis; absent T3 and T4 eliminate negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, causing persistent TRH and TSH hypersecretion; chronically elevated TSH drives follicular cell hypertrophy, columnar transformation, epithelial infolding, and increased cell number, with excessive thyroglobulin colloid accumulation enlarging the gland up to 10 to 20 times its normal size.","A":"Iodine deficiency causes thyroid follicular cells to undergo oncogenic transformation from chronic oxidative stress; these transformed cells proliferate autonomously independent of TSH stimulation, producing a multinodular goiter that is intrinsically independent of hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis control.","B":"Low iodine intake stimulates hypothalamic leptin receptors to increase TRH synthesis; elevated TRH then directly binds TSH receptors on thyroid follicular cells through structural homology between TRH and TSH, driving glandular hypertrophy independent of pituitary TSH secretion.","D":"Dietary iodine deficiency reduces intracellular iodide levels in thyroid cells, activating a compensatory AMPK-mediated energy-sensing pathway that upregulates cell cycle proteins and drives follicular cell hyperplasia to maximize available iodide uptake surface area.","E":"Iodine deficiency increases pendrin antiporter expression on the apical membrane to concentrate available iodide in the follicular lumen; the resulting osmotic effect of excess pendrin draws water into the follicle, physically expanding colloid volume and enlarging the gland through a hydrostatic mechanism."},"explanation":"Endemic goiter is the classic consequence of dietary iodine deficiency. Without adequate iodine, thyroid hormone synthesis fails, T3 and T4 levels fall, and negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary is removed. The result is persistent TRH and TSH hypersecretion. Chronically elevated TSH exerts its trophic effects on thyroid follicular cells: cells increase in size, transform from cuboidal to columnar, fold extensively into the follicles, and increase in number. Thyroglobulin colloid accumulates in the follicles but cannot be iodinated, so the follicles enlarge. The gland can grow to 10-20 times its normal size. The elevated radioactive iodine uptake reflects the overactive iodide pump driven by chronic TSH stimulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Endemic goiter = iodine deficiency + elevated TSH from absent negative feedback + follicular hyperplasia. The gland enlarges from TSH trophic effect, not autonomous growth. COMLEX: Iodized salt programs prevent endemic goiter in landlocked regions; worldwide, iodine deficiency remains the most common preventable cause of intellectual disability (cretinism) and the most common cause of goiter globally.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Iodide Trapping via the Sodium-Iodide Symporter — the same NIS upregulation responsible for iodide trapping in normal physiology becomes massively amplified by chronic TSH stimulation in iodine deficiency, explaining the markedly elevated radioactive iodine uptake seen in endemic goiter.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme do methimazole and propylthiouracil primarily inhibit to reduce thyroid hormone synthesis?","choices":{"E":"Thyroid peroxidase, which catalyzes organification of iodide onto tyrosine residues and the coupling of iodotyrosines to form T3 and T4","A":"Sodium-iodide symporter, which concentrates iodide from the bloodstream into thyroid follicular cells against an electrochemical gradient","B":"TSH receptor kinase, which phosphorylates downstream targets after thyrotropin binds to follicular cell surface receptors","C":"Type 1 deiodinase, which converts circulating T4 into the biologically active T3 in peripheral tissues such as the liver and kidney","D":"Adenylyl cyclase, which generates cyclic AMP as a second messenger downstream of Gs-coupled receptor activation"},"explanation":"Both methimazole and PTU inhibit thyroid peroxidase, the enzyme responsible for oxidizing iodide, incorporating it onto tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin (organification), and coupling monoiodotyrosine and diiodotyrosine to form T3 and T4. PTU additionally inhibits peripheral type 1 deiodinase. The sodium-iodide symporter, TSH receptor, and adenylyl cyclase are not the primary targets of thioamides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thioamide MOA = TPO inhibition; PTU also blocks peripheral T4→T3 conversion via type 1 deiodinase (unique to PTU). COMLEX: Both cross the placenta; PTU preferred in 1st trimester due to lower teratogenic risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"The sodium-iodide symporter, T"}},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with Graves disease is started on propranolol as adjunct therapy. Which mechanism best explains its usefulness in this setting?","choices":{"B":"Blockade of beta-adrenergic receptors, which relieves tachycardia, tremor, anxiety, and heat intolerance without changing thyroid hormone levels","A":"Inhibition of thyroid peroxidase, which reduces new thyroid hormone synthesis and gradually lowers circulating T3 and T4 concentrations over several weeks","C":"Induction of the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, which acutely suppresses both organification and thyroglobulin proteolysis within 2 to 7 days of administration","D":"Radiation-mediated destruction of thyroid follicular cells, which permanently reduces gland size and intrinsic hormone-producing capacity","E":"Competitive inhibition of the sodium-iodide symporter, which decreases iodide uptake and depletes the substrate required for thyroid hormone synthesis"},"explanation":"Beta-blockers control the adrenergic symptoms of hyperthyroidism by blocking sympathetic receptors. They do not lower T3 or T4 levels (except propranolol, which modestly inhibits T4→T3 conversion). Thyroid peroxidase inhibition is the mechanism of thioamides; the Wolff-Chaikoff effect is iodide-mediated; follicular cell destruction occurs with RAI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta-blockers do NOT lower T4/T3 (except propranolol’s minor type 1 deiodinase effect). Used for symptom control while waiting for thioamides or RAI to work. COMLEX: Key adjunct in thyroid storm alongside PTU and iodides.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Thyroid peroxidase inhibition "}},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"Radioactive iodine-131 achieves its therapeutic effect in hyperthyroidism primarily through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Beta particle emission within thyroid follicular cells destroys the parenchyma, reducing the gland's hormone output over weeks to months","B":"Competitive inhibition of thyroid peroxidase, which blocks iodination of tyrosine residues and halts new hormone synthesis within the first few days","C":"Competitive blockade of TSH receptors on follicular cell surfaces, which prevents pituitary thyrotropin from stimulating glandular growth and secretion","D":"Inhibition of thyroglobulin proteolysis, which prevents release of stored T3 and T4 from colloid into the circulation over 2 to 7 days","E":"Induction of the Wolff-Chaikoff effect through acute iodide loading, which transiently suppresses organification during the first week of therapy"},"explanation":"Iodine-131 is concentrated by the sodium-iodide symporter into follicular cells, where its beta emissions cause local radiation damage and cell death, progressively reducing thyroid mass and hormone output. This process takes weeks for onset and 3 to 6 months for maximum effect. TPO inhibition is the thioamide mechanism; TSH receptor blockade and thyroglobulin proteolysis inhibition are not mechanisms of RAI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAI = beta radiation destroys follicular cells; half-life of I-131 is 5 days. Contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding. COMLEX: Patients should be euthyroid or on beta-blockers before RAI to prevent thyroid storm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"TPO inhibition is the thioamid","C":"TPO inhibition is the thioamid"}},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to blocking thyroid peroxidase, propylthiouracil possesses a second clinically important mechanism of action. Which of the following best describes it?","choices":{"B":"Inhibition of type 1 deiodinase in peripheral tissues, which reduces conversion of less active T4 to more potent T3 and lowers active hormone faster","A":"Inhibition of the sodium-iodide symporter in follicular cells, which prevents iodide accumulation and depletes the substrate required for organification","C":"Competitive blockade of TSH receptors at the pituitary level, which disrupts the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis and reduces trophic stimulation of the gland","D":"Induction of the Wolff-Chaikoff effect through endogenous iodide accumulation, which transiently suppresses both organification and hormone release","E":"Direct destruction of hyperactive thyroid follicular cells through oxidative mechanisms, which reduces total gland mass and hormone-producing capacity"},"explanation":"PTU uniquely inhibits type 1 deiodinase in peripheral tissues such as the liver, kidney, and skeletal muscle, reducing T4→T3 conversion. This makes PTU particularly valuable in thyroid storm where rapidly lowering the most active hormone form is critical. Methimazole does not share this peripheral deiodinase inhibitory effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTU = TPO inhibition PLUS peripheral T4→T3 blockade. Methimazole = TPO inhibition only. PTU preferred in thyroid storm for dual mechanism. COMLEX: PTU also preferred in 1st trimester; both agents cross the placenta.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with Graves disease is given Lugol solution to prepare for urgent thyroidectomy. Which of the following best explains the pharmacologic mechanism of high-dose iodide in this setting?","choices":{"E":"High-dose iodides inhibit organification of iodide via the Wolff-Chaikoff effect and block thyroglobulin proteolysis, thereby reducing both synthesis and release of thyroid hormone and decreasing gland vascularity","A":"Iodides competitively block TSH receptors on follicular cells, preventing adenylyl cyclase activation and downstream cAMP-mediated hormone synthesis","B":"Iodides stimulate type 2 deiodinase in thyroid tissue, accelerating conversion of T4 to the inactive reverse T3 and lowering circulating active hormone","C":"Iodides selectively destroy hyperactive follicular cells through an oxidative mechanism similar to that of radioactive iodine-131","D":"Iodides irreversibly inhibit thyroid peroxidase at its active site, producing sustained blockade of organification that persists after the drug is discontinued"},"explanation":"Large doses of stable iodide produce the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, acutely blocking organification, and also inhibit thyroglobulin hydrolysis, reducing hormone release. Iodides also reduce gland vascularity, which is useful for surgical preparation. TSH receptor blockade, deiodinase stimulation, and follicular cell destruction are not iodide mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iodides for pre-op thyroid storm prep. Wolff-Chaikoff = organification blockade by excess iodide. Escape from Wolff-Chaikoff occurs in normal glands due to downregulation of NIS. COMLEX: Used acutely in thyroid storm alongside thioamides and beta-blockers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"TSH receptor blockade, deiodin","B":"TSH receptor blockade, deiodin","C":"TSH receptor blockade, deiodin"}},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pharmacologic principles and mechanisms for treating hyperthyroidism including thionamides, radioactive iodine, beta-blockers, iodides, and hypothyroidism with levothyroxine.","batch":1,"stem":"Levothyroxine is the standard replacement therapy for hypothyroidism. Which of the following best describes its mechanism of therapeutic action?","choices":{"D":"Levothyroxine binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors after intracellular conversion to T3, promoting gene transcription and increasing synthesis of proteins that mediate metabolic and cardiovascular effects","A":"Levothyroxine activates membrane-bound TSH receptors on follicular cells, stimulating cAMP-mediated synthesis of endogenous thyroid hormone","B":"Levothyroxine directly activates mitochondrial enzymes in peripheral tissues without nuclear receptor binding, rapidly increasing ATP production","C":"Levothyroxine binds Gs-coupled receptors in the hypothalamus, suppressing TRH release and normalizing the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis feedback loop","E":"Levothyroxine inhibits thyroid peroxidase in a dose-dependent fashion that reduces hormone-excess states while supplying basal hormone replacement"},"explanation":"Levothyroxine is synthetic T4 that undergoes intracellular deiodination to T3, which then binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors. The hormone-receptor complex promotes transcription of genes encoding proteins responsible for metabolic rate, heart rate, and sympathetic tone. This nuclear mechanism explains the 6 to 8 week delay in reaching steady state. TSH receptor activation, mitochondrial direct action, and TPO inhibition are not its mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 is a prohormone; T3 is the active form that binds nuclear receptors. Steady state reached in 6 to 8 weeks. COMLEX: Monitor TSH and free T4 at 6 to 8 weeks after any dose change; do not check labs earlier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — Thyroid peroxidase-mediated organification is the exact enzymatic step that thioamides block to halt hormone synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"TSH receptor activation, mitoc","E":"TSH receptor activation, mitoc"}},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient in thyroid storm is given IV propranolol in the emergency department. How quickly would you expect relief of the adrenergic manifestations such as tachycardia and tremor?","choices":{"A":"Within hours, as beta-blockers act rapidly on adrenergic receptors and do not require depletion of circulating thyroid hormone to work","B":"Within 2 to 7 days, as propranolol must first accumulate in thyroid follicular cells to inhibit organification and reduce stored hormone reserves","C":"Within 3 to 4 weeks, reflecting the time required for circulating T3 and T4 to fall as thyroid peroxidase inhibition takes effect","D":"Within 6 to 12 weeks, corresponding to the onset of thyroid parenchymal destruction and the resulting decline in follicular hormone production","E":"Within 3 to 6 months, after beta-adrenergic receptor downregulation in cardiac and smooth muscle tissue reaches a new equilibrium"},"explanation":"Beta-blockers control adrenergic symptoms directly at receptors within hours of administration. They do not reduce thyroid hormone synthesis or secretion, so they cannot normalize the underlying hyperthyroid state. The 2 to 7 day onset describes iodides; 3 to 4 weeks reflects thioamide-mediated store depletion; 6 to 12 weeks is the RAI onset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta-blockers are the fastest-acting symptomatic agents in hyperthyroidism; onset hours. They are NOT definitive treatment. COMLEX: Propranolol is preferred in thyroid storm partly because it also inhibits peripheral T4→T3 conversion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The 2 to 7 day onset describes"}},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient started on methimazole for Graves disease asks why they still feel hyperthyroid after 2 weeks. Which of the following best explains the delayed clinical response to thioamide therapy?","choices":{"E":"Thioamides block new hormone synthesis but not release of preformed T4 and T3 stored in thyroglobulin, so symptoms persist until stores deplete","A":"Methimazole must undergo hepatic activation to an active metabolite before it can reach the thyroid gland and begin blocking thyroid peroxidase","B":"Thioamides require 6 to 12 weeks to destroy sufficient follicular parenchyma through oxidative damage before hormone output declines","C":"TSH receptor antibodies in Graves disease maintain follicular stimulation through a pathway that bypasses the thyroid peroxidase block","D":"Methimazole does not inhibit the sodium-iodide symporter, so continued iodide uptake allows hormone synthesis to proceed despite TPO blockade"},"explanation":"Thioamides stop new hormone production immediately but the thyroid gland stores large quantities of preformed T4 and T3 in thyroglobulin colloid. These reserves continue to supply hormone until depleted, which takes 3 to 4 weeks. T4 has a half-life of approximately 7 days in circulation. This explains the latency period before symptom relief, unlike beta-blockers which act within hours.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thioamide onset delayed 3 to 4 weeks because stored T4 persists. Classic board trap: thioamides work immediately at enzyme level but clinical response is delayed. COMLEX: Always bridge with beta-blockers for symptom control while waiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with severe hyperthyroidism requires emergency surgery and is given Lugol solution to reduce thyroid hormone secretion rapidly. What is the expected onset of effect of high-dose iodide therapy?","choices":{"D":"Within 2 to 7 days, as high-dose iodide acutely blocks organification via the Wolff-Chaikoff effect and inhibits hormone release","A":"Within hours, as potassium iodide directly and rapidly blocks nuclear thyroid hormone receptors in peripheral target tissues","B":"Within 3 to 4 weeks, reflecting the time needed to deplete the large pool of preformed thyroid hormone stored in thyroglobulin colloid","C":"Within 6 to 12 weeks, as the iodide must accumulate to critical levels before initiating destruction of follicular epithelial cells","E":"Within 3 to 6 months, as prolonged iodide administration is required to achieve sustained suppression of thyroid hormone secretion"},"explanation":"High-dose iodide (Lugol solution or SSKI) has a rapid onset of 2 to 7 days and is used for pre-surgical preparation and thyroid storm. It inhibits organification and blocks thyroglobulin hydrolysis. This is faster than thioamides but slower than beta-blockers. Escape from the Wolff-Chaikoff effect can occur with prolonged use in normal glands, limiting long-term utility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iodide onset 2 to 7 days. Unique: also reduces gland vascularity, useful for surgeons. COMLEX: Iodides are indicated for thyroid storm and pre-op prep, not for long-term control because of escape phenomenon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"This is faster than thioamides","B":"This is faster than thioamides"}},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with Graves disease is counseled on treatment options. Which of the following thyroid medications requires the longest time to achieve its maximum therapeutic effect?","choices":{"B":"Radioactive iodine-131, which achieves maximum thyroid tissue destruction and reduction in hormone output 3 to 6 months after administration","A":"Propranolol, which exerts its peak beta-adrenergic blocking effect within 48 hours due to saturation of cardiac and peripheral receptor binding sites","C":"Lugol solution, which requires 3 to 4 weeks before the Wolff-Chaikoff effect fully suppresses organification and release of thyroid hormone","D":"Methimazole, which achieves complete and sustained thyroid peroxidase inhibition within the first 24 hours due to its once-daily dosing schedule","E":"Levothyroxine, which normalizes serum T4 concentrations within 3 to 5 days because of rapid intestinal absorption and high receptor affinity"},"explanation":"Radioactive iodine-131 has the longest time to maximum effect: onset 6 to 12 weeks, maximum effect at 3 to 6 months. RAI half-life is 5 days, and gradual follicular cell death must accumulate before hormone output falls substantially. Beta-blockers act within hours; iodides within 2 to 7 days; methimazole slows synthesis immediately but store depletion takes 3 to 4 weeks; levothyroxine steady state takes 6 to 8 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAI = longest time to effect (3 to 6 months max). Patients should be euthyroid or on beta-blockers first to prevent post-RAI storm. COMLEX: Contraindicated in pregnancy and nursing; main toxicity is hypothyroidism and sore throat from sialadenitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Beta-blockers act within hours","C":"Beta-blockers act within hours","D":"Beta-blockers act within hours","E":"Beta-blockers act within hours"}},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with newly diagnosed hypothyroidism is started on levothyroxine. You counsel him on when to expect full therapeutic benefit and when to schedule follow-up labs. Which of the following is most accurate?","choices":{"C":"Full effect is reached within 3 to 4 days because liothyronine, the active form secreted after T4 conversion, has a short half-life of 24 hours","A":"Full effect is typically reached within 1 to 2 weeks because levothyroxine is rapidly converted to T3, which immediately activates nuclear receptors in target tissues","B":"Full effect occurs within 24 to 48 hours because levothyroxine bypasses hepatic first-pass metabolism and enters the circulation intact after intestinal absorption","D":"Full therapeutic effect and steady-state serum T4 levels are typically reached in 6 to 8 weeks, reflecting the approximately 7-day half-life of T4 and the approximately 5 half-lives required to achieve steady state","E":"Full effect is reached in 3 to 6 months because the pituitary TSH setpoint requires prolonged exposure to normalized T4 before it fully recalibrates"},"explanation":"T4 has a serum half-life of approximately 7 days, bound extensively to thyroid-binding globulin and other plasma proteins. Steady state requires approximately 5 half-lives, placing maximum effect at 6 to 8 weeks. Checking TSH earlier than 6 weeks is unreliable. Liothyronine (T3) has a 24-hour half-life but is not the standard replacement; levothyroxine absorption is not immediate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Do not check TSH until 6 to 8 weeks after a dose change. T4 half-life 7 days. COMLEX: In pregnancy, TSH should be checked more frequently as TBG rises and dose needs to be increased by approximately 25 to 30%.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the expected time-course of action of thyroid medications.","batch":1,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with hyperthyroidism receives radioactive iodine-131 therapy. His physician explains the expected time-course. Which of the following best describes the anticipated clinical response?","choices":{"D":"Onset of thyroid reduction occurs over 6 to 12 weeks as follicular cell death accumulates, with maximum effect achieved at 3 to 6 months after treatment","A":"Onset occurs within hours because the iodide-131 immediately blocks thyroid peroxidase upon uptake into follicular cells, stopping new hormone synthesis","B":"Full effect is seen within 2 to 7 days because beta radiation rapidly destroys follicular epithelium and the resulting hormone decline parallels the drug half-life","C":"RAI has a half-life of 30 days, producing a gradual but steady reduction in thyroid mass over 6 to 12 months before maximum hormone suppression is achieved","E":"Full effect is achieved within 3 to 4 weeks because the T4 serum half-life of 7 days limits how quickly circulating levels can fall after gland damage begins"},"explanation":"RAI half-life is 5 days. Beta emission destroys follicular cells progressively; initial onset of hormone decline occurs at 6 to 12 weeks and maximum effect is at 3 to 6 months. RAI does not inhibit TPO directly; its effect is destructive rather than enzymatic. The 3 to 4 week timeline describes thioamide-mediated store depletion, not RAI.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RAI half-life = 5 days. Onset 6 to 12 weeks. Max effect 3 to 6 months. Complication = hypothyroidism requiring lifelong T4 replacement. COMLEX: RAI is the most common definitive treatment for Graves disease in non-pregnant adults in the US.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — The nuclear receptor mechanism and long T4 protein-binding half-life explain why thioamides require weeks before circulating hormone levels fall.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"RAI does not inhibit TPO direc","E":"The 3 to 4 week timeline descr"}},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis has been taking levothyroxine 100 mcg daily for 6 months. Her TSH remains elevated at 6.2 mIU/L despite reported compliance. She takes her pill each morning with breakfast, which includes a glass of milk and a calcium carbonate supplement. Physical exam is unremarkable. Which of the following best explains her persistently elevated TSH?","choices":{"C":"Calcium carbonate and concurrent food intake form insoluble complexes with levothyroxine in the small intestinal lumen, decreasing the absorbed fraction of the dose","A":"Autoimmune mucosal damage from Hashimoto thyroiditis selectively impairs enterocyte transport of levothyroxine across the intestinal epithelial barrier","B":"Dietary calcium stimulates hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes, increasing levothyroxine metabolism and reducing circulating T4 levels before it can bind thyroid hormone receptors","D":"Elevated anti-TPO antibodies from Hashimoto disease form complexes with T4 in the circulation, sequestering free T4 and preventing receptor binding","E":"Morning cortisol peaks interfere with nuclear thyroid hormone receptor binding, transiently reducing levothyroxine efficacy when taken concurrently with physiologic cortisol elevation"},"explanation":"Calcium carbonate and food are two of the most common causes of reduced levothyroxine absorption. Calcium forms insoluble chelates with levothyroxine in the alkaline small intestinal environment. Co-ingestion with food also reduces the bioavailability from approximately 70 to 80% down to significantly lower levels. Levothyroxine should be taken on an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before meals and separated from calcium, iron, and antacids by at least 4 hours. Anti-TPO antibodies are directed against thyroid peroxidase in the gland, not against circulating T4, so they do not explain a high TSH on a steady dose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Top absorption interactors: calcium carbonate, ferrous sulfate, antacids, cholestyramine, PPIs, sucralfate, soy, bran, coffee. All reduce absorption. COMLEX: Taking levothyroxine at bedtime is an acceptable alternative if morning separation is difficult.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Sodium-iodide symporter and follicular iodide handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with primary hypothyroidism is being counseled on how to take levothyroxine for optimal bioavailability. He currently takes multiple morning medications and asks for guidance. Which of the following instructions is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Take levothyroxine on an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast so that food, calcium, and other medications cannot interfere with its absorption","B":"Take levothyroxine with orange juice each morning, as the citric acid acidifies the stomach and enhances the dissolution and mucosal uptake of synthetic T4","C":"Take levothyroxine with a high-fiber cereal, as slowed gastric emptying prolongs intestinal contact time and increases the total fraction of drug absorbed","D":"Take levothyroxine with ferrous sulfate simultaneously, as iron chelates T4 and protects it from acid degradation in the gastric environment","E":"Take levothyroxine with a glass of warm milk at bedtime, as high TBG levels during sleep allow for greater plasma protein binding and longer duration of action"},"explanation":"Levothyroxine is best absorbed in the duodenum and ileum on an empty stomach. Oral bioavailability is approximately 70 to 80% under fasting conditions. Taking it 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, or at bedtime, avoids competition from food, calcium, iron, and other drugs that impair absorption. Ferrous sulfate actually reduces absorption by forming insoluble complexes; fiber and milk do not enhance absorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Levothyroxine absorption site = duodenum and ileum. Bioavailability 70 to 80% fasting. COMLEX: Bedtime dosing is equivalent to morning fasting dosing and may improve adherence; check TSH 6 to 8 weeks after any administration change.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Sodium-iodide symporter and follicular iodide handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Ferrous sulfate actually reduc","E":"Ferrous sulfate actually reduc"}},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with hypothyroidism, stable on levothyroxine 88 mcg daily for 3 years, is started on omeprazole for newly diagnosed GERD. Four weeks later her TSH has risen from 1.8 to 5.1 mIU/L. She denies dietary changes and continues to take levothyroxine before breakfast. Which of the following best explains the change in her TSH?","choices":{"B":"Omeprazole reduces gastric acid secretion, raising intraluminal pH and impairing dissolution of levothyroxine tablets before intestinal absorption","A":"Omeprazole inhibits intestinal deiodinase enzyme activity, reducing peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 and lowering the effective level of active hormone","C":"Omeprazole induces CYP3A4 in the liver, accelerating levothyroxine metabolism and reducing the plasma half-life of the drug from 7 days to approximately 2 days","D":"Omeprazole stimulates TSH secretion through a direct pituitary agonist effect, raising the TSH setpoint and falsely elevating measured thyrotropin levels","E":"Omeprazole binds thyroid-binding globulin competitively, displacing T4 from its carrier protein and increasing non-specific clearance of free T4"},"explanation":"Levothyroxine tablet dissolution requires gastric acid. Proton pump inhibitors like omeprazole raise gastric pH, reducing dissolution efficiency and decreasing T4 absorption (Katzung Ch 38 lists proton pump inhibitors among drugs that interfere with T4 absorption). Similarly, antacids and sucralfate impair absorption. The TSH rise reflects decreased effective levothyroxine dose. The levothyroxine dose should be increased or the PPI dose timing adjusted, with TSH rechecked in 6 to 8 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs reduce levothyroxine absorption by raising gastric pH. Also sucralfate, antacids. Classic board scenario: stable patient starts a new GI drug and TSH rises. COMLEX: This is a common drug interaction in clinical practice; always review concurrent medications when TSH drifts up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Sodium-iodide symporter and follicular iodide handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with primary hypothyroidism, previously well-controlled on levothyroxine 75 mcg daily, is now 14 weeks pregnant. Her TSH has risen to 5.8 mIU/L. She denies missing doses or dietary changes. Which of the following best explains why her levothyroxine dose likely needs to be increased during pregnancy?","choices":{"D":"Estrogen-driven elevation of thyroid-binding globulin during pregnancy increases total T4 binding and reduces free T4, so a higher dose is needed to maintain free hormone","A":"Estrogen suppresses hepatic thyroid-binding globulin synthesis during pregnancy, so more unbound T4 is cleared and the circulating hormone pool steadily shrinks","B":"Fetal brain development does not rely on maternal thyroxine, so the dose rises only to keep pace with maternal weight gain across all three trimesters","C":"Renal clearance of T4 increases markedly during pregnancy due to glomerular hyperfiltration, substantially shortening the levothyroxine half-life and requiring daily dose increases","E":"Progesterone competes with T3 for nuclear thyroid hormone receptor binding sites in maternal tissues, reducing tissue responsiveness and requiring higher circulating T4 levels"},"explanation":"Estrogen stimulates hepatic synthesis of thyroid-binding globulin, increasing total T4 binding capacity; Katzung lists estrogens among drugs that increase TBG and notes that when TBG sites are increased by pregnancy, patients dependent on T4 replacement may require higher dosages. Free T4 falls, stimulating more TSH secretion. To normalize the serum TSH, the thyroxine dose typically must be increased by about 25 to 30% during pregnancy (Katzung Ch 38), beginning in the first trimester. Estrogen raises TBG rather than suppressing it; androgens and glucocorticoids are the drugs Katzung lists as decreasing TBG. Katzung stresses that early development of the fetal brain depends on maternal thyroxine, so the dose matters for the fetus and is not a matter of maternal weight gain. Katzung attributes the higher requirement to increased TBG binding, not to faster renal clearance of T4 or to progesterone competing for thyroid hormone receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy raises estrogen, raises TBG, lowers free T4, raises TSH. Levothyroxine dose up approximately 25 to 30% in pregnancy. COMLEX: Maternal hypothyroidism is associated with fetal neurodevelopmental impairment; aggressive TSH normalization is critical.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Sodium-iodide symporter and follicular iodide handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Estrogen raises TBG rather tha"}},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hypothyroidism and iron-deficiency anemia is prescribed levothyroxine and ferrous sulfate at the same clinic visit. He asks if he can take both pills together each morning to simplify his routine. Which of the following counseling points is most important?","choices":{"A":"Ferrous sulfate reduces levothyroxine absorption by forming insoluble iron-T4 complexes in the intestinal lumen; the two medications should be separated by at least 4 hours","B":"Ferrous sulfate enhances levothyroxine absorption by acidifying the intestinal environment, so co-administration is actually preferred to maximize T4 bioavailability","C":"Ferrous sulfate has no meaningful effect on levothyroxine absorption because each drug is transported by a distinct carrier system in separate intestinal epithelial cell populations","D":"Ferrous sulfate inhibits thyroid peroxidase activity when taken within 2 hours of levothyroxine, potentially causing paradoxical hypothyroidism despite adequate T4 supplementation","E":"Ferrous sulfate should be taken within 15 minutes of levothyroxine to utilize the same transporter window and reduce competition from other dietary iron sources"},"explanation":"Iron forms insoluble chelates with levothyroxine in the alkaline small intestine, markedly reducing T4 bioavailability. This is a well-documented drug interaction. A minimum 4-hour separation is recommended. The same applies to calcium carbonate, cholestyramine, aluminum-containing antacids, and sucralfate. Ferrous sulfate does not inhibit TPO and does not enhance T4 absorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iron-levothyroxine interaction = chelation, reduced absorption. Separate by at least 4 hours. COMLEX: This interaction is especially clinically relevant in pregnant women, who often take both prenatal iron and levothyroxine simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Sodium-iodide symporter and follicular iodide handling","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Ferrous sulfate does not inhib","C":"This is a well-documented drug","D":"Ferrous sulfate does not inhib"}},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe where levothyroxine is absorbed and how food, calcium and gastric acidity impact absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has taken levothyroxine 100 mcg daily for 4 years, always on an empty stomach, with stable TSH. Over 6 months her TSH has risen to 11 mIU/L. She reports bloating, loose stools, and fatigue, and new iron-deficiency anemia is found. Tissue transglutaminase IgA is markedly positive. Which best explains her rising TSH?","choices":{"C":"Celiac autoantibodies bind circulating T4 and block its entry into cells, so TSH rises even though absorption is unchanged","A":"Celiac disease reduces levothyroxine absorption, which normally occurs best in the duodenum and ileum","B":"Celiac disease induces hepatic enzymes that speed levothyroxine clearance, shortening its half-life without changing absorption","D":"Celiac disease raises thyroxine-binding globulin, which lowers free T4 and raises TSH while drug absorption stays unchanged","E":"Celiac disease destroys remaining thyroid tissue, so lost residual hormone output explains the rise"},"explanation":"Katzung (Ch 38) states that thyroxine is absorbed best in the duodenum and ileum, that its absorption is modified by intraluminal factors such as food, drugs, gastric acidity, and intestinal flora, and that oral bioavailability of tablets averages 70 to 80 percent. Higher thyroxine requirements are reported after bariatric surgery and with malabsorptive disorders, including atrophic gastritis, Helicobacter pylori gastritis, celiac disease, and lactose intolerance, and doses may be reduced after these disorders are treated. Her diarrhea, iron deficiency, and positive tissue transglutaminase point to celiac disease reducing absorption. Hepatic enzyme induction, hormone-binding autoantibodies, and a rise in thyroxine-binding globulin are not the mechanisms the source links to celiac disease. Loss of residual thyroid tissue would not account for the new gastrointestinal findings that coincide with the rise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Levothyroxine is absorbed best in the duodenum and ileum; malabsorption (celiac disease, atrophic or H. pylori gastritis, bariatric surgery) and binders such as calcium, iron, PPIs, bile acid sequestrants, and sucralfate reduce it (Katzung Ch 38). COMLEX: After adjusting the dose, recheck TSH once a new steady state is reached.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — expected time course of thyroid medications, including levothyroxine steady state","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Hepatic enzyme induction, horm","C":"Hepatic enzyme induction, horm"}},{"id":"sdl15_q19","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman who is 7 weeks pregnant is newly diagnosed with Graves disease and requires antithyroid drug therapy. Her physician considers methimazole versus propylthiouracil. Which of the following best explains why PTU is preferred over methimazole during the first trimester?","choices":{"A":"PTU does not cross the placenta and therefore carries no risk of fetal hypothyroidism at any dose level throughout all trimesters of gestation","B":"Methimazole crosses the placenta at a substantially higher rate than PTU due to greater lipid solubility, causing more fetal thyroid suppression per unit of maternal dose","C":"Methimazole is linked to first-trimester teratogenic effects including aplasia cutis congenita and choanal and esophageal atresia, risks lower with PTU","D":"Methimazole blocks fetal TSH receptors directly at the pituitary level during a critical window of neurodevelopment, whereas PTU acts only at the thyroid gland","E":"PTU is preferred because its longer half-life in pregnant patients allows once-daily dosing, reducing dose peaks that could cause transient fetal thyroid suppression"},"explanation":"Methimazole is a known teratogen associated with aplasia cutis congenita, choanal atresia, and esophageal atresia during organogenesis in the first trimester. PTU is therefore preferred in the first trimester. However, PTU is switched back to methimazole after the first trimester due to the risk of PTU-associated fatal hepatotoxicity. Both agents cross the placenta and can cause fetal hypothyroidism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First trimester = PTU (lower teratogenicity). After 1st trimester = switch to methimazole (lower hepatotoxicity). Both cross placenta. COMLEX: Aplasia cutis is a scalp skin defect associated with methimazole teratogenicity; high-yield board association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"Both agents cross the placenta"}},{"id":"sdl15_q20","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man taking methimazole 30 mg daily for hyperthyroidism presents with a 3-day history of sore throat, fever, and malaise. His CBC reveals a granulocyte count of 350 cells per microliter. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"C":"Discontinue methimazole immediately, as a granulocyte count below 500 cells per microliter meets criteria for agranulocytosis and the causative drug must be stopped without delay","A":"Reduce the methimazole dose by 50% and recheck the CBC in 1 week, monitoring closely for spontaneous recovery of the granulocyte count to baseline levels","B":"Switch immediately to propylthiouracil at a full therapeutic dose, as the two thioamides act through distinct mechanisms and do not share cross-sensitivity for hematologic toxicity","D":"Continue methimazole at the current dose and add granulocyte colony-stimulating factor to support bone marrow recovery while the underlying hyperthyroidism is controlled","E":"Reassure the patient that transient leukopenia is an expected side effect in the first 90 days of therapy and does not require any change in his current regimen"},"explanation":"Agranulocytosis is defined as a granulocyte count below 500 cells per microliter and is a potentially life-threatening complication of thioamides. It requires immediate discontinuation of the offending drug. Cross-sensitivity between methimazole and PTU is approximately 50%, so switching is not always safe without careful consideration. Reducing the dose is insufficient once agranulocytosis has developed. The patient should be monitored for sepsis and may require G-CSF in severe cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Agranulocytosis threshold = less than 500 granulocytes per microliter. Occurs most often in first 90 days or at doses greater than 40 mg daily. Presentation: fever, sore throat. COMLEX: Instruct all patients starting thioamides to stop the drug and seek care immediately if they develop fever or sore throat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Reducing the dose is insuffici","D":"It requires immediate disconti"}},{"id":"sdl15_q21","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with hyperthyroidism has been taking propylthiouracil for 8 months. She presents with fatigue, lower extremity edema, and hematuria. Urinalysis reveals red blood cell casts. Serum p-ANCA (anti-MPO) is positive. Her creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 2.3 mg/dL. Which of the following best explains her presentation?","choices":{"E":"PTU-induced ANCA-positive vasculitis has produced pauci-immune glomerulonephritis, a recognized serious complication that requires stopping the drug immediately","A":"PTU has triggered immune-mediated hemolytic anemia that is causing hematuria and edema through complement-mediated erythrocyte destruction in the renal microcirculation","B":"Chronic PTU therapy has caused direct tubular toxicity through iodide-mediated complement deposition, producing a tubulointerstitial nephritis pattern on biopsy","C":"PTU-induced hypothyroidism has caused secondary ANCA-negative membranous nephropathy through immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane","D":"PTU stimulates production of TSH receptor antibodies that cross-react with renal antigens and produce a proliferative glomerulonephritis through molecular mimicry"},"explanation":"PTU is well known to cause ANCA-positive vasculitis, most commonly p-ANCA directed against myeloperoxidase. This can lead to pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis, pulmonary hemorrhage, or cutaneous vasculitis. It requires immediate PTU discontinuation and often immunosuppression. Vasculitis is listed as a rare adverse effect of the thioamides as a class, but ANCA-positive vasculitis is the toxicity First Aid ties to PTU, making it a distinguishing feature in board questions comparing the two thioamides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTU signature toxicities: severe hepatotoxicity (boxed warning) + ANCA-positive vasculitis. Methimazole signature toxicity: teratogenicity (aplasia cutis). Both share: agranulocytosis, rash. COMLEX: p-ANCA vasculitis with renal involvement in a patient on PTU = stop the drug immediately.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl15_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with newly diagnosed Graves disease is seen by a pediatric endocrinologist. The physician discusses antithyroid drug options with the family and explains why propylthiouracil is generally not recommended in children. Which of the following best explains this recommendation?","choices":{"B":"PTU carries a black box warning for potentially fatal hepatotoxicity, and this risk is considered unacceptably high in pediatric patients where the safer profile of methimazole makes it the preferred first-line antithyroid drug","A":"PTU causes a higher rate of agranulocytosis in children than in adults due to the relative immaturity of pediatric bone marrow reserve and reduced myeloid replenishment capacity","C":"PTU irreversibly inhibits thyroid peroxidase in growing children and causes permanent hypothyroidism after even a brief treatment course due to glandular atrophy","D":"PTU inhibits type 2 deiodinase in the developing central nervous system, impairing local T3 production required for myelination and normal neurodevelopmental milestones","E":"PTU induces premature fusion of growth plates through TSH suppression and reduced IGF-1 signaling, resulting in short stature if used during active skeletal development"},"explanation":"PTU carries a black box warning for serious, potentially fatal, liver injury. This hepatotoxicity has been observed in both adults and children, and because methimazole provides equivalent antithyroid efficacy with a safer hepatic profile, PTU is generally avoided in children. The FDA advises that PTU should be reserved for patients who cannot tolerate methimazole or RAI. The agranulocytosis rate is similar for both agents. PTU's deiodinase action is on peripheral conversion of T4 to T3, and neither impaired myelination nor growth-plate fusion is among its listed adverse effects (rash, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, hepatotoxicity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTU black box warning = hepatotoxicity, potentially fatal. Avoid in children. Methimazole is preferred in pediatrics. COMLEX: PTU is the only option during the 1st trimester of pregnancy; switch to methimazole after the first trimester due to hepatotoxicity concern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The agranulocytosis rate is si","D":"PTU's deiodinase action is on ","E":"PTU's deiodinase action is on "}},{"id":"sdl15_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman develops a diffuse maculopapular pruritic rash 3 weeks after starting methimazole. Her physician considers switching to propylthiouracil to avoid stopping antithyroid therapy. Which of the following is the most critical consideration before making this substitution?","choices":{"D":"Switching is absolutely contraindicated in women of reproductive age because propylthiouracil carries a substantially higher teratogenic risk than methimazole at equivalent doses","A":"Cross-sensitivity between the two thioamides is rare, occurring in fewer than 1% of patients, making it generally safe to switch from methimazole to PTU when a mild rash develops","B":"The maculopapular rash results from excess iodide released into the circulation after organification is blocked and will resolve spontaneously without changing the drug regimen","C":"Approximately 50% of patients who develop a rash on one thioamide react to the other through cross-sensitivity, making definitive therapy such as RAI or surgery worth considering","E":"The cross-sensitivity rate is zero because methimazole and PTU have entirely distinct chemical structures that do not share any antigenic determinants capable of producing the same immune response"},"explanation":"Cross-sensitivity between methimazole and PTU exists in approximately 50% of patients with thioamide-induced rash. This means switching agents carries a substantial risk of recurrence. When a rash occurs, clinicians should weigh whether RAI or thyroidectomy is a more appropriate definitive treatment rather than assuming the second thioamide will be tolerated. For severe reactions such as agranulocytosis, cross-sensitivity exists and switching is strongly discouraged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thioamide cross-sensitivity is about 50% (Katzung Ch 38), so switching is avoided after severe reactions such as agranulocytosis. Do not switch for severe reactions; consider RAI or surgery. COMLEX: Both agents share the same chemical class and antigenic structure; this explains the cross-reactivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl15_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the major complications of thionamide antithyroid drugs including methimazole and propylthiouracil.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with Graves disease has been taking methimazole 45 mg daily for 7 weeks. His physician orders a CBC as part of routine monitoring. Results show a WBC of 6,200 per microliter with 58% granulocytes (normal). He is currently afebrile and feels well. Which of the following most accurately characterizes the risk pattern for methimazole-induced agranulocytosis?","choices":{"C":"Agranulocytosis occurs most often within the first 90 days of therapy and is more likely at doses above 40 mg per day, placing this patient in a higher-risk category","A":"Agranulocytosis risk increases in a linear fashion proportional to total cumulative dose over the full course of therapy, so monthly CBC monitoring is required indefinitely regardless of when treatment began","B":"Agranulocytosis is strictly dose-independent and equally likely at any methimazole dose level, so the 45 mg daily dose does not confer additional risk compared to lower doses","D":"Agranulocytosis occurs predominantly after 2 or more years of continuous use due to cumulative bone marrow suppression from sustained thyroid peroxidase inhibition","E":"Agranulocytosis is irreversible once a granulocyte count below 1,500 per microliter is documented and reliably requires allogeneic bone marrow transplant for hematologic recovery"},"explanation":"Methimazole-induced agranulocytosis occurs in approximately 0.1 to 0.5% of patients and is most common within the first 90 days of therapy, with increased risk at doses above 40 mg per day. This patient is at elevated risk given both the dose and timing. Agranulocytosis is generally reversible with drug discontinuation and supportive care including antibiotics if infection develops; bone marrow transplant is not required. Monthly CBCs beyond the initial high-risk period are not routinely recommended; patient education to report fever and sore throat is more practical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Methimazole agranulocytosis: 0.1 to 0.5% incidence, first 90 days, dose greater than 40 mg/d = higher risk. Teach patients to stop drug and seek care if fever or sore throat develops. COMLEX: Agranulocytosis is reversible in most cases after stopping the drug; distinguish from aplastic anemia which is irreversible.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Clinical manifestations of thyroid hormone over and under exposure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Monthly CBCs beyond the initia","E":"Agranulocytosis is generally r"}}]};
